WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.590 --> 00:00:17.420
Tim Ferriss: Justin, it is nice to see you.

00:00:17.420 --> 00:00:18.450
Very nice to see you again.

00:00:18.450 --> 00:00:19.480
Justin Gary: Yeah, Tim.

00:00:19.480 --> 00:00:20.970
Great to see you again as well.

00:00:20.970 --> 00:00:26.980
Tim Ferriss: And you are at maybe undisclosed,
or you could disclose, but you are overseas

00:00:26.980 --> 00:00:31.460
at the moment, so we are across the pond or
many ponds, as it were.

00:00:31.460 --> 00:00:37.930
I'm glad we were able to make the timing work
and I thought we could begin with Magic.

00:00:37.930 --> 00:00:39.270
So, Magic.

00:00:39.270 --> 00:00:49.450
Magic: The Gathering is a game that I never
really became familiar with because I was

00:00:49.450 --> 00:00:55.880
older guard at that point, Dungeons &amp; Dragons,
a lot of the very first editions.

00:00:55.880 --> 00:01:00.559
But my younger brother, on the other hand,
really became immersed in Magic and actually

00:01:00.559 --> 00:01:07.660
competed on some level and his friends were
just obsessed with Magic: The Gathering.

00:01:07.660 --> 00:01:14.270
So could you, please, for people who don't
have the context, explain what Magic is and

00:01:14.270 --> 00:01:18.810
I suppose how you became involved and what
that trajectory looked like?

00:01:18.810 --> 00:01:23.360
Justin Gary: Magic is a trading card game
invented by Richard Garfield.

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:27.860
And so what trading card game means is that
you can buy it, not just like a normal game

00:01:27.860 --> 00:01:30.400
like Monopoly, you'd buy it in a store, it'd
be a single box here.

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:34.259
Here, you buy packs of cards like you would
baseball cards, and each one has different

00:01:34.259 --> 00:01:35.750
gameplay elements.

00:01:35.750 --> 00:01:40.220
The way I like to describe it for people who
don't know is it's sort of a cross between

00:01:40.220 --> 00:01:44.229
chess and poker where you get to decide what
deck of cards you're going to play and what

00:01:44.229 --> 00:01:45.480
pieces you get to play with.

00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:49.500
So there's the poker element of I'm drawing
a hand of cards and maybe I can bluff what

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:50.830
I have and you don't know.

00:01:50.830 --> 00:01:54.500
And there's the chess element of there's tactically,
once I play those cards, there's tactically

00:01:54.500 --> 00:01:57.420
ways I use them that kind of battle back and
forth.

00:01:57.420 --> 00:02:01.400
Then, of course, it has fantasy characters
and so it appeals to people who like Dungeons

00:02:01.400 --> 00:02:06.810
&amp; Dragons so you'll be playing wizards and
dragons and warriors and stuff like that.

00:02:06.810 --> 00:02:10.569
I first got involved with Magic in a pretty
funny way, actually.

00:02:10.569 --> 00:02:15.590
So I used to be on a competitive laser tag
league.

00:02:15.590 --> 00:02:18.230
So I am a very, a very competitive person
by nature.

00:02:18.230 --> 00:02:22.450
I've been involved in pretty much whatever
I do, I'll find some way to compete with it.

00:02:22.450 --> 00:02:27.390
In between games of laser tag, people were
playing this card game that looked really

00:02:27.390 --> 00:02:28.800
cool I'd never heard of before.

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:30.750
I watched them play and I was like, "Okay,
what is that?"

00:02:30.750 --> 00:02:34.390
Okay, so I go to the store and I just buy
a pack of cards and, as I mentioned, the cards

00:02:34.390 --> 00:02:35.390
are totally random.

00:02:35.390 --> 00:02:39.010
So I just took all the random cards I bought
and just went to go play and I got my butt

00:02:39.010 --> 00:02:42.890
kicked and was, of course, okay, you actually
have to construct and build your deck and

00:02:42.890 --> 00:02:45.700
build your strategy and that helps it come
to life.

00:02:45.700 --> 00:02:51.209
So I came back the next week and, okay, now
I played a little better and a little better

00:02:51.209 --> 00:02:56.830
and eventually, at one point, all the guys
from my group were all going to go to a state

00:02:56.830 --> 00:02:57.830
championship tournament.

00:02:57.830 --> 00:03:00.520
Tim Ferriss: And how old were you at this
point?

00:03:00.520 --> 00:03:02.120
Justin Gary: I was 16 years old.

00:03:02.120 --> 00:03:03.320
Tim Ferriss: Okay, got it.

00:03:03.320 --> 00:03:06.200
Justin Gary: So I was a young kid, so I was
like, "Oh, hey, come along.

00:03:06.200 --> 00:03:07.300
Come on the car ride."

00:03:07.300 --> 00:03:09.680
And those were some of my best memories in
general.

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:14.519
Just a bunch of guys hanging out, jumping
in the car and just talking about games, riding

00:03:14.519 --> 00:03:15.519
up.

00:03:15.519 --> 00:03:19.940
As it turns out, I won that tournament and
so I ended up becoming the state champion

00:03:19.940 --> 00:03:24.690
and that got me an invitation to the national
championships.

00:03:24.690 --> 00:03:29.800
Again, I was not ever taking this sort of
seriously, I wasn't even intending to go.

00:03:29.800 --> 00:03:32.120
In fact, I was supposed to go to debate camp
that summer.

00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:36.099
Yet another thing I was arbitrarily competitive
in.

00:03:36.099 --> 00:03:40.850
And it ended up being my parents couldn't
afford to send me that year to debate camp.

00:03:40.850 --> 00:03:46.959
So last minute, again, my friends were all
super supportive here, I ran a tournament

00:03:46.959 --> 00:03:48.099
at my local game store.

00:03:48.099 --> 00:03:49.790
They let me run the event.

00:03:49.790 --> 00:03:51.849
I collected the entry fees from that tournament.

00:03:51.849 --> 00:03:57.790
I used that to pay for a plane ticket to fly
to Columbus, where the US National Championships

00:03:57.790 --> 00:03:58.790
was happening.

00:03:58.790 --> 00:04:01.060
I slept on a friend's floor.

00:04:01.060 --> 00:04:05.819
I was now 17 at this time and I ended up winning
the US National Championships and that ends

00:04:05.819 --> 00:04:10.250
up taking me on a career of traveling around
the world, playing Magic for a living.

00:04:10.250 --> 00:04:14.909
Tim Ferriss: So let's dive into a bunch of
things that you mentioned.

00:04:14.909 --> 00:04:21.370
I was not aware of the debate camp component,
the debating piece, and I have watched debates.

00:04:21.370 --> 00:04:24.629
I have never been part of a debate team.

00:04:24.629 --> 00:04:32.300
Could you just explain for folks what a debate
competition looks like, or a debate as a game,

00:04:32.300 --> 00:04:33.300
per se?

00:04:33.300 --> 00:04:38.850
What does that look like and why were you
good or at least competent at it?

00:04:38.850 --> 00:04:41.220
Justin Gary: There's a few different categories.

00:04:41.220 --> 00:04:47.060
I'll just talk about my favorite one, it was
called Lincoln-Douglas debate at the time.

00:04:47.060 --> 00:04:53.600
It basically means that you have a topic that
you're debating like, say, the US should have

00:04:53.600 --> 00:05:00.190
closer relationships with China or it's better
to sacrifice the good of a few for the good

00:05:00.190 --> 00:05:02.280
of the many or something like that, some topic.

00:05:02.280 --> 00:05:05.100
And then you would have to pick one side of
the argument.

00:05:05.100 --> 00:05:08.970
There's a judge in the room and you have a
certain amount of time to make your case.

00:05:08.970 --> 00:05:10.949
Then the other person has a certain amount
of time to make their case.

00:05:10.949 --> 00:05:13.979
Then you have some time for rebuttal and they
have some time for rebuttal.

00:05:13.979 --> 00:05:19.110
At the end, the judge or judges would then
decide who won.

00:05:19.110 --> 00:05:20.220
So that's kind of the outline.

00:05:20.220 --> 00:05:23.250
Tim Ferriss: And there's a coin toss to decide
who goes first?

00:05:23.250 --> 00:05:24.250
Justin Gary: Yes.

00:05:24.250 --> 00:05:25.250
Tim Ferriss: Because that seems like a major
advantage.

00:05:25.250 --> 00:05:26.400
All right, so there's a —
Justin Gary: Well —

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:27.400
Tim Ferriss: Go ahead.

00:05:27.400 --> 00:05:28.400
Justin Gary: It varies, right?

00:05:28.400 --> 00:05:30.280
So from round to round, you'll go first or
second and this was sort of how we did it

00:05:30.280 --> 00:05:31.280
in high school.

00:05:31.280 --> 00:05:35.420
My favorite version of debate was what I did
in college, which was called parliamentary

00:05:35.420 --> 00:05:40.290
debate where you could, literally, make up
any topic you wanted and just start talking

00:05:40.290 --> 00:05:42.750
about it and the other side would have to
defend the other side.

00:05:42.750 --> 00:05:46.760
You would just play and have fun and try to
figure out and convince people that this was

00:05:46.760 --> 00:05:47.760
the way to go.

00:05:47.760 --> 00:05:54.060
It was such a fun exercise in just persuasion
and kind of tactically how you wanted to frame

00:05:54.060 --> 00:05:55.060
everything.

00:05:55.060 --> 00:05:58.080
It became something I really spent quite a
bit of time on and really enjoyed.

00:05:58.080 --> 00:06:01.510
Then it ended up being very useful for quite
a few things later in life.

00:06:01.510 --> 00:06:04.460
It turns out being able to speak good has
value.

00:06:04.460 --> 00:06:13.000
Tim Ferriss: So if we look at, say, the Lincoln-Douglas
format or the parliamentary, I suppose the

00:06:13.000 --> 00:06:16.500
former is maybe a better example to use here,
knowing nothing about it.

00:06:16.500 --> 00:06:18.320
But how much time do you have to prepare?

00:06:18.320 --> 00:06:24.000
Once you are assigned or have chosen one side
of, let's just say, the US should have closer

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:26.070
ties with China and you're either pro or con.

00:06:26.070 --> 00:06:27.910
How much time do you have to prepare that?

00:06:27.910 --> 00:06:33.880
And are you just basically making it up, but
you're using the strength of your logic that

00:06:33.880 --> 00:06:37.750
you construct, even if your assumptions are
off to defeat your opponent?

00:06:37.750 --> 00:06:38.750
How does that work?

00:06:38.750 --> 00:06:44.111
Justin Gary: So for those formats, the same
topic will hold for a while, for months, like

00:06:44.111 --> 00:06:47.330
a season, and so you can prepare very much
ahead of time.

00:06:47.330 --> 00:06:48.330
Tim Ferriss: I see.

00:06:48.330 --> 00:06:49.330
Got it.

00:06:49.330 --> 00:06:50.330
Justin Gary: Then there's other ones that
like the parliamentary one, where you have

00:06:50.330 --> 00:06:54.009
no idea what you're going to talk about until
you start going up there a lot of the times.

00:06:54.009 --> 00:06:58.140
So that was more fun because it was a little
bit more extemporaneous, so there's varying

00:06:58.140 --> 00:06:59.140
degrees.

00:06:59.140 --> 00:07:02.170
So when you have the same topic over and over
again, you'd start to notice common threads

00:07:02.170 --> 00:07:04.750
about how people will present it.

00:07:04.750 --> 00:07:07.230
Then some people would, you'd try crazy things.

00:07:07.230 --> 00:07:10.420
So may be that the US should have closer ties
with China and you would take a position,

00:07:10.420 --> 00:07:12.990
actually the US should not exist anymore,
it should dissolve.

00:07:12.990 --> 00:07:17.319
And you start making arguments around that
and you just kind of have to take the argument

00:07:17.319 --> 00:07:22.240
seriously, in a sense, because the job of
the judge is to sort of leave outside their

00:07:22.240 --> 00:07:25.000
preconceived notions and just hear what you
have to say.

00:07:25.000 --> 00:07:29.919
So that, having to think on your feet and
react and be able to kind of come up with

00:07:29.919 --> 00:07:33.900
unique answers and change the flow of things
was really quite a bit of fun.

00:07:33.900 --> 00:07:35.810
Tim Ferriss: Okay, so in that, I will move
on.

00:07:35.810 --> 00:07:40.020
To everybody listening, I will move on in
a moment, but I really want to understand

00:07:40.020 --> 00:07:44.720
this because I suspect, as you mentioned,
that these are threads that tie into other

00:07:44.720 --> 00:07:46.479
things later, at least some of them.

00:07:46.479 --> 00:07:50.830
These are not isolated skills.

00:07:50.830 --> 00:07:56.389
If you're practicing, say, the parliamentary
style and you are developing this approach

00:07:56.389 --> 00:08:02.169
to structured thinking and presenting and
you don't know what you're going to be speaking

00:08:02.169 --> 00:08:06.169
about until you really sort of get on the
stage and somebody hands you the mic, it's

00:08:06.169 --> 00:08:09.340
a sort of freestyle rap battle.

00:08:09.340 --> 00:08:15.199
When you get up and then you give your spiel,
how do the judges judge?

00:08:15.199 --> 00:08:17.979
What does that look like?

00:08:17.979 --> 00:08:19.450
Justin Gary: Great question.

00:08:19.450 --> 00:08:20.590
So —
Tim Ferriss: Because presumably they might

00:08:20.590 --> 00:08:24.819
not know anything about the topic that you're
discussing, so they can't really fact-check

00:08:24.819 --> 00:08:26.470
a lot of what you're saying, I would assume.

00:08:26.470 --> 00:08:27.470
Justin Gary: That's right.

00:08:27.470 --> 00:08:33.190
There's no live fact-checking in these formats,
it's about your persuasive ability.

00:08:33.190 --> 00:08:36.940
If I were going to break this down into kind
of core skillsets and what the judges are

00:08:36.940 --> 00:08:42.360
doing, which is also what I'm doing as a competitor,
is any time my opponent is saying something,

00:08:42.360 --> 00:08:43.650
I'm writing down their points.

00:08:43.650 --> 00:08:48.399
So I'm breaking them down into kind of concrete
notes and I want to make sure that for each

00:08:48.399 --> 00:08:51.540
point that's brought up, I am addressing it
in some key way.

00:08:51.540 --> 00:08:55.940
Now a lot of times I can make some broader
point that will maybe take out a few of their

00:08:55.940 --> 00:08:56.940
points.

00:08:56.940 --> 00:08:59.860
And because you only have a certain amount
of time, how efficiently are you able to use

00:08:59.860 --> 00:09:04.450
that time to both refute the points that they're
saying, as well as bring up your own points

00:09:04.450 --> 00:09:06.779
that are hopefully harder to refute.

00:09:06.779 --> 00:09:10.860
So it becomes a kind of process of being very
good at listening and taking notes.

00:09:10.860 --> 00:09:16.640
Then being able to structure how do you want
to set up your replies so that you're gaining

00:09:16.640 --> 00:09:21.600
time advantage, if you will, with your persuasive
ability to give you more time to make your

00:09:21.600 --> 00:09:26.540
own row of arguments so that hopefully they
don't have time in their thing to properly

00:09:26.540 --> 00:09:27.770
address and attack everything.

00:09:27.770 --> 00:09:29.779
Tim Ferriss: Okay, great.

00:09:29.779 --> 00:09:32.800
So, as promised, I'm going to move forward
in the chronology.

00:09:32.800 --> 00:09:33.810
Justin Gary: This is great.

00:09:33.810 --> 00:09:36.230
I've never been asked about this before, so
this is fun.

00:09:36.230 --> 00:09:42.940
Tim Ferriss: Well, I'm trying to do my job
as best I can.

00:09:42.940 --> 00:09:43.940
Returning to Magic.

00:09:43.940 --> 00:09:48.829
So you mentioned that led to all these adventures
traveling around the world, competing.

00:09:48.829 --> 00:09:50.029
What does that mean?

00:09:50.029 --> 00:09:59.860
Could you explain what that looks like in
terms of incentives, stakes, how profitable

00:09:59.860 --> 00:10:03.880
is it for someone to be a full-time Magic
player?

00:10:03.880 --> 00:10:10.390
I imagine it depends on the person, but what
does it mean to travel around the world and

00:10:10.390 --> 00:10:11.390
compete?

00:10:11.390 --> 00:10:12.829
Justin Gary: So now, we're taking this back
now.

00:10:12.829 --> 00:10:13.839
Date myself a little bit.

00:10:13.839 --> 00:10:18.620
But this is '97 when I won the US National
Championships and the core of my career was

00:10:18.620 --> 00:10:23.100
about the six years from '97 to about 2003.

00:10:23.100 --> 00:10:26.800
That time there was what was called the Magic
Pro Tour, which was put on by the company

00:10:26.800 --> 00:10:30.790
that made the game as a way to promote it
and they would have millions of dollars of

00:10:30.790 --> 00:10:32.320
prizes available.

00:10:32.320 --> 00:10:34.120
So at any given tournament you could win.

00:10:34.120 --> 00:10:37.199
So my biggest tournament winnings would typically
be in the $30,000 range.

00:10:37.199 --> 00:10:41.071
So a pro tour in Houston where I won that
or these different tournaments.

00:10:41.071 --> 00:10:46.950
So to give you a sense, a first place in one
of those tournaments was $30,000-ish all the

00:10:46.950 --> 00:10:50.530
way down till top 32, maybe you'd get a thousand
dollars.

00:10:50.530 --> 00:10:53.440
Then you'd have to get invited to these tournaments.

00:10:53.440 --> 00:10:56.570
There'd be maybe 300 people-ish that would
get invited.

00:10:56.570 --> 00:10:58.570
So I would do a tournament in Prague.

00:10:58.570 --> 00:11:00.090
Then there'd be another one in Sydney.

00:11:00.090 --> 00:11:01.230
Then there'd be another one in Seattle.

00:11:01.230 --> 00:11:05.709
There'd be another one in Tokyo and so we
got to literally just meet up.

00:11:05.709 --> 00:11:08.800
And again, I'm in high school, I'm a teenager,
so this is just like crazy.

00:11:08.800 --> 00:11:10.190
Tim Ferriss: It's a lot of money.

00:11:10.190 --> 00:11:12.370
Justin Gary: Oh, my god, it's crazy money,
right?

00:11:12.370 --> 00:11:15.620
So I was just, such a fun thing.

00:11:15.620 --> 00:11:20.660
But when you think about it as an adult, it's
not a crazy, crazy amount of money, but it

00:11:20.660 --> 00:11:21.839
was great at the time.

00:11:21.839 --> 00:11:26.740
You can't win every tournament, but when I
was playing at my peak, it was maybe $80,000

00:11:26.740 --> 00:11:31.560
a year I was making, so it's still not —
Tim Ferriss: That's no joke for a teenager?

00:11:31.560 --> 00:11:32.920
Justin Gary: At that time, it was great.

00:11:32.920 --> 00:11:34.500
I mean, it's how I paid my way through college.

00:11:34.500 --> 00:11:37.720
So it was better than flipping burgers, I'll
put it that way.

00:11:37.720 --> 00:11:39.950
Tim Ferriss: What was your major in college?

00:11:39.950 --> 00:11:41.670
Justin Gary: I was a philosophy major.

00:11:41.670 --> 00:11:43.660
Tim Ferriss: How did you choose philosophy?

00:11:43.660 --> 00:11:48.070
Justin Gary: Well, as you might guess, I love
debating.

00:11:48.070 --> 00:11:51.500
I mean, I've always been very interested in
just sort of talking about the deep questions,

00:11:51.500 --> 00:11:55.920
trying to get down to the fundamentals of
what is interesting and what matters.

00:11:55.920 --> 00:12:03.610
I tried a few different majors in history
and I was on my path to be a lawyer because

00:12:03.610 --> 00:12:06.810
my parents are lawyers and that's what I thought
I was supposed to do.

00:12:06.810 --> 00:12:11.700
But I found most classes, when I went to them,
the job as a student in the class was to regurgitate

00:12:11.700 --> 00:12:16.860
whatever it was that the professor told you
back to them and that bored the hell out of

00:12:16.860 --> 00:12:17.860
me.

00:12:17.860 --> 00:12:20.980
Whereas in philosophy, the job was to make
a reasonable argument.

00:12:20.980 --> 00:12:25.250
It didn't matter, just like in debate, it
didn't matter what your position was, is can

00:12:25.250 --> 00:12:26.250
you defend it?

00:12:26.250 --> 00:12:27.290
Do you have good logic for it?

00:12:27.290 --> 00:12:30.279
And to try to, I loved being proven wrong.

00:12:30.279 --> 00:12:31.930
I loved when I would have, I think there was
something, this was a kind of strength and

00:12:31.930 --> 00:12:32.930
a weakness.

00:12:32.930 --> 00:12:35.300
I loved debating with people and I loved it
when someone told me something I didn't know

00:12:35.300 --> 00:12:36.470
or convinced me I was wrong.

00:12:36.470 --> 00:12:37.470
I was like, "That's amazing.

00:12:37.470 --> 00:12:38.470
That's great."

00:12:38.470 --> 00:12:42.070
It took me a very long time to realize most
people are not like that.

00:12:42.070 --> 00:12:47.720
In high school I was voted most likely to
disagree with anything you say.

00:12:47.720 --> 00:12:52.660
So I learned to be a little bit more cautious
and compassionate in my conversations with

00:12:52.660 --> 00:12:54.051
people, but I really enjoyed it.

00:12:54.051 --> 00:12:59.720
So philosophy let me explore that space of
really what matters in the world and what

00:12:59.720 --> 00:13:03.279
matters to me and really refine my thinking
and get challenged on my thinking when I was

00:13:03.279 --> 00:13:04.870
unclear or fuzzy.

00:13:04.870 --> 00:13:11.310
Tim Ferriss: I want to mention for people
who may not notice this pattern that on this

00:13:11.310 --> 00:13:18.550
podcast there is disproportionate representation
of former philosophy majors, however, that

00:13:18.550 --> 00:13:23.199
could be severe survivorship bias.

00:13:23.199 --> 00:13:28.810
I'm sure there are many, many philosophy majors
who do not end up on large podcasts.

00:13:28.810 --> 00:13:30.350
However, it is notable to me.

00:13:30.350 --> 00:13:36.589
You have people who are deeply interested
in philosophy who also end up in these conversations

00:13:36.589 --> 00:13:37.589
with me.

00:13:37.589 --> 00:13:40.850
Reid Hoffman would be another example who,
I'm not sure if he majored in philosophy,

00:13:40.850 --> 00:13:45.160
but he has taught philosophy and takes it
very, very seriously.

00:13:45.160 --> 00:13:52.079
Why were you comfortable and maybe even invigorated
by being proven wrong or converted in that

00:13:52.079 --> 00:13:53.079
way?

00:13:53.079 --> 00:13:56.820
Did that come from being raised in a family
of lawyers?

00:13:56.820 --> 00:13:59.079
Did it come from something else?

00:13:59.079 --> 00:14:00.830
What contributed to that?

00:14:00.830 --> 00:14:01.830
Justin Gary: Yes.

00:14:01.830 --> 00:14:02.980
I think there's two factors here.

00:14:02.980 --> 00:14:04.779
I think one, you're absolutely right.

00:14:04.779 --> 00:14:11.399
My parents were wonderful in the sense that
as a kid, I could argue my way to a later

00:14:11.399 --> 00:14:15.899
bedtime or I could argue my way to, "Hey,
if I finished my homework, I can get an extra

00:14:15.899 --> 00:14:18.190
setting of dessert," or whatever.

00:14:18.190 --> 00:14:22.050
They encouraged if I could kind of debate
my way to something, then I could get it and

00:14:22.050 --> 00:14:24.589
so it trained me very young.

00:14:24.589 --> 00:14:28.980
And similarly, my dad and I, we would be playing
cutthroat games of Monopoly around the kitchen

00:14:28.980 --> 00:14:30.850
table quite a bit.

00:14:30.850 --> 00:14:36.320
But they did, they reinforced that after the
game, what did you learn?

00:14:36.320 --> 00:14:38.120
If you lost, what did you learn from it?

00:14:38.120 --> 00:14:42.639
And that's the biggest thing that I credit
for, certainly my success in Magic, and I've

00:14:42.639 --> 00:14:48.430
tried to apply that everywhere else in my
life is that when you lose, which you inevitably

00:14:48.430 --> 00:14:49.430
are going to lose in life.

00:14:49.430 --> 00:14:53.120
It doesn't matter what it is that you look
first to, "Okay, what could I have done differently?

00:14:53.120 --> 00:14:56.430
How could I have set this up differently so
that I wouldn't even be in this position?"

00:14:56.430 --> 00:15:00.529
It's easy when you're playing a game like
Magic where your opponent draws a lucky card.

00:15:00.529 --> 00:15:04.019
It's a one out of 60 that they draw that card
and they beat you because of it.

00:15:04.019 --> 00:15:07.800
And you'd hear in the hallways, other people
would be complaining about what's called bad

00:15:07.800 --> 00:15:08.800
beats, right?

00:15:08.800 --> 00:15:09.800
"I got unlucky.

00:15:09.800 --> 00:15:10.800
Oh, my God."

00:15:10.800 --> 00:15:11.800
But the best players wouldn't do that.

00:15:11.800 --> 00:15:15.260
The best players would say, "Actually, if
five turns earlier I had made this different

00:15:15.260 --> 00:15:18.550
play, it wouldn't have mattered what card
they drew at that turn."

00:15:18.550 --> 00:15:24.490
That skillset was something that was drilled
into me, and it's something I definitely credit

00:15:24.490 --> 00:15:26.959
quite a bit of success across a variety of
fields.

00:15:26.959 --> 00:15:35.170
Tim Ferriss: This might seem a strange question,
but do Magic competitors — or did they study

00:15:35.170 --> 00:15:41.589
games in the way that chess players study
historic games of chess where those moves

00:15:41.589 --> 00:15:45.209
and decisions are recorded in some way?

00:15:45.209 --> 00:15:47.259
I'm not sure if it lends itself to that.

00:15:47.259 --> 00:15:49.060
Justin Gary: Oh, it absolutely does.

00:15:49.060 --> 00:15:50.510
And people would break these things down.

00:15:50.510 --> 00:15:54.600
So again, we're going to take ourselves in
the way back machine, '97, '98.

00:15:54.600 --> 00:15:58.260
This era, the internet is still relatively
new, right?

00:15:58.260 --> 00:16:03.149
Still relatively light and so people would
go to what at the time was called the Magic

00:16:03.149 --> 00:16:07.019
Dojo, which you can now find in the internet
archives.

00:16:07.019 --> 00:16:11.840
It was where everybody would post tournament
reports of, "Okay, here's what I played, here's

00:16:11.840 --> 00:16:12.840
what happened."

00:16:12.840 --> 00:16:17.860
And people would comment on them and break
down kind of more classic bulletin board style.

00:16:17.860 --> 00:16:23.550
That's where the core of Magic strategy and
discussion started to form.

00:16:23.550 --> 00:16:26.750
And now there's dozens of sites and dozens
of places where you can find this.

00:16:26.750 --> 00:16:30.220
But it was unlike with chess where people
have been doing this strategy and breaking

00:16:30.220 --> 00:16:34.590
down games for hundreds of years, Magic's
30 years old or whatever and at the time it

00:16:34.590 --> 00:16:35.590
was very new.

00:16:35.590 --> 00:16:41.680
And so I got to see the origins of these strategies
come together and break down individual games

00:16:41.680 --> 00:16:44.040
and individual moves, and it became obsessive.

00:16:44.040 --> 00:16:51.029
I mean, if you look at my old college notebooks,
they are covered in Magic deck ideas and scribbles,

00:16:51.029 --> 00:16:54.350
and at least as much as there are notes about
philosophy or whatever it was I was supposed

00:16:54.350 --> 00:16:55.580
to be studying.

00:16:55.580 --> 00:16:58.839
Tim Ferriss: What were the main innovations?

00:16:58.839 --> 00:17:09.329
If you had to try to identify some game design
elements that led to or contributed to Magic

00:17:09.329 --> 00:17:15.310
becoming such a global phenomenon, what would
you say were the innovations?

00:17:15.310 --> 00:17:22.970
I mean, there's definitely the slot machine,
dopamine potential of buying these packs just

00:17:22.970 --> 00:17:25.770
like buying baseball cards, right?

00:17:25.770 --> 00:17:33.640
But what else is there in the game that made
it at least have the possibility of exploding

00:17:33.640 --> 00:17:34.890
in the way that it did?

00:17:34.890 --> 00:17:37.220
Justin Gary: So there's a lot of factors.

00:17:37.220 --> 00:17:41.160
There's a deep answer, but we have a long-form
podcast, so if you don't mind.

00:17:41.160 --> 00:17:43.370
Tim Ferriss: I love deep answers.

00:17:43.370 --> 00:17:44.400
Take all the time you want.

00:17:44.400 --> 00:17:49.370
Justin Gary: I teach game design and there's
sort of five major categories of what people

00:17:49.370 --> 00:17:54.010
are looking for out of games and Magic hits
all of them in ways.

00:17:54.010 --> 00:17:57.660
So I'll talk about the categories first and
then I'll talk about why Magic succeeded in

00:17:57.660 --> 00:17:59.200
such an incredible way.

00:17:59.200 --> 00:18:01.480
So the first is immersion.

00:18:01.480 --> 00:18:05.590
You're looking for certain experiences or
you're looking to craft a story.

00:18:05.590 --> 00:18:07.330
The second is connection, right?

00:18:07.330 --> 00:18:11.799
You're looking to socialize, you want help
other people, you want to connect with others.

00:18:11.799 --> 00:18:13.860
The third is aspiration.

00:18:13.860 --> 00:18:16.350
This can be in competition, this can be achievements.

00:18:16.350 --> 00:18:22.110
You want to win, you want to prove you're
better than, prove you can achieve something.

00:18:22.110 --> 00:18:24.789
Then the fourth is growth.

00:18:24.789 --> 00:18:30.280
You want to learn, you want to continue to
move up and improve your skills.

00:18:30.280 --> 00:18:32.050
And the last is expression.

00:18:32.050 --> 00:18:36.350
This idea that you want to customize or role
playing works this way because we express

00:18:36.350 --> 00:18:37.800
different parts of ourselves through role
playing.

00:18:37.800 --> 00:18:40.450
And so those are kind of the major categories.

00:18:40.450 --> 00:18:48.150
I think Magic allowed people to both not only
have these competitions like I was doing,

00:18:48.150 --> 00:18:54.200
but it allowed people to customize and grow
in a world where it's infinitely deep.

00:18:54.200 --> 00:18:57.909
At a certain point with chess, it's a deep
game, but there's only so many pieces.

00:18:57.909 --> 00:18:58.950
I can learn them all.

00:18:58.950 --> 00:19:02.390
I can know kind of the frame I'm playing it.

00:19:02.390 --> 00:19:06.890
With Magic, there's constantly new cards coming,
there's constantly new things to do, and I

00:19:06.890 --> 00:19:10.730
get to own, I get to customize and own my
experience.

00:19:10.730 --> 00:19:15.600
So to relate it to something you're more familiar
with, Dungeons &amp; Dragons, the idea that you

00:19:15.600 --> 00:19:20.110
get to tell the story, that you get to create
the character is so powerful and so immersive

00:19:20.110 --> 00:19:21.150
and Magic is very similar.

00:19:21.150 --> 00:19:25.200
If you want to build a deck with all elf cards,
you could do that.

00:19:25.200 --> 00:19:29.310
If you want to build a deck that has every
card that you could imagine and is 300 cards

00:19:29.310 --> 00:19:31.730
deep, you can do that.

00:19:31.730 --> 00:19:34.440
If you want to be the hyper competitive, hyper-focused
guy, you could do that.

00:19:34.440 --> 00:19:38.860
There are so many experiences that you get
to, in a sense, design your own game.

00:19:38.860 --> 00:19:42.150
And it's one of the things that I've learned,
much like you talked about, a lot of philosophy

00:19:42.150 --> 00:19:49.230
majors end up on your podcast, a lot of the
game designers started from a game like Magic

00:19:49.230 --> 00:19:51.010
or Dungeons &amp; Dragons.

00:19:51.010 --> 00:19:54.580
Because as you play, you're crafting your
own experience, and I think that was such

00:19:54.580 --> 00:19:58.260
a unique and powerful thing.

00:19:58.260 --> 00:20:01.510
Dungeons &amp; Dragons could be a little intimidating
for people because it's so open-ended.

00:20:01.510 --> 00:20:02.799
Tim Ferriss: No, it's so intimidating.

00:20:02.799 --> 00:20:03.799
It's so complex.

00:20:03.799 --> 00:20:05.250
Or at least it can be.

00:20:05.250 --> 00:20:06.250
Justin Gary: It can be.

00:20:06.250 --> 00:20:07.250
Tim Ferriss: It's a real commitment.

00:20:07.250 --> 00:20:08.250
Justin Gary: It can be.

00:20:08.250 --> 00:20:09.250
Exactly.

00:20:09.250 --> 00:20:13.480
Whereas Magic, you're playing inside of these
box and these rules, but once you start exploring

00:20:13.480 --> 00:20:15.260
it, then this whole world opens up.

00:20:15.260 --> 00:20:21.350
I think that was really the "magic" of it,
and it really made it feel like something

00:20:21.350 --> 00:20:24.309
that was very new and spawned an entire genre
of games.

00:20:24.309 --> 00:20:30.220
Tim Ferriss: And I remember at one point,
I think it was certainly at the time, I think

00:20:30.220 --> 00:20:34.740
TSR, I was reading this book called, I believe
it's Art &amp; Arcana.

00:20:34.740 --> 00:20:35.740
Or arcana.

00:20:35.740 --> 00:20:39.230
I'm not sure how you pronounce that word properly,
even though I've read it a thousand times,

00:20:39.230 --> 00:20:44.659
which is a visual history of Dungeons &amp; Dragons.

00:20:44.659 --> 00:20:49.169
And at some point they realized one of the
major stumbling blocks was people having to

00:20:49.169 --> 00:20:52.070
develop from scratch their own characters.

00:20:52.070 --> 00:20:58.190
And they provided out-of-the-box templates,
which was one of the ways they jump started

00:20:58.190 --> 00:20:59.190
wider adoption.

00:20:59.190 --> 00:21:06.471
It's giving people an easy place to start
with some type of positive constraints, let's

00:21:06.471 --> 00:21:09.320
just say, in a sense.

00:21:09.320 --> 00:21:11.309
That makes me think about Magic.

00:21:11.309 --> 00:21:15.660
Now, can you remind me and listeners of who
developed Magic?

00:21:15.660 --> 00:21:18.920
Justin Gary: So Richard Garfield was the lead
designer.

00:21:18.920 --> 00:21:21.800
He created the game along with of course a
lot of other people that supported, but he

00:21:21.800 --> 00:21:22.860
was the lead, the creator.

00:21:22.860 --> 00:21:27.720
Tim Ferriss: I remember listening to, and
you're going to have to fact-check me on this,

00:21:27.720 --> 00:21:32.640
but I think I'm recalling this correctly,
listening to at least one, maybe two interviews

00:21:32.640 --> 00:21:38.130
with Richard on your podcast, Think Like a
Game Designer.

00:21:38.130 --> 00:21:44.380
He was talking about game development, and
I guess folks who were auditing the cards

00:21:44.380 --> 00:21:50.360
and the decks and the idea that some cards
would get retired or removed from circulation

00:21:50.360 --> 00:21:52.170
or banned because they were too powerful.

00:21:52.170 --> 00:21:57.770
But if there were no cards that were too powerful
that they were also erring on the side of

00:21:57.770 --> 00:21:59.520
caution, something along those lines.

00:21:59.520 --> 00:22:04.700
Could you expand on what I'm very clumsily
trying to recall from an interview I listened

00:22:04.700 --> 00:22:05.860
to a year ago?

00:22:05.860 --> 00:22:10.789
Justin Gary: So there's this idea in games
of balance, which is a very, it's a term that

00:22:10.789 --> 00:22:13.299
people throw around a lot but gets very confused.

00:22:13.299 --> 00:22:18.850
And the principle of it is that you don't
want some part of your game to be so unfair

00:22:18.850 --> 00:22:21.460
that you can't, nobody can beat you.

00:22:21.460 --> 00:22:26.909
So if it's in Tic-Tac-Toe, if you get to make
three moves at once, that would be pretty

00:22:26.909 --> 00:22:27.909
unfair.

00:22:27.909 --> 00:22:28.909
The game is already over.

00:22:28.909 --> 00:22:30.250
You got your three in a row, that doesn't
make any sense.

00:22:30.250 --> 00:22:31.950
So the game's no fun.

00:22:31.950 --> 00:22:36.309
So the idea is you want to make sure that
there's no one strategy that's too good.

00:22:36.309 --> 00:22:41.630
But some people think that that means everything
needs to be exactly the same and that's also

00:22:41.630 --> 00:22:46.179
not true because the idea, if every card is
just as equally good as every other card,

00:22:46.179 --> 00:22:48.970
then you deny people that discovery.

00:22:48.970 --> 00:22:52.260
You deny people that ability to learn and
say, "Oh, wait, hold on."

00:22:52.260 --> 00:22:53.799
As a new player you don't know anything.

00:22:53.799 --> 00:22:54.980
And then you can learn, oh, actually, you
know what?

00:22:54.980 --> 00:22:56.039
This card's better than this card.

00:22:56.039 --> 00:23:00.080
Or the excitement of chasing and finding something
that's very exciting that now you can combine

00:23:00.080 --> 00:23:02.490
with something else that you have and suddenly
that gets better.

00:23:02.490 --> 00:23:06.559
And so the way I define balance is not everything
has to be equal.

00:23:06.559 --> 00:23:11.150
It's that everything, there's no one strategy
that should be unbeatable.

00:23:11.150 --> 00:23:15.289
The best analogy is if you think Rock-Paper-Scissors.

00:23:15.289 --> 00:23:16.660
Perfectly balanced game.

00:23:16.660 --> 00:23:20.360
Rock is great, but if I know you're going
to throw rock, I've got a plan.

00:23:20.360 --> 00:23:23.050
I could throw paper and I'm going to be okay.

00:23:23.050 --> 00:23:28.110
So there's a lot of ways that you can approach
balance, but that can give you a sense of

00:23:28.110 --> 00:23:29.450
what it should look like.

00:23:29.450 --> 00:23:34.700
And so for Richard, he wanted to make things
that were exciting and chase and you're really

00:23:34.700 --> 00:23:36.789
hoping you open up in a pack.

00:23:36.789 --> 00:23:39.880
Of course, he didn't have any idea that the
game was going to be as popular as it was.

00:23:39.880 --> 00:23:44.610
He talked about in the podcast episode that
he thought you would just never even see all

00:23:44.610 --> 00:23:45.610
the cards.

00:23:45.610 --> 00:23:47.420
That some people would have a couple of packs
and other people would have a couple of packs,

00:23:47.420 --> 00:23:49.770
and you just discovered as you went.

00:23:49.770 --> 00:23:54.330
And that is actually, it's an exciting prospect
but very quickly people would buy all the

00:23:54.330 --> 00:23:55.330
cards.

00:23:55.330 --> 00:23:58.159
Now there's spoilers on the internet and everybody
sees everything immediately.

00:23:58.159 --> 00:24:00.500
So it's changed a lot since his initial concept.

00:24:00.500 --> 00:24:07.660
Tim Ferriss: All right, so as the next lily
pad, I want to explore one of two things.

00:24:07.660 --> 00:24:11.620
We're going to get to both so you can choose
which you think makes sense to talk about

00:24:11.620 --> 00:24:12.620
first.

00:24:12.620 --> 00:24:17.870
So the first option is your path to where
you are now as a senior partner at a law firm.

00:24:17.870 --> 00:24:20.170
Just kidding.

00:24:20.170 --> 00:24:21.549
So law school.

00:24:21.549 --> 00:24:33.429
The other is what happened in your Magic life
after winning the nationals and that story?

00:24:33.429 --> 00:24:36.630
So which of those do you think makes sense
to tackle first?

00:24:36.630 --> 00:24:38.830
Justin Gary: Well, I think let's start —
Tim Ferriss: Or you can imagine, or you could

00:24:38.830 --> 00:24:39.830
talk about both in tandem.

00:24:39.830 --> 00:24:40.830
Either one, either or.

00:24:40.830 --> 00:24:44.500
Justin Gary: We'll go in chronology order
so that the Magic career kind of has its arc

00:24:44.500 --> 00:24:48.679
before my law career has its much shorter
arc.

00:24:48.679 --> 00:24:51.260
So I'm 17 years old, I win the US National
Championships.

00:24:51.260 --> 00:24:53.970
This was a huge, awesome, cool thing for me.

00:24:53.970 --> 00:25:00.570
But then I go to the world championships and
at that point in Magic's life cycle, the US

00:25:00.570 --> 00:25:01.570
was dominant.

00:25:01.570 --> 00:25:04.860
The US had never lost a world championships
ever.

00:25:04.860 --> 00:25:09.750
And not just me, but a team of people that
were representing the US and unfortunately

00:25:09.750 --> 00:25:10.750
we did not win.

00:25:10.750 --> 00:25:12.299
We did not do very well.

00:25:12.299 --> 00:25:19.190
So that world championships, I had this albatross
around my neck of being the first loser US

00:25:19.190 --> 00:25:20.190
National Champion.

00:25:20.190 --> 00:25:21.970
At least this is the story I was telling myself.

00:25:21.970 --> 00:25:25.190
So I was like, "Okay, this is not great."

00:25:25.190 --> 00:25:28.340
And my career was good, I was doing good,
but it was something that always stuck with

00:25:28.340 --> 00:25:32.909
me and so I kept wanting to try to get back
on the national team to redeem myself.

00:25:32.909 --> 00:25:37.090
So fast forward five years later.

00:25:37.090 --> 00:25:39.360
Finally the US loses again.

00:25:39.360 --> 00:25:40.360
Loses to the Germans.

00:25:40.360 --> 00:25:41.960
So now I'm not the only loser.

00:25:41.960 --> 00:25:47.080
Not that I was rooting against the US, but
it was nice to not be the only loser.

00:25:47.080 --> 00:25:51.790
And then the very next year I make it back
on the US National Team.

00:25:51.790 --> 00:25:55.510
And so this is my chance, my chance at redemption.

00:25:55.510 --> 00:26:00.850
After six years, and I'm with two other people
on the team and the two people that are on

00:26:00.850 --> 00:26:03.390
the team with me, they were pretty new to
the game.

00:26:03.390 --> 00:26:07.299
They kind of had come out of nowhere just
like I did when I first started, and I was

00:26:07.299 --> 00:26:09.570
so committed to making sure we did well.

00:26:09.570 --> 00:26:15.320
I paid to fly both of them to Boston where
I was living at the time, to stay at my place

00:26:15.320 --> 00:26:16.320
and work.

00:26:16.320 --> 00:26:21.740
And we trained for a month, a month and a
half together with my team, who was Your Move

00:26:21.740 --> 00:26:25.159
Games was the local store and group we would
playtest with.

00:26:25.159 --> 00:26:31.330
So we trained, we slept on my couch, we worked
together to be able to make it back.

00:26:31.330 --> 00:26:33.020
That year, the world championships were in
Berlin.

00:26:33.020 --> 00:26:40.289
So we're going into enemy territory and it
comes down to literally we make it to the

00:26:40.289 --> 00:26:42.570
final table, to the final game.

00:26:42.570 --> 00:26:48.539
It comes down to me playing this match and
we've got stage lights, we're covering it.

00:26:48.539 --> 00:26:51.120
This is everything I have worked for.

00:26:51.120 --> 00:26:55.080
There's some online records of the experience
but I remember just sort of feeling in that

00:26:55.080 --> 00:26:58.730
moment that — I'd been in tournaments, I'd
won tournaments, I'd lost tournaments.

00:26:58.730 --> 00:27:00.289
There's a certain amount of excitement and
pressure.

00:27:00.289 --> 00:27:04.781
But for this one, it was so much of a personal
story, a personal kind of drama that I was

00:27:04.781 --> 00:27:06.600
playing out for me.

00:27:06.600 --> 00:27:11.840
And I drew my hand the first time for this
final match where everything's on the line,

00:27:11.840 --> 00:27:13.110
people are watching.

00:27:13.110 --> 00:27:15.909
There's a rule in Magic where if you don't
like your hand, if you don't like the starting

00:27:15.909 --> 00:27:17.730
cards you have, you can take a mulligan.

00:27:17.730 --> 00:27:22.090
And what that means is you throw the cards
away, but you get one less card and it's a

00:27:22.090 --> 00:27:23.090
big deal.

00:27:23.090 --> 00:27:24.830
Less cards is a huge disadvantage in the game.

00:27:24.830 --> 00:27:28.899
But I draw my hand and I'm trying to make
this decision, and I'm just, "All right, do

00:27:28.899 --> 00:27:32.110
I deal with what I have or do I just take
a shot?"

00:27:32.110 --> 00:27:33.740
And I didn't like the hand.

00:27:33.740 --> 00:27:34.740
I wasn't going to take a shot.

00:27:34.740 --> 00:27:36.530
I wasn't going to let the world championships
be decided on that hand.

00:27:36.530 --> 00:27:38.500
So I mulligan, throw the cards away.

00:27:38.500 --> 00:27:43.500
My teammates are groaning in the background
just like, "All right, here we go."

00:27:43.500 --> 00:27:46.000
Shuffle up, draw the hand.

00:27:46.000 --> 00:27:49.309
And it becomes one of those moments where
you kind of see it come together.

00:27:49.309 --> 00:27:50.750
The hand was good enough.

00:27:50.750 --> 00:27:52.490
We start playing it out.

00:27:52.490 --> 00:27:55.010
I get some board advantage.

00:27:55.010 --> 00:27:58.400
My opponent's kind of on his back foot and
he's got a couple tricks.

00:27:58.400 --> 00:28:00.980
There's some cards I have to play around,
I'm not really sure.

00:28:00.980 --> 00:28:08.179
And then finally I'm able to get to the point
and win this fight after however long people

00:28:08.179 --> 00:28:12.820
rush the stage, able to finally take that
off of my back.

00:28:12.820 --> 00:28:14.779
And it was a very exciting moment for me.

00:28:14.779 --> 00:28:18.870
It was a very fun journey, and it was a result
of a lot of hard work.

00:28:18.870 --> 00:28:24.390
It was a lot of dedication that got there,
but it kind of felt, for me that it was the

00:28:24.390 --> 00:28:25.390
end of my Magic career.

00:28:25.390 --> 00:28:28.890
It was the thing that I had, I had been striving
to do that.

00:28:28.890 --> 00:28:33.299
I kind of checked those boxes and that was
kind of like, let me move to this next stage.

00:28:33.299 --> 00:28:42.230
Tim Ferriss: So before we get to your short
arc in the world of law, was there something,

00:28:42.230 --> 00:28:47.571
coming back to your previous comment that
you learned after losing on the world championship

00:28:47.571 --> 00:28:53.779
stage first, was there something you learned
or decided to change about your approach after

00:28:53.779 --> 00:28:54.779
that?

00:28:54.779 --> 00:28:57.529
Justin Gary: I think that this is, now we're
talking about years of gap in between.

00:28:57.529 --> 00:29:03.010
And so what I did learn and the major thing
that I realized in that Magic is generally

00:29:03.010 --> 00:29:05.720
a single-person game.

00:29:05.720 --> 00:29:08.230
I'm playing a match against somebody else
in competition.

00:29:08.230 --> 00:29:10.470
But in this case, it's a team game.

00:29:10.470 --> 00:29:14.789
And I think the biggest shift in focus was
I didn't just think about myself.

00:29:14.789 --> 00:29:19.890
I had to think about how do I lift everybody
else up and all thrive together to be able

00:29:19.890 --> 00:29:21.340
to make it through.

00:29:21.340 --> 00:29:26.380
And it really changed the way I focused there,
changed the way I focused with other teams

00:29:26.380 --> 00:29:27.860
I've worked with.

00:29:27.860 --> 00:29:29.260
It made a huge impact.

00:29:29.260 --> 00:29:33.600
And then just being willing to, again, come
back from a loss and not let it discourage

00:29:33.600 --> 00:29:35.240
you that you can't overcome it.

00:29:35.240 --> 00:29:40.120
But I think that being able to work, typically
most of the games I've played have been single

00:29:40.120 --> 00:29:45.700
player, whether it was debate or wrestling
or most of Magic, it was all about, "I'm here

00:29:45.700 --> 00:29:46.700
to win."

00:29:46.700 --> 00:29:49.970
And I think my shift in mindset was, "No,
I'm here for us to win."

00:29:49.970 --> 00:29:53.039
And that was a really huge turnaround for
me.

00:29:53.039 --> 00:29:56.539
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, that's a big, big shift
in terms of looking through the prism.

00:29:56.539 --> 00:29:57.539
Tim Ferriss: So law school.

00:29:57.539 --> 00:29:58.539
Justin Gary: Yes.

00:29:58.539 --> 00:29:59.539
Tim Ferriss: Tell us about law school.

00:29:59.539 --> 00:30:03.250
Justin Gary: So just to frame this, right,
so I've already mentioned both of my parents

00:30:03.250 --> 00:30:06.169
are lawyers, my mom's a lawyer, my dad's a
lawyer, my stepdad's a lawyer.

00:30:06.169 --> 00:30:08.539
I was debate captain.

00:30:08.539 --> 00:30:10.809
I was supposed to be a lawyer.

00:30:10.809 --> 00:30:16.220
That was just my path from as early as I can
remember, that's what I was supposed to do.

00:30:16.220 --> 00:30:19.970
And even though I had been making a living
and traveling around the world playing Magic,

00:30:19.970 --> 00:30:24.669
at no point did it ever occur to me that I
would make a living in games, none.

00:30:24.669 --> 00:30:28.640
It was just, oh, this is a fun thing to do
while I'm in college.

00:30:28.640 --> 00:30:30.690
I had this mental block.

00:30:30.690 --> 00:30:36.340
And so I graduate, go get into NYU Law.

00:30:36.340 --> 00:30:38.800
I teach the LSAT for a while.

00:30:38.800 --> 00:30:43.690
I go to law school and I'm lucky, I'm a pretty
happy guy by default.

00:30:43.690 --> 00:30:47.169
But this was the first time I was really depressed.

00:30:47.169 --> 00:30:50.850
I was very unhappy for an extended period
of time.

00:30:50.850 --> 00:30:58.679
And I will remember very specifically, there's
one day I'm getting up in Brooklyn, snowing

00:30:58.679 --> 00:31:04.081
outside, just covered in ice in the floor,
and I have to get trudged up, put on all of

00:31:04.081 --> 00:31:09.250
these ridiculous clothes, and go to get on
the A train and pretend like I'm not staring

00:31:09.250 --> 00:31:10.250
at anybody else.

00:31:10.250 --> 00:31:12.560
So everybody has to studiously avoid looking
at anybody else in New York.

00:31:12.560 --> 00:31:14.299
That's just the rule.

00:31:14.299 --> 00:31:16.210
And make it all the way in.

00:31:16.210 --> 00:31:20.900
Have my Boston cream donut for breakfast,
because that's just how I lived back then.

00:31:20.900 --> 00:31:26.620
And I go into this NYU law library, which
is like, you know, could picture this just

00:31:26.620 --> 00:31:30.940
cavernous, giant library with old oak and
towers of books.

00:31:30.940 --> 00:31:32.890
It was back when you had to use books to learn
things.

00:31:32.890 --> 00:31:35.510
It was a wild time.

00:31:35.510 --> 00:31:42.130
And I checked out the law books I had to read
before class and I'm — stack them up, these

00:31:42.130 --> 00:31:45.590
just towers around me, and I'm just there
and I'm there for hours just reading case

00:31:45.590 --> 00:31:48.920
law and just not very happy.

00:31:48.920 --> 00:31:51.529
But a couple of hours later I realized I've
got to get to class.

00:31:51.529 --> 00:31:57.490
And so I sit up and I have this jolt of pain
through my spine, like, huh, jolt of pain.

00:31:57.490 --> 00:31:59.159
I can barely move.

00:31:59.159 --> 00:32:00.820
I'm hobbling to get out of there.

00:32:00.820 --> 00:32:04.880
And I realize I threw my back out from reading,
from reading.

00:32:04.880 --> 00:32:06.520
That is how unhealthy I was.

00:32:06.520 --> 00:32:08.450
I was over about 40 pounds heavier than I
am now.

00:32:08.450 --> 00:32:10.110
How just miserable I was.

00:32:10.110 --> 00:32:13.400
And this was my first clue that I'm on the
wrong path.

00:32:13.400 --> 00:32:18.010
And I think this is something I, it's a really
important lesson, I think.

00:32:18.010 --> 00:32:25.050
I imagine there's a lot of overachievers in
your audience, and that this path of trying

00:32:25.050 --> 00:32:30.240
to win whatever game's in front of you, it
can be way worse to win the wrong game than

00:32:30.240 --> 00:32:33.049
even to lose one that you actually enjoy playing.

00:32:33.049 --> 00:32:35.659
And that was a big shift for me.

00:32:35.659 --> 00:32:41.559
And fortunately that summer I had an opportunity
to go, instead of interning at a law firm,

00:32:41.559 --> 00:32:47.081
I got to go work on a Marvel Comics card game,
a Marvel versus DC Comics card game for a

00:32:47.081 --> 00:32:48.890
company called Upper Deck in San Diego.

00:32:48.890 --> 00:32:53.159
So summer internship, working on comic book
games instead of — that sounded great.

00:32:53.159 --> 00:32:57.250
And so I flew across country to go and worked
there for the summer.

00:32:57.250 --> 00:33:00.930
And again, even though I knew I was miserable,
even though I'd had this terrible experience,

00:33:00.930 --> 00:33:05.140
even though I now had a kind of opportunity
in front of me still, my assumption at this

00:33:05.140 --> 00:33:07.620
point was, I'm going to go back to law school,
I'm going to go back.

00:33:07.620 --> 00:33:09.390
I've got to finish that path.

00:33:09.390 --> 00:33:13.160
But I come out to start working on games and
I am in love with it.

00:33:13.160 --> 00:33:14.740
I'm super passionate about it.

00:33:14.740 --> 00:33:15.740
I'm enjoying it.

00:33:15.740 --> 00:33:20.050
Again, we're debating these really interesting
ideas about design and definitely happy to

00:33:20.050 --> 00:33:22.399
dig into what that looks like.

00:33:22.399 --> 00:33:28.130
But then when they offer me a full-time job,
I'm still stressing about this.

00:33:28.130 --> 00:33:32.970
And it's still finally when I make the decision,
I tell my mom, she literally cries when I

00:33:32.970 --> 00:33:35.640
tell her I'm leaving law school to go become
a game designer.

00:33:35.640 --> 00:33:36.640
It was so hard.

00:33:36.640 --> 00:33:38.159
And I look back, and it's so silly to me.

00:33:38.159 --> 00:33:40.360
"This is one of the things you've taught me."

00:33:40.360 --> 00:33:42.340
I didn't have these words at the time, but,
"I learned it through you."

00:33:42.340 --> 00:33:47.200
This idea of fear-setting where, in fact,
I wasn't really giving up anything.

00:33:47.200 --> 00:33:50.160
I could always go back to law school, but
it just didn't feel like that at the time.

00:33:50.160 --> 00:33:53.929
And so being able to break out of that spell
and then go follow a path that was really

00:33:53.929 --> 00:33:57.639
passionate for me that I was really excited
about, one of the best decisions I ever made.

00:33:57.639 --> 00:33:59.870
Tim Ferriss: How did you find the internship?

00:33:59.870 --> 00:34:05.540
Justin Gary: So this was fortunate because
of my Magic career.

00:34:05.540 --> 00:34:10.579
They had reached out to me to just vet their
game and try to check it out and stuff.

00:34:10.579 --> 00:34:14.139
So some of the other people on the Magic Pro
tour who people who I'd known had already

00:34:14.139 --> 00:34:15.679
kind of been hired there.

00:34:15.679 --> 00:34:16.720
So they were like, "Hey."

00:34:16.720 --> 00:34:17.740
And they knew I was miserable.

00:34:17.740 --> 00:34:18.770
I would chat with them.

00:34:18.770 --> 00:34:20.669
They're like, "Hey, come out for the summer,
come work on this thing.

00:34:20.669 --> 00:34:22.220
You've got nothing to lose," kind of deal.

00:34:22.220 --> 00:34:26.751
Tim Ferriss: Has your mom come around, or
is she still hoping for that day when you

00:34:26.751 --> 00:34:27.751
return to law school?

00:34:27.751 --> 00:34:30.230
Justin Gary: No, my mom has come around.

00:34:30.230 --> 00:34:32.030
I'm sure she'll listen to this episode.

00:34:32.030 --> 00:34:33.349
Hi, Mom.

00:34:33.349 --> 00:34:34.679
She did.

00:34:34.679 --> 00:34:38.109
And this is the thing, the people in our lives,
there's a lot of pressure that we all feel,

00:34:38.109 --> 00:34:41.950
whether it's from parents, from friends, they
want the best for us.

00:34:41.950 --> 00:34:44.919
So you just want us, me, to be happy and safe
and comfortable.

00:34:44.919 --> 00:34:50.740
And you've got to realize that you, in order
to find the path that you actually want for

00:34:50.740 --> 00:34:53.889
yourself, the path that you will be fulfilled
by, you've got to be uncomfortable.

00:34:53.889 --> 00:34:55.230
You've got to be unsafe, right?

00:34:55.230 --> 00:35:00.130
Parents, I think, try to protect their kids
so much and reality, you can't.

00:35:00.130 --> 00:35:01.820
You've got to be able to push past that.

00:35:01.820 --> 00:35:03.220
And she knows that now.

00:35:03.220 --> 00:35:04.650
And she was very happy it all worked out.

00:35:04.650 --> 00:35:05.650
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:35:05.650 --> 00:35:06.650
All right.

00:35:06.650 --> 00:35:10.800
Well, we're going to fill in the gaps for
those people who aren't your mom listening.

00:35:10.800 --> 00:35:12.690
Hi, Mom.

00:35:12.690 --> 00:35:17.310
Since we have a lot to fill in and look now
I feel like I should defend your mom also,

00:35:17.310 --> 00:35:21.849
certain paths are more predictable than others,
and there is the perception and maybe even

00:35:21.849 --> 00:35:27.800
the reality of safety, consistency, stability
with certain choices.

00:35:27.800 --> 00:35:34.119
But as you said, choosing the right game is
more important than winning the game that

00:35:34.119 --> 00:35:36.619
just happens to be in front of you.

00:35:36.619 --> 00:35:41.099
And at least certainly in my experience, in
the experience of a lot of people on this

00:35:41.099 --> 00:35:46.630
podcast, and just because something is unpredictable
does not automatically make it unsafe.

00:35:46.630 --> 00:35:48.200
It just makes it less certain.

00:35:48.200 --> 00:35:53.000
And as you put it, many of these things, for
instance, I'll give you another example, and

00:35:53.000 --> 00:35:54.599
this is very analogous.

00:35:54.599 --> 00:36:02.570
When people invoke the names of, say, Zuckerberg
or someone else who dropped out as this throwing

00:36:02.570 --> 00:36:08.640
caution to the wind, burning the boats behind
them to take the risk of entrepreneurship,

00:36:08.640 --> 00:36:13.810
whenever possible, I try to point out that's
actually not the case for most people who,

00:36:13.810 --> 00:36:16.750
say, take a leave of absence from a Harvard
or Stanford.

00:36:16.750 --> 00:36:18.849
They can go back any time they want to go
back.

00:36:18.849 --> 00:36:20.040
So it's —
Justin Gary: Yes, 100 percent.

00:36:20.040 --> 00:36:21.040
100 percent.

00:36:21.040 --> 00:36:24.069
And again, you've said this better than honestly
anybody, like this idea that most of the fears

00:36:24.069 --> 00:36:29.829
that we have, this resistance to uncertainty
is just made up in our head that there's so

00:36:29.829 --> 00:36:34.460
many ways that you could recover and go back
and that if you can — just remember that

00:36:34.460 --> 00:36:37.892
I could have gone back to law school later
on when I quit my game design job to start

00:36:37.892 --> 00:36:40.250
a company, I could always get another job.

00:36:40.250 --> 00:36:45.490
Those things are those illusions of uncertainty
and fear.

00:36:45.490 --> 00:36:47.800
If you can push past that, it changes everything.

00:36:47.800 --> 00:36:52.050
I mean, most of the times we take risks, we're
not jumping off cliffs or into burning buildings.

00:36:52.050 --> 00:36:57.099
It's just, okay, yeah, your career takes a
different path or you're behind in school

00:36:57.099 --> 00:36:58.099
or whatever.

00:36:58.099 --> 00:36:59.540
It's a very low cost.

00:36:59.540 --> 00:37:01.000
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:37:01.000 --> 00:37:02.830
Most often recoverable.

00:37:02.830 --> 00:37:06.450
So I will, I'll link to fear-setting and people
can just search fear-setting, goal-setting

00:37:06.450 --> 00:37:07.890
with a hyphen in it.

00:37:07.890 --> 00:37:12.720
And all sorts of things will pop up if you
just search that or go to tim.blog/ted because

00:37:12.720 --> 00:37:19.109
that was the topic of my TED Talk, I suppose
quite a few years ago now.

00:37:19.109 --> 00:37:29.150
But moving on from fear-setting for a moment,
it might come back into the conversation,

00:37:29.150 --> 00:37:30.150
who knows?

00:37:30.150 --> 00:37:37.010
But what did that first year or six months,
month could be the first week, however you

00:37:37.010 --> 00:37:41.160
want to frame it, of designing games look
like.

00:37:41.160 --> 00:37:42.319
What was that experience like?

00:37:42.319 --> 00:37:43.319
Justin Gary: Yeah, yeah.

00:37:43.319 --> 00:37:47.610
So it's a really fun question because people
just make this assumption that because I was

00:37:47.610 --> 00:37:50.830
good at playing games, then that means I would
be good at making games.

00:37:50.830 --> 00:37:53.849
And they are 100 percent different skills.

00:37:53.849 --> 00:37:59.430
I did not think of myself as a creative person
there, thought there was just some kind of

00:37:59.430 --> 00:38:03.370
magic special secret sauce that other people
had, and I was just going to fake it and go

00:38:03.370 --> 00:38:05.420
out there and see what happened.

00:38:05.420 --> 00:38:09.470
And so when I go there, I'm having serious
imposter syndrome.

00:38:09.470 --> 00:38:10.470
I don't know what I'm doing.

00:38:10.470 --> 00:38:13.130
I don't know how to make this come to life.

00:38:13.130 --> 00:38:15.420
And I researched a ton.

00:38:15.420 --> 00:38:22.040
I read some great books on creativity, A Whack
on the Side of the Head is one I'd recommend.

00:38:22.040 --> 00:38:27.079
And I started to talk to other people who
I did respect as designers and kind of break

00:38:27.079 --> 00:38:28.079
it down.

00:38:28.079 --> 00:38:34.359
And over time I realized that there's nothing
that differentiates a creative person from

00:38:34.359 --> 00:38:36.190
a not-creative person other than process.

00:38:36.190 --> 00:38:41.490
And so as an analytically minded person, I
was able to break it down into what I call

00:38:41.490 --> 00:38:47.010
the core design loop, which is a six-step
process of how do you create not just games,

00:38:47.010 --> 00:38:50.110
but I think every creative field that applies
to.

00:38:50.110 --> 00:38:53.952
And so learning that, and I was very lucky,
I'll talk about the details of it.

00:38:53.952 --> 00:38:55.710
I won't leave you hanging on that piece, but
the experience being —

00:38:55.710 --> 00:38:59.140
Tim Ferriss: I won't let you leave me hanging.

00:38:59.140 --> 00:39:00.140
Go ahead.

00:39:00.140 --> 00:39:02.780
Justin Gary: Just to immerse the experience
of it.

00:39:02.780 --> 00:39:07.440
I was lucky in a sense in that I went to go
work at a company where I had a lot of friends

00:39:07.440 --> 00:39:11.500
that were already there, and I had immediate
deadlines.

00:39:11.500 --> 00:39:12.960
This game is going to release.

00:39:12.960 --> 00:39:17.170
You have to turn over the file with the cards
and the things within two months you have.

00:39:17.170 --> 00:39:19.740
And so there was no choice but to act.

00:39:19.740 --> 00:39:21.560
So what I say is that deadlines are magic.

00:39:21.560 --> 00:39:23.710
It doesn't matter if you have no idea what
you're doing, you have a deadline.

00:39:23.710 --> 00:39:27.870
Tim Ferriss: I was literally going to just
say the exact same thing verbatim, and it's

00:39:27.870 --> 00:39:30.400
like, what a beautiful constraint.

00:39:30.400 --> 00:39:32.430
It's just remarkable what you get done.

00:39:32.430 --> 00:39:36.450
Justin Gary: So that was, I think, a real
powerful tool about why.

00:39:36.450 --> 00:39:41.040
So one, really short deadlines, and my job
was on the line, so I had stakes, right?

00:39:41.040 --> 00:39:43.170
And I didn't want to go back to law school.

00:39:43.170 --> 00:39:48.240
And two, that I had other people that I could
ask and talk to who were smarter than I was,

00:39:48.240 --> 00:39:50.800
who had done it before, and I could get some
insight into.

00:39:50.800 --> 00:39:55.680
So being able to find either books or mentors
or other people that you can talk to has also

00:39:55.680 --> 00:39:56.859
helped accelerate it.

00:39:56.859 --> 00:40:00.329
So that eventually turned into this kind of
step-by-step process.

00:40:00.329 --> 00:40:03.980
So if you'd like, I can go into that now or
I can jump into other stuff.

00:40:03.980 --> 00:40:04.980
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:40:04.980 --> 00:40:11.040
Actually before we get into the process, could
you explain why a A Whack on the Side of the

00:40:11.040 --> 00:40:16.390
Head was impactful for you or what you took
from that that was valuable?

00:40:16.390 --> 00:40:21.680
Because in the course of doing homework for
this conversation, I noticed that you had

00:40:21.680 --> 00:40:25.210
mentioned this book before, and I actually
have a deck of cards designed by the same

00:40:25.210 --> 00:40:26.210
author.

00:40:26.210 --> 00:40:28.230
I have not read the book, however.

00:40:28.230 --> 00:40:32.510
Could you speak to why that had an impact
or why you recommend it?

00:40:32.510 --> 00:40:41.750
Justin Gary: So it's nice in that it helps
to, in a sense, demystify creativity and just

00:40:41.750 --> 00:40:45.750
bring it down to a very kind of simple, granular
level.

00:40:45.750 --> 00:40:46.750
And I'll give some examples.

00:40:46.750 --> 00:40:54.349
So one of the principles in it is that I've
used all the time is basically, and I forget

00:40:54.349 --> 00:40:58.390
how they word it in the book, but basically
turn the object around in your mind.

00:40:58.390 --> 00:41:01.760
If you take something that you normally think
of one way, how many different ways can you

00:41:01.760 --> 00:41:02.970
look at that same thing?

00:41:02.970 --> 00:41:05.460
So if you have a pen, right?

00:41:05.460 --> 00:41:07.280
You can say, all right, well, what do you
do with a pen?

00:41:07.280 --> 00:41:08.280
Well, you can write with it.

00:41:08.280 --> 00:41:09.280
Okay, well, no, all right.

00:41:09.280 --> 00:41:11.790
I could use it to help force me to smile by
biting it.

00:41:11.790 --> 00:41:13.490
I could use it to prop up my microphone.

00:41:13.490 --> 00:41:14.490
I could use it.

00:41:14.490 --> 00:41:18.480
You could, trying exercises that give you
different frames were very powerful and ways

00:41:18.480 --> 00:41:20.849
to break you out of your thinking.

00:41:20.849 --> 00:41:24.099
The idea of using random constraints.

00:41:24.099 --> 00:41:28.200
So one of the exercises I encourage anybody
to do if they want to is if you're having

00:41:28.200 --> 00:41:34.600
a problem, some creative challenge, some block,
go to a random book on your shelf, open it

00:41:34.600 --> 00:41:38.980
to a random page and point to a random set
word, some substantive word.

00:41:38.980 --> 00:41:43.260
Now, figure out how that word relates to whatever
your problem is, and journal on that for five

00:41:43.260 --> 00:41:44.300
minutes, 10 minutes.

00:41:44.300 --> 00:41:48.160
And you'll be amazed at how, all of a sudden,
it can crack open something because we get

00:41:48.160 --> 00:41:51.310
so linearly focused on the problem in front
of us.

00:41:51.310 --> 00:41:55.069
And even a completely random constraint can
open up the door for you.

00:41:55.069 --> 00:41:59.770
And we'll give one more to just move past
the, quote unquote, right answer.

00:41:59.770 --> 00:42:02.200
That you may have an answer and say, "Okay,
that's fine."

00:42:02.200 --> 00:42:03.200
And then you stop.

00:42:03.200 --> 00:42:04.310
Most people stop.

00:42:04.310 --> 00:42:06.940
Instead say, "Okay, I'm going to look for
the third right answer, the fourth right answer,

00:42:06.940 --> 00:42:07.940
the fifth," right?

00:42:07.940 --> 00:42:10.120
"I'm going to look beyond that to go deeper
and find more things."

00:42:10.120 --> 00:42:11.720
So just these little exercises.

00:42:11.720 --> 00:42:15.690
And you could just do those exercises would
suddenly say like, "Oh, wow, okay.

00:42:15.690 --> 00:42:17.609
I'm already seeing myself be more creative.

00:42:17.609 --> 00:42:19.740
I'm already seeing myself do more than I was."

00:42:19.740 --> 00:42:24.040
And so the book was impactful because it could
just, by the time I was done reading it, I

00:42:24.040 --> 00:42:27.600
had already seen myself do creative things
I didn't think I could do in ways that sound

00:42:27.600 --> 00:42:28.600
pretty stupid.

00:42:28.600 --> 00:42:31.610
I'm sure to me, saying it, pick a random page
of a book, be more creative, trust me, try

00:42:31.610 --> 00:42:32.610
it.

00:42:32.610 --> 00:42:33.610
It works.

00:42:33.610 --> 00:42:35.780
Tim Ferriss: Without having read that book,
I have used this approach.

00:42:35.780 --> 00:42:41.480
And actually writing teachers that I had long
ago, back when I had hair and was in school,

00:42:41.480 --> 00:42:44.970
also would offer these types of random constraints.

00:42:44.970 --> 00:42:47.890
It's almost like the itching approach to creativity,
right?

00:42:47.890 --> 00:42:53.420
It's like, okay, you just throw a bunch of
Scrabble tiles on the floor and you'd pick

00:42:53.420 --> 00:42:58.290
the three that are closest to you, and that's
your constraint, or that's the filter in a

00:42:58.290 --> 00:43:00.400
sense, or what directs the exercise.

00:43:00.400 --> 00:43:01.960
Very, very powerful.

00:43:01.960 --> 00:43:08.819
And I wanted to mention, since you were talking
about going past the right answer and finding

00:43:08.819 --> 00:43:15.849
the third right answer actually reminded me
of something a former guest said, Derek Sivers,

00:43:15.849 --> 00:43:17.809
who's one of my favorite people, he's been
on a few times.

00:43:17.809 --> 00:43:22.119
And when I asked him who he thought of when
he heard the word "successful," he said, "Well,

00:43:22.119 --> 00:43:29.300
my first answer might be, say, Richard Branson,
but that really ultimately depends on his

00:43:29.300 --> 00:43:30.300
goals in life.

00:43:30.300 --> 00:43:35.820
And if his goal was to have a quiet life undisturbed
by entrepreneurship, then that wouldn't be

00:43:35.820 --> 00:43:36.820
successful.

00:43:36.820 --> 00:43:39.500
So let me skip the first answer, get to the
second answer, get to the third answer."

00:43:39.500 --> 00:43:43.470
And he said, "What you should ask people is,
'Who is the third person who comes to mind

00:43:43.470 --> 00:43:44.840
when you hear the word successful?'"

00:43:44.840 --> 00:43:51.040
And it's really a powerful heuristic that
you can use in all sorts of ways.

00:43:51.040 --> 00:43:57.450
All right, so that's a snapshot on A Whack
on the Side of the Head.

00:43:57.450 --> 00:43:58.650
Let's talk about the core loop.

00:43:58.650 --> 00:43:59.650
Justin Gary: Yeah.

00:43:59.650 --> 00:44:02.040
Before we jump into the core design loop,
I'm going to give one more thing.

00:44:02.040 --> 00:44:05.530
It's not from A Whack on the Side of the Head,
but it's something I've actually developed

00:44:05.530 --> 00:44:08.819
more recently as a process that I find super
valuable.

00:44:08.819 --> 00:44:13.590
And one of the things that we try to do in
our company is always surface our assumptions.

00:44:13.590 --> 00:44:17.030
There's a lot of things where you're assuming
a game is going to be fun because of this.

00:44:17.030 --> 00:44:18.490
We assume people are looking for that.

00:44:18.490 --> 00:44:21.849
We assume we're making this category of game,
et cetera.

00:44:21.849 --> 00:44:26.790
And a really great exercise to do that's in
this spirit is once a quarter, we'll do an

00:44:26.790 --> 00:44:29.430
assumptions challenging exercise.

00:44:29.430 --> 00:44:32.619
And what that means is you surface every assumption
you can.

00:44:32.619 --> 00:44:35.300
So if you're on a, you can do this by yourself,
or if you're on a team, everybody writes down

00:44:35.300 --> 00:44:39.190
on their own all their assumptions, you put
them all up on a shared board or a shared

00:44:39.190 --> 00:44:41.540
Google Doc if you're doing remote like we
are.

00:44:41.540 --> 00:44:44.440
And then you, one by one, "Okay, what if that
weren't true?"

00:44:44.440 --> 00:44:47.400
Or to use your terminology, "What if I did
the opposite?"

00:44:47.400 --> 00:44:51.030
And it's so powerful because sometimes it
doesn't mean anything, right?

00:44:51.030 --> 00:44:52.470
Sometimes it's like, okay, that doesn't make
any sense.

00:44:52.470 --> 00:44:53.470
You move on.

00:44:53.470 --> 00:44:57.780
But without fail, by the time we finish that
exercise, we find some core assumption that

00:44:57.780 --> 00:44:59.819
all of a sudden changes everything.

00:44:59.819 --> 00:45:04.150
And that ability to just take what you assume
to be true, one, making it explicit in the

00:45:04.150 --> 00:45:07.710
first place is super powerful, just so you
know really what you're doing.

00:45:07.710 --> 00:45:12.119
And then two is this inversion process is
incredibly powerful.

00:45:12.119 --> 00:45:13.119
So, yeah.

00:45:13.119 --> 00:45:17.180
Tim Ferriss: Could you give any examples just
to make it a little concrete in the minds

00:45:17.180 --> 00:45:18.180
—
Justin Gary: Yeah, absolutely.

00:45:18.180 --> 00:45:20.050
Tim Ferriss: — of folks listening of what
those assumptions might be?

00:45:20.050 --> 00:45:26.050
Justin Gary: I made this game called SolForge
with Richard Garfield, the guy that created

00:45:26.050 --> 00:45:27.050
Magic.

00:45:27.050 --> 00:45:30.890
And we did this over a decade ago, and we
made it as a digital trading card game.

00:45:30.890 --> 00:45:34.570
And so purely you play it on your phone, no
physical objects at all.

00:45:34.570 --> 00:45:35.810
And the game was successful.

00:45:35.810 --> 00:45:40.119
We ran it for a few years, and eventually
we took it down and we were talking about

00:45:40.119 --> 00:45:43.440
we wanted to bring it back because we loved
the game, we wanted to bring it back.

00:45:43.440 --> 00:45:47.700
And one of the exercises we did, okay, SolForge
is a digital game.

00:45:47.700 --> 00:45:49.510
And then we're like, wait, what if it's not?

00:45:49.510 --> 00:45:50.780
Like, what would that look like?

00:45:50.780 --> 00:45:53.540
And we had built it to be a digital game,
but then all of a sudden we're like, well,

00:45:53.540 --> 00:45:56.150
now it's seven years later.

00:45:56.150 --> 00:45:58.630
Digital printing technology is way better
than it was.

00:45:58.630 --> 00:46:02.800
You can actually algorithmically create one-of-a-kind
cards and create things.

00:46:02.800 --> 00:46:05.470
And that started us down this road of investigating
this new technology.

00:46:05.470 --> 00:46:09.750
And so the new version of SolForge, called
SolForge Fusion, is now a physical card game

00:46:09.750 --> 00:46:13.280
that has this new technology built into it,
which never would've happened if we had just

00:46:13.280 --> 00:46:16.510
gone with our assumptions of, oh, okay, we're
going to remake the same game again.

00:46:16.510 --> 00:46:19.170
So it's one example that was recently impactful
for me in this.

00:46:19.170 --> 00:46:21.040
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, I love that.

00:46:21.040 --> 00:46:26.230
I have, well, I can't tip my tip hand too
hard here publicly, but I've been very much

00:46:26.230 --> 00:46:30.790
focused on possibly working on my first book
in five or six years.

00:46:30.790 --> 00:46:32.340
It's been a long time.

00:46:32.340 --> 00:46:35.359
I wasn't sure I would do another one, but
I've been very strongly considering it.

00:46:35.359 --> 00:46:43.190
And I've been not in written form, which would
be more helpful, I think, but in a sort of

00:46:43.190 --> 00:46:54.790
meandering mental form, which is sort of a
mix of reverie, skepticism, and daydreaming,

00:46:54.790 --> 00:46:57.100
looking at some of the basic assumptions.

00:46:57.100 --> 00:46:58.210
Is it a book?

00:46:58.210 --> 00:47:02.390
I think of it as a book, but does it even
need to be a book, period, or I should say

00:47:02.390 --> 00:47:03.390
question mark?

00:47:03.390 --> 00:47:09.970
And if it is a book, for instance, generally
if you're going through the publishing process,

00:47:09.970 --> 00:47:15.640
or you have been through the publishing process,
your thinking has been shaped in a certain

00:47:15.640 --> 00:47:17.099
way over and over again.

00:47:17.099 --> 00:47:23.579
You're like, okay, I have to do print, audio
ebook, and you sell all of those to one person.

00:47:23.579 --> 00:47:26.600
Well, what if we split those up?

00:47:26.600 --> 00:47:28.930
What if there was no print whatsoever?

00:47:28.930 --> 00:47:30.660
What would that look like?

00:47:30.660 --> 00:47:34.690
And just testing these very basic assumptions.

00:47:34.690 --> 00:47:40.099
And I'll give you one example also, which
people might not realize, or if you're outside

00:47:40.099 --> 00:47:42.599
of publishing, why would you realize this?

00:47:42.599 --> 00:47:46.900
But none of my books in the US have ever gone
to paperback.

00:47:46.900 --> 00:47:51.490
And if you were to ask most authors, "Why
have you gone to paperback?"

00:47:51.490 --> 00:47:55.860
The answer would generally be something along
the lines of, "Well, the publishers always

00:47:55.860 --> 00:47:56.860
do that.

00:47:56.860 --> 00:48:02.750
A year and a half after the hardcover, they
lower the price to make the market larger

00:48:02.750 --> 00:48:04.340
for the book.

00:48:04.340 --> 00:48:05.530
And you come out in paperback."

00:48:05.530 --> 00:48:10.700
And I was like, "But if you look at the math,
does that make any sense for you?"

00:48:10.700 --> 00:48:13.710
And then they think about it and they're like,
"Well, let me think about that.

00:48:13.710 --> 00:48:14.710
Okay.

00:48:14.710 --> 00:48:18.380
I was making 15 percent on each hardcover
book, which is already discounted on Amazon

00:48:18.380 --> 00:48:21.089
to something very affordable.

00:48:21.089 --> 00:48:25.780
And now when I go to paperback, wait a second,
my royalties just got cut to seven and a half

00:48:25.780 --> 00:48:26.780
or six and a half.

00:48:26.780 --> 00:48:30.890
Now I have to sell twice as many books to
make the same amount in royalties.

00:48:30.890 --> 00:48:33.510
Oh, shit, that makes no sense whatsoever."

00:48:33.510 --> 00:48:39.240
It's also not really relevant in a world where
ebook is an option, if that makes any sense.

00:48:39.240 --> 00:48:42.240
If somebody is looking for a lower price,
they can buy it on ebook.

00:48:42.240 --> 00:48:49.150
So hence from the beginning, none of my books
have ever gone to paperback.

00:48:49.150 --> 00:48:54.829
So if you're open to it, we can certainly,
I mean love, as you know, you know this very

00:48:54.829 --> 00:49:00.790
well, I love digging into testing assumptions
because those are the rules that, that's like

00:49:00.790 --> 00:49:04.000
the operating — your assumptions of the
operating system that governs your life in

00:49:04.000 --> 00:49:05.000
a way.

00:49:05.000 --> 00:49:06.000
Justin Gary: That's right.

00:49:06.000 --> 00:49:11.210
Tim Ferriss: And you want to make sure want
to, those rules can be justified on some level,

00:49:11.210 --> 00:49:16.110
but maybe it makes sense to hop into the core
design loop and talk about that.

00:49:16.110 --> 00:49:17.110
Justin Gary: Yeah, it's perfect.

00:49:17.110 --> 00:49:20.349
I think it's a perfect segue because exactly
what we're talking about with assumptions

00:49:20.349 --> 00:49:22.150
is going to come into play in the core design
loop.

00:49:22.150 --> 00:49:27.070
So the core design loop is how games are made,
it's how creative projects are done, but we're

00:49:27.070 --> 00:49:28.500
going to focus on games.

00:49:28.500 --> 00:49:31.569
That's the kind of main topic I teach at it.

00:49:31.569 --> 00:49:34.140
So the core design loop is six steps.

00:49:34.140 --> 00:49:41.420
It's inspiring, framing, brainstorming, prototyping,
testing, and iterating.

00:49:41.420 --> 00:49:43.770
So step one is inspiring.

00:49:43.770 --> 00:49:45.790
What is it that's driving you, right?

00:49:45.790 --> 00:49:47.940
What's the core of what you're excited about?

00:49:47.940 --> 00:49:51.820
So it could be you want to make a game for
this IP, this world that you love.

00:49:51.820 --> 00:49:53.230
Could be you want to make a CØCKPUNCH game.

00:49:53.230 --> 00:49:54.600
Could be want to make a game about fantasy
warriors, right?

00:49:54.600 --> 00:49:56.049
Hypothetically speaking, it could be that
you want to —

00:49:56.049 --> 00:49:58.410
Tim Ferriss: Or miniatures if you love miniatures.

00:49:58.410 --> 00:49:59.510
Justin Gary: Exactly, yeah, you love miniatures.

00:49:59.510 --> 00:50:00.510
You know you want to make a miniatures game.

00:50:00.510 --> 00:50:04.230
It could be a component, could be a theme,
could be a mechanic, could be you want to

00:50:04.230 --> 00:50:07.020
make a game about dog walking, whatever it
is, you're excited, what is at the heart of

00:50:07.020 --> 00:50:08.559
what you're doing.

00:50:08.559 --> 00:50:13.109
And in general, the heart is going to be an
experience you want to create for your audience,

00:50:13.109 --> 00:50:16.869
but you start at something, whatever high-level
thing gets you excited.

00:50:16.869 --> 00:50:18.790
Then framing is a lot what we talked about.

00:50:18.790 --> 00:50:19.980
We want to put a box around it.

00:50:19.980 --> 00:50:22.140
We want to put constraints around it.

00:50:22.140 --> 00:50:25.920
And so that constraint should always include
a deadline, ideally a short deadline to whatever

00:50:25.920 --> 00:50:27.520
your next step is.

00:50:27.520 --> 00:50:30.190
So I'm going to have a prototype to test in
two weeks.

00:50:30.190 --> 00:50:32.910
It could be —
Look, one of the most common mistakes I see

00:50:32.910 --> 00:50:37.690
game designers make, they come to me, "Okay,
I want to make a game that's Halo and World

00:50:37.690 --> 00:50:41.910
of Warcraft and Grand Theft Auto, but all
rolled into one with a bunch of cool stuff

00:50:41.910 --> 00:50:42.910
happening."

00:50:42.910 --> 00:50:46.540
And these are massive games that take millions
and millions of dollars and teams of people,

00:50:46.540 --> 00:50:47.880
and it's like, well, you've never made a game
before.

00:50:47.880 --> 00:50:50.650
Maybe let's start with a simple card game.

00:50:50.650 --> 00:50:54.859
So constraints with your components, constraints
with time.

00:50:54.859 --> 00:50:57.290
What's the space I'm playing in?

00:50:57.290 --> 00:50:59.070
Step three is brainstorming.

00:50:59.070 --> 00:51:01.970
And brainstorming is where you ideate and
come up with your ideas.

00:51:01.970 --> 00:51:05.230
And I'm very particular about how I recommend
people brainstorm.

00:51:05.230 --> 00:51:08.619
And this comes from research from the Wharton
School of Business and work that I've done

00:51:08.619 --> 00:51:09.619
with them.

00:51:09.619 --> 00:51:11.359
But the basics are three phases.

00:51:11.359 --> 00:51:15.859
And to get really granular, because I know
that your audience likes that, I break it

00:51:15.859 --> 00:51:18.670
into three steps, 20 minutes each.

00:51:18.670 --> 00:51:25.460
First is where you are open exploring, you
write as many things as you can, as many ideas

00:51:25.460 --> 00:51:28.520
as you can, and don't stop.

00:51:28.520 --> 00:51:31.130
There's always this critic part of our brain
that's like, "No, that's stupid.

00:51:31.130 --> 00:51:32.130
No, that won't work."

00:51:32.130 --> 00:51:33.130
Turn that off.

00:51:33.130 --> 00:51:34.640
Just write down as many things as you can.

00:51:34.640 --> 00:51:38.230
If your pen stops moving for more than 10
seconds, you're doing it wrong.

00:51:38.230 --> 00:51:40.250
As many ideas as you can on the paper.

00:51:40.250 --> 00:51:46.010
Then the second stage, also recommend 20 minutes,
is organizing where you take this massive,

00:51:46.010 --> 00:51:50.470
ridiculous, some cool ideas, some ridiculous
ideas, and you start to try to find patterns

00:51:50.470 --> 00:51:51.470
between them.

00:51:51.470 --> 00:51:55.319
And if you're trying to make something specific,
you start to think through, okay, if I'm trying

00:51:55.319 --> 00:51:57.690
to make a game, what's the victory condition?

00:51:57.690 --> 00:52:00.770
How do people figure out what are their moves?

00:52:00.770 --> 00:52:01.940
How do they interact?

00:52:01.940 --> 00:52:04.320
You start trying to put some structure around
it.

00:52:04.320 --> 00:52:06.340
Or if it's a book, okay, what's the book?

00:52:06.340 --> 00:52:07.520
What's the opening of the book?

00:52:07.520 --> 00:52:08.960
How are people — how's it going to help
people?

00:52:08.960 --> 00:52:09.960
Who's my audience?

00:52:09.960 --> 00:52:10.960
Whatever it is, right?

00:52:10.960 --> 00:52:15.300
And then as you start making these connections,
you're naturally going to see gaps and you're

00:52:15.300 --> 00:52:16.940
going to fill those gaps in.

00:52:16.940 --> 00:52:21.760
And then the last step, which is also 20 minutes,
is elimination.

00:52:21.760 --> 00:52:25.480
And that's where you went from starting with
as many ideas as you can.

00:52:25.480 --> 00:52:29.809
And now you want to get to as few ideas as
possible so that you can start moving to testing

00:52:29.809 --> 00:52:30.950
them.

00:52:30.950 --> 00:52:34.770
And so that's where you go and you'll try
to like, okay, what's the minimum amount that

00:52:34.770 --> 00:52:35.840
I can prototype?

00:52:35.840 --> 00:52:39.980
Which is the next step to test my idea, to
test my assumption, to test the core of what

00:52:39.980 --> 00:52:40.980
I'm doing?

00:52:40.980 --> 00:52:44.569
And so prototyping is exactly what it sounds
like.

00:52:44.569 --> 00:52:46.589
Talk about an MVP or this minimum viable product.

00:52:46.589 --> 00:52:49.780
What's the smallest thing I can do to test
my idea?

00:52:49.780 --> 00:52:51.950
What's the easiest way I can do this?

00:52:51.950 --> 00:52:56.220
And then step five is testing where you actually
show it to somebody, you get some feedback,

00:52:56.220 --> 00:52:58.550
you actually figure out what's going on there.

00:52:58.550 --> 00:53:01.960
And then step six is iterating where you take
what you've learned and then you cycle back

00:53:01.960 --> 00:53:03.520
through the process.

00:53:03.520 --> 00:53:07.150
And what I've learned is that the skill of
creativity, specifically with game design,

00:53:07.150 --> 00:53:11.660
but really with anything is how well you can
kind of flow through that process and get

00:53:11.660 --> 00:53:13.990
the most out of each loop and go through as
many loops as possible.

00:53:13.990 --> 00:53:17.140
Tim Ferriss: All right, we're going to come
back to brainstorming in a second, but what

00:53:17.140 --> 00:53:25.380
I want to do for people listening is highlight,
and you said this already, but I want to just

00:53:25.380 --> 00:53:28.809
make a few notes and give a few examples.

00:53:28.809 --> 00:53:32.230
This process can apply to almost anything.

00:53:32.230 --> 00:53:39.569
And for instance, you want to start a podcast,
I'll use that as an example, right?

00:53:39.569 --> 00:53:41.859
Inspiration thematically, where are you going?

00:53:41.859 --> 00:53:43.609
What is the format that you're excited about?

00:53:43.609 --> 00:53:48.412
What is driving you to be considering starting
a podcast, right?

00:53:48.412 --> 00:53:51.260
Setting parameters, thinking about the constraints.

00:53:51.260 --> 00:53:52.650
What are the constraints?

00:53:52.650 --> 00:53:56.720
How do you keep it interesting enough that
you continue to do it, but simple enough that

00:53:56.720 --> 00:53:58.420
you don't quit after three episodes?

00:53:58.420 --> 00:54:03.570
And the example you gave of, "I want it to
be like Halo plus World of Warcraft plus Warhammer,"

00:54:03.570 --> 00:54:06.130
and you're like, "Whoa, whoa, whoa, whoa,
whoa, easy."

00:54:06.130 --> 00:54:11.230
The reason there is an elephant graveyard
of a million dead podcasts that stopped after

00:54:11.230 --> 00:54:15.099
episode three is they're like, "I love This
American Life and I think it's going to be

00:54:15.099 --> 00:54:18.040
like This American Life meets Serial meets
Joe Rogan."

00:54:18.040 --> 00:54:21.180
And I'm like, "Whoa, slow down Tex.

00:54:21.180 --> 00:54:24.299
There's a reason the credits are five minutes
long at the end of This American Life.

00:54:24.299 --> 00:54:29.700
Yeah, I'm not sure you realize how much goes
into making that and then brainstorming,"

00:54:29.700 --> 00:54:30.700
right?

00:54:30.700 --> 00:54:35.119
Coming up with whether it's guests, ideas
for questions, et cetera.

00:54:35.119 --> 00:54:36.119
Prototyping, right?

00:54:36.119 --> 00:54:37.950
Giving it a go.

00:54:37.950 --> 00:54:42.700
Maybe you record as I did five or six episodes
before publishing your first so that you have

00:54:42.700 --> 00:54:47.339
a chance to kick the tires and try to figure
out what's working, the testing you're going

00:54:47.339 --> 00:54:53.160
to get in this case, testing with your guests,
but also with test listeners, let's just say.

00:54:53.160 --> 00:54:54.170
And then iterating.

00:54:54.170 --> 00:54:55.700
This also applies to books.

00:54:55.700 --> 00:55:01.069
This also applies to really any creative project
that I can think about.

00:55:01.069 --> 00:55:06.540
And to come back to the brainstorming for
a moment, when you are brainstorming, what

00:55:06.540 --> 00:55:14.180
is your personal preferred method of doing
this whiteboard versus pen and paper versus

00:55:14.180 --> 00:55:18.340
typing on a keyboard versus a tablet of some
type?

00:55:18.340 --> 00:55:19.380
How would you like to do it?

00:55:19.380 --> 00:55:25.110
Justin Gary: Yeah, so I am a huge fan of,
in particular, an app called Workflowy.

00:55:25.110 --> 00:55:31.300
And it's a totally free app, and it's basically
a series of nested lists.

00:55:31.300 --> 00:55:33.450
And the nice thing about it is it's infinite.

00:55:33.450 --> 00:55:37.260
So I can continue to make list and sublists
and sublists and sublists and I can link pieces

00:55:37.260 --> 00:55:38.670
to pieces.

00:55:38.670 --> 00:55:42.480
And so I find over the years now, I've been
using that for seven or eight years, that

00:55:42.480 --> 00:55:45.599
is my personal favorite tool, but I have used
— the important advice for people is not

00:55:45.599 --> 00:55:50.460
to copy the tool, but whatever works for you,
whatever's easiest, flow, the least resistance.

00:55:50.460 --> 00:55:55.420
So a lot of people like Notion, it's a similar
type of tool, but it's bulkier and a little

00:55:55.420 --> 00:55:56.930
bit slower, in my experience.

00:55:56.930 --> 00:55:58.809
And that speed is everything.

00:55:58.809 --> 00:56:00.869
I don't want anything that's going to slow
me down.

00:56:00.869 --> 00:56:04.640
So that's where pen and paper's great, but
I don't like, unless I have a giant sheet

00:56:04.640 --> 00:56:06.140
of paper, which I do enjoy.

00:56:06.140 --> 00:56:10.450
If it's a big, big poster board and you have
markers and bigger things, especially if I'm

00:56:10.450 --> 00:56:11.450
working with a team.

00:56:11.450 --> 00:56:16.250
So either a giant whiteboard or a large poster
board on the table because you can all share

00:56:16.250 --> 00:56:19.619
the same space and see ideas form, that I
think is great.

00:56:19.619 --> 00:56:25.780
But a small sheet of paper for me, my brain,
when it sees a page start to get full, my

00:56:25.780 --> 00:56:28.710
brain just subconsciously thinks, "Okay, well
you don't need any more ideas.

00:56:28.710 --> 00:56:29.710
Your page is full.

00:56:29.710 --> 00:56:30.710
You're good."

00:56:30.710 --> 00:56:31.710
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:56:31.710 --> 00:56:32.710
It's like the goldfish in the fishbowl, right?

00:56:32.710 --> 00:56:34.450
It's like, "All right, I'm not going to grow
anymore.

00:56:34.450 --> 00:56:36.750
This space is getting a little constrained."

00:56:36.750 --> 00:56:37.750
Justin Gary: Yeah.

00:56:37.750 --> 00:56:41.359
So I like Workflowy because it's got that
infinite kind of growth and I can shrink or

00:56:41.359 --> 00:56:46.520
grow nodes or I want to deep dive deeper into
this idea and link ideas together.

00:56:46.520 --> 00:56:47.609
That's been my favorite.

00:56:47.609 --> 00:56:53.250
Tim Ferriss: And what are some of the ways
that you like to prototype?

00:56:53.250 --> 00:57:01.400
I'd love to know where you think people most
often get stuck — in between or after, which

00:57:01.400 --> 00:57:02.980
step do people get stuck?

00:57:02.980 --> 00:57:06.250
And as someone who's been, as you know, because
we've had conversations about this and you've

00:57:06.250 --> 00:57:11.740
been incredibly helpful, thinking about prototyping
and games and design.

00:57:11.740 --> 00:57:15.750
I've been very excited about this for a while
now.

00:57:15.750 --> 00:57:22.250
I tended to get stuck at the prototyping phase
because, well, it's also my first sort of

00:57:22.250 --> 00:57:24.570
rodeo with this.

00:57:24.570 --> 00:57:30.030
But the inclination is to try to make something
that's really good and kind of polished.

00:57:30.030 --> 00:57:35.859
I know I'm not trying to make a finished product,
but the more I have studied what you've done,

00:57:35.859 --> 00:57:43.849
listened to the podcast, talk to people like
Elan Lee or others, the more I appreciate

00:57:43.849 --> 00:57:47.910
how quick and dirty it is.

00:57:47.910 --> 00:57:57.039
And also just as a reference, I think I have
a blog post about Stephen Key, who is an inventor,

00:57:57.039 --> 00:58:03.750
product developer who's based in California,
at least he was at the time, who has done

00:58:03.750 --> 00:58:07.740
extremely well developing toys for major companies.

00:58:07.740 --> 00:58:12.480
And the toys are ultimately some finished
product that look great, but he will use construction

00:58:12.480 --> 00:58:17.020
paper and newspaper to prototype.

00:58:17.020 --> 00:58:21.650
And based on that and a description and maybe
a video of how it functions, he successfully

00:58:21.650 --> 00:58:24.329
licensed products all the time to huge companies.

00:58:24.329 --> 00:58:27.210
So could you speak to maybe the prototyping
phase?

00:58:27.210 --> 00:58:28.290
Justin Gary: Yes.

00:58:28.290 --> 00:58:32.490
And you've already hit on the core of this,
but I'll just leave it, keep it simple, stupid.

00:58:32.490 --> 00:58:33.960
The KISS principle.

00:58:33.960 --> 00:58:40.380
It is the tendency to overdevelop and make
your prototype super nice or not feel like

00:58:40.380 --> 00:58:44.560
you can show it to somebody or test it without
it being super nice is the graveyard of the

00:58:44.560 --> 00:58:48.090
most games probably of anything on the planet.

00:58:48.090 --> 00:58:50.819
And the most ideas, frankly, in general.

00:58:50.819 --> 00:58:53.079
So let's make this very concrete.

00:58:53.079 --> 00:58:57.119
Probably one of the games I'm most well known
for is a deck-building game called Ascension.

00:58:57.119 --> 00:58:59.200
Been around for 13 years.

00:58:59.200 --> 00:59:00.440
We've got lots.

00:59:00.440 --> 00:59:02.390
So I first prototyped that game.

00:59:02.390 --> 00:59:05.079
There was another deck-building game, and
a deck-building game, for those that don't

00:59:05.079 --> 00:59:08.790
know, it's unlike Magic, which is a trading
card game where you're buying packs of cards

00:59:08.790 --> 00:59:10.039
and trying to build a deck.

00:59:10.039 --> 00:59:14.020
In a deck-building game, you have a fixed
box of cards, you buy it like a box like you

00:59:14.020 --> 00:59:18.090
would Monopoly, but during the game, you're
acquiring cards for your deck.

00:59:18.090 --> 00:59:21.599
So it's like you get to play the game of building
a deck, deck-building game.

00:59:21.599 --> 00:59:24.829
So there was another deck-building game called
Dominion, which was released.

00:59:24.829 --> 00:59:26.510
It was the first of its category.

00:59:26.510 --> 00:59:28.309
And I fell in love with that game.

00:59:28.309 --> 00:59:32.700
I started playing it a bunch, but then as
I played it like 100 times, I was like, "There's

00:59:32.700 --> 00:59:35.299
really some things I would like to see differently.

00:59:35.299 --> 00:59:38.780
The cards are too static, there's too many
rules and resources.

00:59:38.780 --> 00:59:40.030
I wish I could change it."

00:59:40.030 --> 00:59:45.460
And so my first prototype of Ascension was
literally just instead of having Dominion

00:59:45.460 --> 00:59:48.700
cards, which are all kind of laid out at the
beginning of the game, takes 20 minutes to

00:59:48.700 --> 00:59:52.319
set up, I just shuffled them all together
and dealt them out and said, "What would a

00:59:52.319 --> 00:59:53.319
game look like if it was randomized?"

00:59:53.319 --> 00:59:58.890
And I played that way, and the game was not
good, but it gave me enough of a proof of

00:59:58.890 --> 01:00:04.490
concept that like, "Oh, there's something
fun here and if I spend the time to do this,

01:00:04.490 --> 01:00:05.660
I can now move forward."

01:00:05.660 --> 01:00:10.070
Tim Ferriss: And just for clarity, you were
using the Dominion cards, but changing the

01:00:10.070 --> 01:00:11.920
rules to see how it impacted gameplay?

01:00:11.920 --> 01:00:12.920
Justin Gary: Exactly right.

01:00:12.920 --> 01:00:17.660
And so if you can prototype using something
like a deck of cards around the house or take

01:00:17.660 --> 01:00:21.660
a Monopoly set, I know I keep using Monopoly
as an example, whatever, most people know

01:00:21.660 --> 01:00:22.660
it.

01:00:22.660 --> 01:00:26.650
So say, "Oh, hey, what would this look like
if instead of going around Go, you actually

01:00:26.650 --> 01:00:29.990
had dinosaurs that would try to eat each other
as you're moving around the board, what would

01:00:29.990 --> 01:00:30.990
that be like?"

01:00:30.990 --> 01:00:32.190
I'm like, "Okay, that sounds kind of fun.

01:00:32.190 --> 01:00:33.190
Let's see what happens."

01:00:33.190 --> 01:00:34.720
And so it's literally that simple.

01:00:34.720 --> 01:00:38.160
So for my prototyping tools, my first round
of prototyping is just, I have a bunch of

01:00:38.160 --> 01:00:39.160
stuff.

01:00:39.160 --> 01:00:42.079
I have a bunch of dice and my D&amp;D dice with
all the random signs.

01:00:42.079 --> 01:00:44.740
I've got decks of cards, I've got random pen
and paper.

01:00:44.740 --> 01:00:45.860
I will draw things.

01:00:45.860 --> 01:00:48.410
In fact, when you and I worked together a
little bit, I showed you some things.

01:00:48.410 --> 01:00:52.119
I showed you what my early sketch prototypes
look like, and they are ugly.

01:00:52.119 --> 01:00:55.059
This is kind of, I talk about the phases of
design.

01:00:55.059 --> 01:00:58.530
And so every time you go through the core
design loop, your questions are go from bigger

01:00:58.530 --> 01:01:00.040
to smaller and smaller.

01:01:00.040 --> 01:01:02.820
And as you go through it, you're going to
get more and more, like you're going to care

01:01:02.820 --> 01:01:03.820
about polish more.

01:01:03.820 --> 01:01:07.260
So if you're just trying to figure out where
is the fun, what is the idea, what's the theme,

01:01:07.260 --> 01:01:10.319
what am I doing here?

01:01:10.319 --> 01:01:14.800
It's like, imagine you're building a house
and before you've even laid the foundation,

01:01:14.800 --> 01:01:18.130
you're worrying about the paint color on the
walls and where the furniture goes.

01:01:18.130 --> 01:01:19.630
You're not going to get very far.

01:01:19.630 --> 01:01:20.730
You're wasting a lot of time.

01:01:20.730 --> 01:01:23.140
Make sure you've got a solid foundation first.

01:01:23.140 --> 01:01:24.590
Worry about the paint color later.

01:01:24.590 --> 01:01:27.910
And so I think a lot of designers start by
worrying about the paint color being wrong

01:01:27.910 --> 01:01:30.490
and they're never going to finish a house
that way.

01:01:30.490 --> 01:01:35.630
Tim Ferriss: Okay, so I'm going to stand in,
I mean, this is a lazy sort of rhetorical

01:01:35.630 --> 01:01:36.849
trick here.

01:01:36.849 --> 01:01:43.059
So I'm going to defend the newbies, including
myself for a second, and ask a question, which

01:01:43.059 --> 01:01:50.950
I think is actually really important, at the
very least helpful in all sorts of disciplines,

01:01:50.950 --> 01:01:53.220
not just game design.

01:01:53.220 --> 01:01:59.280
One of the reasons a lot of writers get stuck
or aspiring writers is that they kick out

01:01:59.280 --> 01:02:00.940
a first draft.

01:02:00.940 --> 01:02:06.750
It's rough, it's ugly, it's really, really
rough on all the edges.

01:02:06.750 --> 01:02:14.220
And they compare their rough draft to the
final draft of incredible writers.

01:02:14.220 --> 01:02:17.330
And the rough draft of those writers is invisible.

01:02:17.330 --> 01:02:20.000
They never see that rough draft.

01:02:20.000 --> 01:02:26.569
So what has been helpful to me, for instance,
when thinking about potentially writing comics

01:02:26.569 --> 01:02:32.670
is seeing the original scripts and how they
were tweaked and how the concept art was modified

01:02:32.670 --> 01:02:36.980
over time to ultimately land on what we see
in the finished product.

01:02:36.980 --> 01:02:40.500
Because then I can see the ugly babies in
the beginning.

01:02:40.500 --> 01:02:41.730
I'm like, "Oh, yeah.

01:02:41.730 --> 01:02:42.730
Okay."

01:02:42.730 --> 01:02:48.660
That's very reassuring and it gives me more
confidence to work with my ugly baby to try

01:02:48.660 --> 01:02:53.010
to shape it into something that ultimately
is good, because if I'm comparing everyone's

01:02:53.010 --> 01:02:56.370
finished product to my rough product, it can
be really demoralizing.

01:02:56.370 --> 01:03:05.809
So the question is there a way for folks to
see the rough drafts of games, the prototyping

01:03:05.809 --> 01:03:10.109
and so on, if they wanted to explore this
as an example?

01:03:10.109 --> 01:03:16.860
Justin Gary: It's one of the reasons why I
started my podcast is to sort of unlock a

01:03:16.860 --> 01:03:20.470
lot of that stuff for other designers, talk
about their origin stories, talk about where

01:03:20.470 --> 01:03:22.619
they're not — the things that inevitably
fail.

01:03:22.619 --> 01:03:30.359
You can also go, there are a variety of places
where people will have prototype testing.

01:03:30.359 --> 01:03:33.410
Let's say you have a favorite game company
or game designers, we do this at Stone Blade,

01:03:33.410 --> 01:03:34.410
at my company.

01:03:34.410 --> 01:03:39.839
You can join our Discord, and then we will
periodically put playtest requests out so

01:03:39.839 --> 01:03:42.769
that people can — because we want to get
feedback, we want to get feedback from people,

01:03:42.769 --> 01:03:44.440
and they could see how terrible those games
are.

01:03:44.440 --> 01:03:48.089
Now, we've already gone through a few iteration
loops before we put it out into the public.

01:03:48.089 --> 01:03:51.680
So to see the most raw forms, I think there's
not enough of it out there.

01:03:51.680 --> 01:03:55.200
I think it's something I really want to encourage
more of because it's really helpful.

01:03:55.200 --> 01:03:59.970
I've been doing this for 20 years and my first
prototypes are hideous.

01:03:59.970 --> 01:04:01.500
So if you want, I can share some.

01:04:01.500 --> 01:04:02.510
I'll put some up on the thinklikeagamedesigner.com/tim.

01:04:02.510 --> 01:04:04.000
Tim Ferriss: Or the show notes.

01:04:04.000 --> 01:04:05.550
Justin Gary: Or the show notes.

01:04:05.550 --> 01:04:06.550
Yeah, whatever.

01:04:06.550 --> 01:04:07.550
Tim Ferriss: That's great.

01:04:07.550 --> 01:04:08.990
Justin Gary: Because I want to demystify that
process.

01:04:08.990 --> 01:04:09.990
It is okay.

01:04:09.990 --> 01:04:11.150
In fact, it's better.

01:04:11.150 --> 01:04:16.780
In fact, I'll go even further because not
only is it faster and because you don't get

01:04:16.780 --> 01:04:21.690
hung up on making it really pretty, it's faster
to prototype, it's actually better to change.

01:04:21.690 --> 01:04:23.260
It's easy because you need to iterate.

01:04:23.260 --> 01:04:27.380
So if you've put a lot of time into making
a beautiful board and beautiful cards and

01:04:27.380 --> 01:04:30.460
then you realize you've got to change a card,
you're going to feel weird about it.

01:04:30.460 --> 01:04:32.480
Whereas if I have just some ugly cards —
Tim Ferriss: Your sunk cost fallacy is going

01:04:32.480 --> 01:04:34.270
to come to latch onto your back.

01:04:34.270 --> 01:04:37.100
Justin Gary: I have a card that I've messed
up, I'm just going to cross out something

01:04:37.100 --> 01:04:39.059
on it with a marker and write something else.

01:04:39.059 --> 01:04:41.720
And then, okay, now it's this, go, and I'll
make a little note.

01:04:41.720 --> 01:04:44.650
The resistance to change is so much lower.

01:04:44.650 --> 01:04:50.349
So it's actually net negative for you to invest
in making something pretty early on in the

01:04:50.349 --> 01:04:51.349
process.

01:04:51.349 --> 01:04:52.980
Tim Ferriss: Makes perfect sense.

01:04:52.980 --> 01:04:58.970
So you've mentioned your company a couple
times, but last time we left young Justin

01:04:58.970 --> 01:05:04.299
in our chronology, he was working at a game
design company.

01:05:04.299 --> 01:05:10.180
So could you fill in the gaps for us and tell
us how you went from working for somebody

01:05:10.180 --> 01:05:12.200
else to working for yourself?

01:05:12.200 --> 01:05:16.430
Justin Gary: Yeah, this is a fun story and
you actually come into this story Tim, so

01:05:16.430 --> 01:05:18.349
I'll share it.

01:05:18.349 --> 01:05:24.880
So I moved from working on pure card games
to, I had an opportunity to lead a project

01:05:24.880 --> 01:05:27.010
that was the World of Warcraft miniatures
game.

01:05:27.010 --> 01:05:29.849
And this is a very exciting project for me.

01:05:29.849 --> 01:05:33.089
I get to build something that's super cool
with an IP everybody knows.

01:05:33.089 --> 01:05:41.359
And I had worked on it for about 18 months
and we had, the company that I worked for

01:05:41.359 --> 01:05:42.359
was called Upper Deck.

01:05:42.359 --> 01:05:45.410
They had made trading cards forever, most
known for baseball cards.

01:05:45.410 --> 01:05:48.440
And they made Yu-Gi-Oh! and this Marvel game
I'd been working on, but they didn't know

01:05:48.440 --> 01:05:50.030
how to make miniatures games.

01:05:50.030 --> 01:05:54.580
And so they eventually just were going to
kill the project.

01:05:54.580 --> 01:05:56.839
The executives come and they tell us after
18 months of working on it.

01:05:56.839 --> 01:06:00.730
And this is pretty common in games, but I
had never had a project I had worked on get

01:06:00.730 --> 01:06:01.730
killed like that.

01:06:01.730 --> 01:06:02.730
And I was like, devastated.

01:06:02.730 --> 01:06:05.790
My dream project, I'm going to change the
world with this game.

01:06:05.790 --> 01:06:08.210
It's going to be incredible, now all of a
sudden, ashes.

01:06:08.210 --> 01:06:09.391
And so I refused.

01:06:09.391 --> 01:06:10.391
I didn't want to let it happen.

01:06:10.391 --> 01:06:13.930
And so I said, "Listen, I don't know what
I'm doing here, but if you're going to kill

01:06:13.930 --> 01:06:15.799
the project, give me six months.

01:06:15.799 --> 01:06:18.130
Let me see if I can figure it out.

01:06:18.130 --> 01:06:20.230
You've got nothing to lose."

01:06:20.230 --> 01:06:25.220
And I was able to convince them to let me
become a project manager and try to figure

01:06:25.220 --> 01:06:28.500
out how to make games, how to make a miniatures
game, specifically.

01:06:28.500 --> 01:06:32.369
And so same kind of process like I did when
I first started to design, I was like, "All

01:06:32.369 --> 01:06:33.990
right, let me talk to people who know what
they're doing.

01:06:33.990 --> 01:06:35.630
I've already got a very clear deadline."

01:06:35.630 --> 01:06:40.150
I talked to Jeremy Cranford, who's an incredible
art director, and I talked to the guy that

01:06:40.150 --> 01:06:41.829
made the Dungeons &amp; Dragons miniatures.

01:06:41.829 --> 01:06:45.329
I just worked months and months and months,
try to figure out how to make this work, fly

01:06:45.329 --> 01:06:48.839
to China, fly to the factory, figure out how
the plastics and paints and all this crazy

01:06:48.839 --> 01:06:49.839
stuff.

01:06:49.839 --> 01:06:53.890
Tim Ferriss: And making it work, just for
my clarity, is making it financially work

01:06:53.890 --> 01:06:54.890
for the company?

01:06:54.890 --> 01:06:55.890
Justin Gary: That's exactly right.

01:06:55.890 --> 01:06:57.220
Financially, how do you make it work?

01:06:57.220 --> 01:07:01.430
Because Blizzard, who makes World of Warcraft,
had very exacting standards about their miniatures.

01:07:01.430 --> 01:07:04.750
They have to have spiky pauldrons this high
and these amounts of colors.

01:07:04.750 --> 01:07:07.190
And nobody had ever done anything quite like
that before.

01:07:07.190 --> 01:07:10.930
And it needs to be financially viable to make
this product for the company I was working

01:07:10.930 --> 01:07:11.930
for.

01:07:11.930 --> 01:07:12.990
So that's a partnership.

01:07:12.990 --> 01:07:18.380
And so I have to go figure out all these logistics,
set all this stuff up.

01:07:18.380 --> 01:07:25.200
Finally, and this is where I first read The
4-Hour Workweek, so this is actually was super

01:07:25.200 --> 01:07:29.339
impactful for me in being very efficient using
80/20 principles.

01:07:29.339 --> 01:07:33.890
And I've learned to set up auto email replies,
so people didn't bother me.

01:07:33.890 --> 01:07:38.529
So already getting my efficiencies in it,
but I'm in this zone of just, I'm going to

01:07:38.529 --> 01:07:39.650
finish this mission.

01:07:39.650 --> 01:07:47.440
And then I finally go to — I have the opportunity
to speak to the executive team.

01:07:47.440 --> 01:07:48.440
I'm going to make my presentation.

01:07:48.440 --> 01:07:49.440
I'm going to make my case.

01:07:49.440 --> 01:07:52.329
Is this project going to live or is this project
going to die?

01:07:52.329 --> 01:07:57.549
And I'm just some kid who wants to make a
game and I've got to convince — so I'm there.

01:07:57.549 --> 01:07:58.690
And I will never forget.

01:07:58.690 --> 01:08:00.230
I mean, they had this ugly green carpet.

01:08:00.230 --> 01:08:03.410
I'm sitting, waiting for my turn to get called
in.

01:08:03.410 --> 01:08:05.369
The details are super stuck in my mind.

01:08:05.369 --> 01:08:08.859
And I'm like, go down this long corridor.

01:08:08.859 --> 01:08:11.980
And I'm like, my palms are sweating.

01:08:11.980 --> 01:08:17.420
I go, you open these huge oak doors and it's
a huge sports memorabilia company.

01:08:17.420 --> 01:08:23.130
So there's a Babe Ruth-signed bat on the wall
and a Michael Jordan jersey and a Tiger Woods

01:08:23.130 --> 01:08:24.130
golf club.

01:08:24.130 --> 01:08:26.370
And the rich mahogany conference table.

01:08:26.370 --> 01:08:31.980
I mean, it's boardroom cliche, but it's very
intimidating for me at this time.

01:08:31.980 --> 01:08:36.520
So I get up there, the owner's sitting in
his leather chair and staring me down, and

01:08:36.520 --> 01:08:42.020
I'm like, I give my presentation and I'm stammering
through it, but I've done my research, I've

01:08:42.020 --> 01:08:46.710
done my homework, and I go through the presentation
and heart's beating.

01:08:46.710 --> 01:08:49.560
Finally, I stop and there's this pause at
the end.

01:08:49.560 --> 01:08:51.260
I'm looking around, what do they think?

01:08:51.260 --> 01:08:54.720
It's like CEO, CFO, all the big wigs.

01:08:54.720 --> 01:08:59.380
And they start talking about the idea and
they start joshing around and I start thinking,

01:08:59.380 --> 01:09:01.400
"Oh, my God, I think they like it.

01:09:01.400 --> 01:09:03.420
I think this is really going to happen."

01:09:03.420 --> 01:09:06.120
And then they start pontificating and they
start talking, "Oh, well there may be this

01:09:06.120 --> 01:09:08.049
problem with this thing or this thing."

01:09:08.049 --> 01:09:13.390
And it took me a minute to realize this, but
as they were talking, I realized that they

01:09:13.390 --> 01:09:17.790
had no idea what they were talking about.

01:09:17.790 --> 01:09:25.319
I had finally done enough research that I
knew they didn't know what they were doing.

01:09:25.319 --> 01:09:30.170
And up to that point in my life, I feel like
I had just assumed that the adults in the

01:09:30.170 --> 01:09:31.199
room knew what they were doing.

01:09:31.199 --> 01:09:36.270
This is the same company that makes hundreds
of millions of dollars a year.

01:09:36.270 --> 01:09:38.680
Of course they know what's happening.

01:09:38.680 --> 01:09:40.530
And now all of a sudden that illusion is shattered.

01:09:40.530 --> 01:09:42.540
And I started out terrified.

01:09:42.540 --> 01:09:43.540
I'm terrified.

01:09:43.540 --> 01:09:47.920
I mean, we're literally, this project, it's
going to cost hundreds of thousands of dollars.

01:09:47.920 --> 01:09:50.550
Hopefully it'll make millions, or it could
lose millions, who knows?

01:09:50.550 --> 01:09:52.060
And nobody knows what they're doing.

01:09:52.060 --> 01:09:54.680
And so I'm like, "Oh, my God, what do we do?"

01:09:54.680 --> 01:09:58.480
And then I walk out of that room, my heart's
beating, and I'm just like, "What's going

01:09:58.480 --> 01:09:59.480
to happen?

01:09:59.480 --> 01:10:00.480
How do we do this?"

01:10:00.480 --> 01:10:04.300
And then all of a sudden, a cold splash of
water in my face: nobody knows what they're

01:10:04.300 --> 01:10:05.300
doing.

01:10:05.300 --> 01:10:09.940
I cannot know what I'm doing at least as well
as anybody else.

01:10:09.940 --> 01:10:14.960
This idea that the difference between a leader
and somebody else is not like that they know

01:10:14.960 --> 01:10:17.100
something or that they have some special access.

01:10:17.100 --> 01:10:20.730
It's that you're just willing to make some
assertions and own the consequences.

01:10:20.730 --> 01:10:21.920
That's it.

01:10:21.920 --> 01:10:23.310
That's the difference.

01:10:23.310 --> 01:10:31.080
And so then [it] finally clicked for me, the
other parts of your book, that not just this

01:10:31.080 --> 01:10:36.310
efficiency and automation, but this idea of
defining what you want and then creating the

01:10:36.310 --> 01:10:37.920
freedom for yourself to do it.

01:10:37.920 --> 01:10:41.580
And so that's where I suddenly started doing
the fear-setting exercise and going through,

01:10:41.580 --> 01:10:44.600
"What's the worst case scenario for me if
I quit and I do my own thing?"

01:10:44.600 --> 01:10:49.120
And the dream lining exercise, which is basically,
"What is it that you really want?"

01:10:49.120 --> 01:10:50.140
How do you define what you want?

01:10:50.140 --> 01:10:52.120
How do you make that very concrete?

01:10:52.120 --> 01:10:54.969
And so then that transformed like, I'm going
to get this game out the door.

01:10:54.969 --> 01:10:58.770
I'm going to save up enough money so I have
a year of savings, and I quit.

01:10:58.770 --> 01:11:00.320
And that's exactly what I did.

01:11:00.320 --> 01:11:04.739
And it was a life-changing moment for me because
it was exactly that.

01:11:04.739 --> 01:11:07.880
That's taking ownership of my future.

01:11:07.880 --> 01:11:10.730
And again, I'm so grateful for you as a key
part of it.

01:11:10.730 --> 01:11:15.280
And I'm so grateful for those executives because
they really opened the door for me.

01:11:15.280 --> 01:11:18.140
And I don't pretend to know what I'm doing
all the time either.

01:11:18.140 --> 01:11:22.470
Most of the time we're making stuff up, but
it's being conscious about that, surfacing

01:11:22.470 --> 01:11:25.150
our assumptions and learning as you go.

01:11:25.150 --> 01:11:26.150
It's been a powerful journey.

01:11:26.150 --> 01:11:30.310
Tim Ferriss: So thank you for sharing that
and for saying all that you said.

01:11:30.310 --> 01:11:35.260
And I would love to know when you did the
fear-setting and you're trying to identify

01:11:35.260 --> 01:11:40.390
the worst case, how you can mitigate against
the worst case, what you would do as sort

01:11:40.390 --> 01:11:45.159
of a backup plan in case the entrepreneurship
does not work out or maybe doesn't work out

01:11:45.159 --> 01:11:47.730
at least with the first inning.

01:11:47.730 --> 01:11:51.700
How were you thinking about that at the time?

01:11:51.700 --> 01:11:55.780
In addition to what you already described,
which is the realization that, "Oh, my God,

01:11:55.780 --> 01:11:57.360
almost all the emperors have no clothing.

01:11:57.360 --> 01:11:59.100
So I think I can do that.

01:11:59.100 --> 01:12:02.780
At the very worst, I can also do that."

01:12:02.780 --> 01:12:07.750
How are you thinking about risk assessment
and what you would do if things didn't pan

01:12:07.750 --> 01:12:10.580
out perfectly as you went into entrepreneurship?

01:12:10.580 --> 01:12:11.580
Justin Gary: Yeah.

01:12:11.580 --> 01:12:13.280
So I got very concrete.

01:12:13.280 --> 01:12:16.010
So what is my actual minimal living expenses?

01:12:16.010 --> 01:12:19.060
What do I need to be okay and have what I
need?

01:12:19.060 --> 01:12:23.760
And I figured out what that number was, and
then I was like, how much time am I willing

01:12:23.760 --> 01:12:27.650
to give myself to run this experiment to see
if I can figure it out and make entrepreneurship

01:12:27.650 --> 01:12:28.650
work?

01:12:28.650 --> 01:12:30.590
And I decided a year of savings was what I
wanted.

01:12:30.590 --> 01:12:32.190
And so I focused on saving up.

01:12:32.190 --> 01:12:35.489
So I have that cushion, because if you're
in the red, if you're like, "I don't know

01:12:35.489 --> 01:12:39.350
how to make my next paycheck, I don't know
how to make rent next month," you can't be

01:12:39.350 --> 01:12:42.000
creative and be free, you're making decisions
for the short term.

01:12:42.000 --> 01:12:43.660
And I wanted to be able to make decisions
for the long term.

01:12:43.660 --> 01:12:47.020
So step one was have enough of a cushion that
I could feel comfortable.

01:12:47.020 --> 01:12:51.130
Now other people can do this as a side project
or after hours, whatever.

01:12:51.130 --> 01:12:53.550
But for me, I wanted space to just focus on
this.

01:12:53.550 --> 01:12:56.800
And then the other piece was like, well, let's
assume it doesn't happen.

01:12:56.800 --> 01:12:59.100
And after a year, I've gotten nothing and
it's not working.

01:12:59.100 --> 01:13:02.120
I had done enough in my career at that point,
I could just get another job.

01:13:02.120 --> 01:13:04.870
I could either be hired back at the same place
or hired at another company.

01:13:04.870 --> 01:13:09.280
It's not like I was going to go hungry on
the streets, or if I have to live on a friend's

01:13:09.280 --> 01:13:11.510
couch for a little while, that's not that
big a deal either.

01:13:11.510 --> 01:13:13.060
So I went through all of those things.

01:13:13.060 --> 01:13:18.210
I could go bankrupt, my house catches on fire,
hilariously like you did in the book.

01:13:18.210 --> 01:13:23.219
And so that's where it highlights the same
kind of lesson as before, which was when I

01:13:23.219 --> 01:13:24.340
quit law school.

01:13:24.340 --> 01:13:25.770
It sounds scary.

01:13:25.770 --> 01:13:27.550
Everybody thinks, "Oh, my God, you're such
a risk-taker."

01:13:27.550 --> 01:13:28.770
I'm not.

01:13:28.770 --> 01:13:30.770
I mitigate risk at every turn.

01:13:30.770 --> 01:13:34.070
It's like when you're playing Magic and you're
trying to set up so you can't get outdrawn

01:13:34.070 --> 01:13:38.190
on a game, I'm just mitigating the number
of ways my opponent could beat me over time.

01:13:38.190 --> 01:13:40.810
And in this one, I could always go back to
law school when I quit law school.

01:13:40.810 --> 01:13:44.780
I could always go back to a job when I started
this entrepreneurship.

01:13:44.780 --> 01:13:47.560
And same thing later on when I became a digital
nomad, sold all my stuff.

01:13:47.560 --> 01:13:49.150
It's like I could always buy more stuff.

01:13:49.150 --> 01:13:50.960
I could always get a house again.

01:13:50.960 --> 01:13:53.480
So I strongly encourage people to just go
through that.

01:13:53.480 --> 01:13:58.909
If you really, really think about the worst
case scenarios, 99 times out of 100, they're

01:13:58.909 --> 01:14:00.520
totally recoverable within a year.

01:14:00.520 --> 01:14:02.030
It's often way less.

01:14:02.030 --> 01:14:08.940
Tim Ferriss: And what was your, knowing how
methodical you are, you were not just hopping

01:14:08.940 --> 01:14:11.929
out of this job with savings, with no plan.

01:14:11.929 --> 01:14:13.600
So what was your plan?

01:14:13.600 --> 01:14:19.900
Justin Gary: I'm sad to say there wasn't that
much of a plan.

01:14:19.900 --> 01:14:22.030
You kind of set me up for having a master
plan.

01:14:22.030 --> 01:14:27.290
Tim Ferriss: Well, I guess with the core design
loop, I guess you are going to be following

01:14:27.290 --> 01:14:32.790
some version of that probably, but did you
have an inkling of what type of game or what

01:14:32.790 --> 01:14:34.010
types of games you wanted to make?

01:14:34.010 --> 01:14:38.350
Justin Gary: Yeah, so I knew I loved — obviously
card games were kind of my specialty.

01:14:38.350 --> 01:14:42.610
And so I knew that that was a space I wanted
to be in, but I really wanted to create that

01:14:42.610 --> 01:14:44.139
open space to figure things out.

01:14:44.139 --> 01:14:46.580
And so as you mentioned, of course, the core
design loop was part of it.

01:14:46.580 --> 01:14:50.450
I didn't have it as clearly defined as I do
now, but this idea that I'm going to try things

01:14:50.450 --> 01:14:51.621
and prototype them and test them.

01:14:51.621 --> 01:14:55.820
And so during the day, I would work on some
designs, make up some prototypes, and then

01:14:55.820 --> 01:14:59.830
I would go find my friends who were still
working at the company or elsewhere to try

01:14:59.830 --> 01:15:02.030
it out, give me some feedback and come back.

01:15:02.030 --> 01:15:03.800
And I didn't have any ideas.

01:15:03.800 --> 01:15:05.489
I hadn't designed a game ahead of time.

01:15:05.489 --> 01:15:09.840
I didn't know what I was going to do ahead
of time, but I had enough space that I could

01:15:09.840 --> 01:15:10.840
figure that out.

01:15:10.840 --> 01:15:16.580
And then it worked out that another person
from another company contacted me to see if

01:15:16.580 --> 01:15:17.980
I could do some contract work for them.

01:15:17.980 --> 01:15:21.160
And so I started working on some kids toy-based
games, which was great.

01:15:21.160 --> 01:15:22.429
And so here's another fun story.

01:15:22.429 --> 01:15:25.130
So they knew me because they used to work
at Upper Deck where I used to work, and then

01:15:25.130 --> 01:15:28.300
they started working at this other company
and he's like, "Hey, we need someone to help

01:15:28.300 --> 01:15:29.300
us design a game.

01:15:29.300 --> 01:15:30.300
Can you do that?"

01:15:30.300 --> 01:15:31.480
And I'm just like, again, no job, no plan.

01:15:31.480 --> 01:15:33.790
I'm just figuring it out as I go at this point.

01:15:33.790 --> 01:15:36.150
And so I'm like, "Yep, definitely I can do
that."

01:15:36.150 --> 01:15:37.270
"Okay, well give us a quote."

01:15:37.270 --> 01:15:39.500
I'm like, "Okay, quote, how do I quote?

01:15:39.500 --> 01:15:40.969
I've never quoted anything before."

01:15:40.969 --> 01:15:43.000
So I'm like, "Okay, let me just think about
it."

01:15:43.000 --> 01:15:45.040
All right, how many hours do I think this
is going to be?

01:15:45.040 --> 01:15:48.920
And then what's my minimum hourly rate that
I think I should be paid?

01:15:48.920 --> 01:15:51.080
And I am like, "Okay, that's what I'll charge,
make it easy."

01:15:51.080 --> 01:15:54.110
And I send him the quote and he literally
laughs at my face.

01:15:54.110 --> 01:15:55.820
And he's like, "Yeah, okay, sure.

01:15:55.820 --> 01:15:56.820
Sure, man.

01:15:56.820 --> 01:15:57.820
No problem."

01:15:57.820 --> 01:15:58.900
Tim Ferriss: And you're like, "Uh-oh, wait
a second."

01:15:58.900 --> 01:16:02.010
Justin Gary: I'm undercharging.

01:16:02.010 --> 01:16:05.180
I still try to overdeliver, do a great job.

01:16:05.180 --> 01:16:08.179
Next time I get another thing, opportunities,
"Okay, what do you quote?"

01:16:08.179 --> 01:16:09.179
I doubled my number.

01:16:09.179 --> 01:16:10.300
They still laughed at me.

01:16:10.300 --> 01:16:11.770
It's like, "Okay, great.

01:16:11.770 --> 01:16:12.770
All right."

01:16:12.770 --> 01:16:13.770
Doubled it again.

01:16:13.770 --> 01:16:17.170
That — my strategy was I kept doubling what
I asked for until I met resistance.

01:16:17.170 --> 01:16:19.150
Until someone was like, "Hmm, I'm not sure."

01:16:19.150 --> 01:16:21.460
I'm like, "Okay, that's what I need to be
charging."

01:16:21.460 --> 01:16:26.540
Tim Ferriss: There's one other story, I'll
just as a side note mention, which also just

01:16:26.540 --> 01:16:33.060
underscores the fact that part of the frustration,
but also joy of entrepreneurship is you have

01:16:33.060 --> 01:16:35.050
to figure out a lot on the fly.

01:16:35.050 --> 01:16:37.770
You cannot prepare for all the eventualities.

01:16:37.770 --> 01:16:39.920
You don't know what you don't know.

01:16:39.920 --> 01:16:43.970
And I recall, I might be misattributing, so
my apologies to the founders of Nantucket

01:16:43.970 --> 01:16:49.000
Nectars if this is misattribution, but when
I took my first entrepreneurship class, first

01:16:49.000 --> 01:16:53.680
and only actually, high-tech entrepreneurship
with Professor Ed Zschau, who changed my life

01:16:53.680 --> 01:16:58.989
in so many ways, he used what a lot of people
refer to as the Harvard case study method

01:16:58.989 --> 01:17:03.710
where you have sort of a business situation,
then a problem, and then there's a break and

01:17:03.710 --> 01:17:04.920
you have to try to figure it out.

01:17:04.920 --> 01:17:10.600
And then only after you've had the chance
to try to figure it out on your own as a class,

01:17:10.600 --> 01:17:13.620
then you read the outcome and the decision
they made and so on.

01:17:13.620 --> 01:17:17.909
And there was part of this Nantucket Nectars
story where these guys are selling juices

01:17:17.909 --> 01:17:23.210
in Nantucket and they end up with a tiger
by the tail because their drinks become really

01:17:23.210 --> 01:17:26.050
successful and they decide to make the shift
to retail.

01:17:26.050 --> 01:17:29.570
And they meet with a distributor and they're
talking and talking, and the distributor's

01:17:29.570 --> 01:17:32.110
like, "So do you have good plans for POS?"

01:17:32.110 --> 01:17:34.889
And they look at each other, they have no
fucking idea what he's talking about.

01:17:34.889 --> 01:17:38.840
And they're like, "Oh, we have great plans
for POS.

01:17:38.840 --> 01:17:41.830
We've thought it through and we think we have
an excellent strategy."

01:17:41.830 --> 01:17:43.170
And he's like, "Okay, great."

01:17:43.170 --> 01:17:45.930
And then they leave the meeting and they're
like, "What the hell is POS?"

01:17:45.930 --> 01:17:47.400
Point of sale.

01:17:47.400 --> 01:17:48.449
Displays.

01:17:48.449 --> 01:17:50.949
So it makes for a lot of good stories.

01:17:50.949 --> 01:17:56.139
So you start to figure out your pricing by
doubling and doubling and doubling.

01:17:56.139 --> 01:18:01.090
And just maybe you could flesh that out a
little bit in terms of you're doing this contract

01:18:01.090 --> 01:18:06.000
work for people who are giving you specs of
some type or another.

01:18:06.000 --> 01:18:07.000
Justin Gary: That's right.

01:18:07.000 --> 01:18:09.969
Tim Ferriss: But you have some money coming
in the door with fewer, fewer, and fewer laughs

01:18:09.969 --> 01:18:11.420
at your proposals along the way.

01:18:11.420 --> 01:18:12.420
Justin Gary: Correct.

01:18:12.420 --> 01:18:14.000
Tim Ferriss: What else is happening in that
first year?

01:18:14.000 --> 01:18:18.000
Justin Gary: Yeah, so I'm using my kind of
spare cycles and there's this really interesting

01:18:18.000 --> 01:18:23.861
thing, the difference between an entrepreneur
who has no employees or anything, and some

01:18:23.861 --> 01:18:29.460
dude just sitting on his couch can be very
subtle.

01:18:29.460 --> 01:18:33.950
Until you start really getting income coming
in and start making things happen.

01:18:33.950 --> 01:18:36.610
So there's definitely a window of uncertainty.

01:18:36.610 --> 01:18:41.270
Tim Ferriss: I mean even now in my life, the
distinction's pretty blurry.

01:18:41.270 --> 01:18:42.600
Justin Gary: Right, it is.

01:18:42.600 --> 01:18:46.610
Now I've got a team and the people, it's like,
okay, I feel like I've got a business.

01:18:46.610 --> 01:18:49.270
But it's not an easy distinction.

01:18:49.270 --> 01:18:54.160
And when you're trying to create something
where you don't necessarily know what it is,

01:18:54.160 --> 01:18:55.160
it's even harder.

01:18:55.160 --> 01:18:59.000
So what I would do is I would, in between
doing this work for hire, I would work on

01:18:59.000 --> 01:19:00.530
just projects that I thought would be fun.

01:19:00.530 --> 01:19:04.250
And so I would just make prototypes, try them
out, try them out on friends.

01:19:04.250 --> 01:19:10.400
And I remember it was actually with Ascension
where I didn't make Ascension specifically

01:19:10.400 --> 01:19:12.230
to think, "Oh, I'm going to start a company
with this game."

01:19:12.230 --> 01:19:14.950
I made it because I really wanted that game
to exist.

01:19:14.950 --> 01:19:19.740
I played enough of Dominion and I was missing
something I really, really wanted.

01:19:19.740 --> 01:19:24.270
And then I showed it to a friend of mine,
Rob Dougherty, and he has his own — he actually

01:19:24.270 --> 01:19:27.219
was the guy that owned Your Move Games, the
store, and where I used to play when I was

01:19:27.219 --> 01:19:29.130
younger.

01:19:29.130 --> 01:19:33.790
And I show him the game and he's like, "Dude,
you have the ball, run with it.

01:19:33.790 --> 01:19:34.790
This is great.

01:19:34.790 --> 01:19:35.790
Make this a thing."

01:19:35.790 --> 01:19:38.140
And he had been an entrepreneur for a while.

01:19:38.140 --> 01:19:41.310
And so he helped me kind of like, "Okay, no,
let's do this."

01:19:41.310 --> 01:19:44.080
And that started down the road.

01:19:44.080 --> 01:19:47.010
So, "This is going to be my project now."

01:19:47.010 --> 01:19:48.010
And I started.

01:19:48.010 --> 01:19:49.010
My early prototypes were ugly.

01:19:49.010 --> 01:19:50.460
And I'll find a way to share them with you.

01:19:50.460 --> 01:19:52.120
I mean, some of them are horribly inappropriate.

01:19:52.120 --> 01:19:56.030
We had just really just rude pictures and
I won't share those, but they — just ridiculous.

01:19:56.030 --> 01:19:57.150
It was just for fun.

01:19:57.150 --> 01:19:58.150
Tim Ferriss: Rude pictures.

01:19:58.150 --> 01:19:59.150
How dare you?

01:19:59.150 --> 01:20:00.150
Justin Gary: I know.

01:20:00.150 --> 01:20:01.150
Tim Ferriss: Continue.

01:20:01.150 --> 01:20:02.150
Justin Gary: Yeah.

01:20:02.150 --> 01:20:03.150
Anyway, it was —
Tim Ferriss: Imagining that's code for lots

01:20:03.150 --> 01:20:04.150
of dicks everywhere?

01:20:04.150 --> 01:20:07.429
Justin Gary: Well, you know, I'm not — I'll
leave it to the imagination of the listeners

01:20:07.429 --> 01:20:09.199
on this one.

01:20:09.199 --> 01:20:11.620
Not appropriate for public consumption.

01:20:11.620 --> 01:20:12.620
Leave it at that.

01:20:12.620 --> 01:20:16.340
Tim Ferriss: You know what, just to defend
my dicks comment, I will say there's a place

01:20:16.340 --> 01:20:19.800
called Hotel Biron in San Francisco.

01:20:19.800 --> 01:20:22.449
Great wine spot, excellent place.

01:20:22.449 --> 01:20:29.530
And I don't know if this is true, but I heard
at one point, this is one of the first places

01:20:29.530 --> 01:20:34.690
with a console for digital signing way back
in the day.

01:20:34.690 --> 01:20:40.880
And the rumor was that of all the guys who
went there and bought drinks, it was like

01:20:40.880 --> 01:20:46.500
75 percent just drew dicks as a signature,
which I thought it was pretty hilarious.

01:20:46.500 --> 01:20:49.460
Justin Gary: Well, there's an important game
design principle I didn't talk about here,

01:20:49.460 --> 01:20:58.260
which is the TTP, which is time to penis.

01:20:58.260 --> 01:21:02.410
Any opportunity that you give people to create
their own thing, at some point someone's going

01:21:02.410 --> 01:21:03.410
to make a dick.

01:21:03.410 --> 01:21:04.410
That's going to happen.

01:21:04.410 --> 01:21:05.620
Tim Ferriss: TTP.

01:21:05.620 --> 01:21:09.520
I'm going to add that to my metrics.

01:21:09.520 --> 01:21:11.870
Justin Gary: Key show notes.

01:21:11.870 --> 01:21:16.770
I mean, so what it is, the more broader point
is anytime you allow people to customize,

01:21:16.770 --> 01:21:19.420
you have to think, what are they going to
do with it and how does that share and how

01:21:19.420 --> 01:21:20.420
does that build your community?

01:21:20.420 --> 01:21:23.210
But TTP's the shorthand for that.

01:21:23.210 --> 01:21:28.840
So it is an unfortunate reality of our society
that is just going to happen.

01:21:28.840 --> 01:21:30.150
Tim Ferriss: Okay.

01:21:30.150 --> 01:21:32.320
So you'll —
Justin Gary: I did not see this interview

01:21:32.320 --> 01:21:35.290
going this way, I'll tell you that right now.

01:21:35.290 --> 01:21:37.270
Tim Ferriss: Oh, I didn't either.

01:21:37.270 --> 01:21:38.270
Thanks, CØCKPUNCH Coffee.

01:21:38.270 --> 01:21:42.580
All right, so let me try to right the ship
here for a second.

01:21:42.580 --> 01:21:46.030
So you're talking about roughs and the prototypes.

01:21:46.030 --> 01:21:50.830
You'll filter out some of the rude pictures
and we'll share some of that.

01:21:50.830 --> 01:21:56.500
When you were chatting with, if I'm remembering
correctly, Rob, and you say, "You've got this,

01:21:56.500 --> 01:22:02.750
run with this," were you at that point at
all thinking of how it fit into the potentially

01:22:02.750 --> 01:22:06.190
competitive landscape and how it was differentiated?

01:22:06.190 --> 01:22:13.239
Or was it just enough for it to be something
that you used to scratch your own itch and

01:22:13.239 --> 01:22:18.130
you assumed, "Hey, if this is exciting for
me as one person who plays a lot of games,

01:22:18.130 --> 01:22:21.180
I assume it's going to be attractive to more
people?"

01:22:21.180 --> 01:22:23.290
Justin Gary: So three things.

01:22:23.290 --> 01:22:27.300
One, absolutely, it has to scratch my own
itch and I have to love it.

01:22:27.300 --> 01:22:28.300
I loved it.

01:22:28.300 --> 01:22:29.300
I wanted to see it exist.

01:22:29.300 --> 01:22:33.481
All of my most successful projects by far
are the things that I make for me and that

01:22:33.481 --> 01:22:34.730
I really want.

01:22:34.730 --> 01:22:35.960
And then I know they're going to be great.

01:22:35.960 --> 01:22:37.679
So I think that's really important.

01:22:37.679 --> 01:22:40.460
Two, I do not chase trends.

01:22:40.460 --> 01:22:41.719
I hate chasing trends.

01:22:41.719 --> 01:22:45.810
In fact, every time I've tried to chase a
trend, it's been unsuccessful.

01:22:45.810 --> 01:22:49.270
The idea of I'm trying to build something
for some hypothetical, I think this is hot

01:22:49.270 --> 01:22:51.239
right now, has never worked.

01:22:51.239 --> 01:22:54.950
However, there's a third point here which
is important, which is it can't be just about

01:22:54.950 --> 01:22:55.950
you.

01:22:55.950 --> 01:23:00.429
And so the rule I like to use is I will show
the game to people and I'll prototype it and

01:23:00.429 --> 01:23:01.429
test it.

01:23:01.429 --> 01:23:06.310
And when people start asking me to play again
without me prompting them, that's when I know

01:23:06.310 --> 01:23:07.560
I've got something.

01:23:07.560 --> 01:23:08.780
So it's not just me.

01:23:08.780 --> 01:23:13.139
That's why Rob, who I respect a lot, he was
like, "No, this is great.

01:23:13.139 --> 01:23:14.139
Play again."

01:23:14.139 --> 01:23:16.400
And I would show it to some other game designer
friends and show it to some other people and

01:23:16.400 --> 01:23:17.400
they're like, "No, this is great.

01:23:17.400 --> 01:23:18.400
Keep..."

01:23:18.400 --> 01:23:21.880
So as you're going through, you're getting
a wider, wider sphere of feedback that's helping

01:23:21.880 --> 01:23:22.880
you move forward.

01:23:22.880 --> 01:23:27.310
And if your game is targeted at a different
target audience — so I make a game called

01:23:27.310 --> 01:23:31.739
Bakugan, which is a toy-based game for kids,
obviously how much fun I have playing it is

01:23:31.739 --> 01:23:35.330
not nearly as important as a five-year-old
or an eight-year-old.

01:23:35.330 --> 01:23:38.210
So you have to test with your target market
in that sense.

01:23:38.210 --> 01:23:41.020
So I don't say that target market and testing
is not important.

01:23:41.020 --> 01:23:43.820
It is, but don't try to chase a trend.

01:23:43.820 --> 01:23:48.300
Make something that you know is fun and that
you enjoy and then get the feedback and iterate

01:23:48.300 --> 01:23:49.300
as you go.

01:23:49.300 --> 01:23:53.670
And so as you get that positive feedback,
now I'm willing to invest more and more into

01:23:53.670 --> 01:23:54.800
the product.

01:23:54.800 --> 01:23:59.350
So now I actually am going to go and start
putting some real art on it.

01:23:59.350 --> 01:24:03.080
And the story behind the art is, I guess it's
at this stage I can tell.

01:24:03.080 --> 01:24:09.170
So back when I was living in Boston, I met
the guy that lived down the hall and he comes

01:24:09.170 --> 01:24:11.780
and knocks on the door and says, "Hello."

01:24:11.780 --> 01:24:13.280
At this time I'd just moved out of college.

01:24:13.280 --> 01:24:16.740
So I'm like — black light posters on the
wall.

01:24:16.740 --> 01:24:18.990
It's still basically a college doom room.

01:24:18.990 --> 01:24:21.960
I just transported it into an apartment.

01:24:21.960 --> 01:24:26.300
And then the guy comes back over 20 minutes
later with a bag of weed and, "Pretty sure

01:24:26.300 --> 01:24:27.690
you guys want to smoke this, right?"

01:24:27.690 --> 01:24:28.690
It's like, "Okay, cool."

01:24:28.690 --> 01:24:33.130
So we hang out, we've become great friends
and he actually, it turns out, he's an incredible

01:24:33.130 --> 01:24:34.130
artist.

01:24:34.130 --> 01:24:35.130
His name's Eric Sabee, and whatever.

01:24:35.130 --> 01:24:36.150
We became friends.

01:24:36.150 --> 01:24:40.840
I have one of his art pieces on my — well,
not on my wall anymore, but in storage now.

01:24:40.840 --> 01:24:43.510
But actually it's on the wall of my dad's
place.

01:24:43.510 --> 01:24:45.260
I didn't put that one in storage.

01:24:45.260 --> 01:24:48.920
Anyway, so 10 years later when I'm making
a game, I'm like, "Oh man, his art was really

01:24:48.920 --> 01:24:49.920
cool.

01:24:49.920 --> 01:24:51.160
I'm going to call him up."

01:24:51.160 --> 01:24:54.860
And I called him up and said, "Hey, would
you want to make art for my game?"

01:24:54.860 --> 01:24:59.780
And I built the IP and the story around his
style because I knew I could get this batch

01:24:59.780 --> 01:25:03.159
of art without having to pay for a whole new
artist and new things because he'd already

01:25:03.159 --> 01:25:04.679
made it so I could pay him less.

01:25:04.679 --> 01:25:06.469
And he was a good friend and he hooked it
up.

01:25:06.469 --> 01:25:10.660
And so now I start bringing art into the game
and figuring out how you can use what's around

01:25:10.660 --> 01:25:14.560
and build the lore and build a style around
what's available.

01:25:14.560 --> 01:25:18.850
Okay, now we've made some art, now we've got
some cool-looking cards.

01:25:18.850 --> 01:25:20.290
I'm investing a little bit more.

01:25:20.290 --> 01:25:27.360
And then I take it to a convention called
GAMA, which is a game manufacturer's association

01:25:27.360 --> 01:25:28.360
event.

01:25:28.360 --> 01:25:31.020
It's like an industry insider tabletop game
thing.

01:25:31.020 --> 01:25:34.400
So publishers and retailers and distributors
are all there.

01:25:34.400 --> 01:25:36.330
So not a public show.

01:25:36.330 --> 01:25:40.230
And I get a little booth and I bring my little
fake prototype there.

01:25:40.230 --> 01:25:45.390
And I realized actually as I was going to
the show that I didn't have — in the game,

01:25:45.390 --> 01:25:46.590
you get what's called honor.

01:25:46.590 --> 01:25:47.960
It's like victory points basically.

01:25:47.960 --> 01:25:53.110
And I forgot to make anything that you track,
how do you track how many points you get,

01:25:53.110 --> 01:25:54.110
how many honor you get?

01:25:54.110 --> 01:25:55.920
And I was like, "Oh, man, what do we do?"

01:25:55.920 --> 01:25:59.810
And I went to a Michaels store, like a little
—

01:25:59.810 --> 01:26:00.810
Tim Ferriss: Craft store.

01:26:00.810 --> 01:26:01.810
Justin Gary: Craft store.

01:26:01.810 --> 01:26:02.810
Yes, thank you.

01:26:02.810 --> 01:26:07.120
And they had these little beads, these little
plastic fishbowl beads, and they're all oddly

01:26:07.120 --> 01:26:08.120
shaped.

01:26:08.120 --> 01:26:09.120
I was like, "All right, whatever.

01:26:09.120 --> 01:26:10.120
Let's try these.

01:26:10.120 --> 01:26:11.120
I'll just use these for the prototype."

01:26:11.120 --> 01:26:13.790
So grabbed a bunch of those, put them in,
and we start demoing the game.

01:26:13.790 --> 01:26:18.179
And people get drawn to these beads and they're
like, "Oh, my God, this is awesome."

01:26:18.179 --> 01:26:20.420
Tim Ferriss: They're like pros finding a shiny
button.

01:26:20.420 --> 01:26:21.420
Justin Gary: Yes, exactly.

01:26:21.420 --> 01:26:24.389
They're super shiny and they come over and
they play the game.

01:26:24.389 --> 01:26:25.389
They like the game.

01:26:25.389 --> 01:26:26.389
It's like, "Oh, this is awesome.

01:26:26.389 --> 01:26:27.389
These are going to be in the game, right?"

01:26:27.389 --> 01:26:29.300
I was like, "Yes, yes they are."

01:26:29.300 --> 01:26:31.580
This is like the POS example you gave earlier.

01:26:31.580 --> 01:26:33.120
I have no idea how to get these things.

01:26:33.120 --> 01:26:34.340
I have no idea how to make them.

01:26:34.340 --> 01:26:37.710
I don't know what's going, but they are definitely
in the game.

01:26:37.710 --> 01:26:41.420
Anyway, so that show, the response was so
overwhelming.

01:26:41.420 --> 01:26:42.540
The people loved it.

01:26:42.540 --> 01:26:44.110
Stores wanted to order it, distributors wanted
to order.

01:26:44.110 --> 01:26:48.460
So now all of a sudden it's like, okay, now
I'm going to literally, I'm all in.

01:26:48.460 --> 01:26:52.940
I literally put my entire life savings now,
all the extra that I'd saved up to print at

01:26:52.940 --> 01:26:56.730
the time was 10,000 units of the game, which
is pretty crazy.

01:26:56.730 --> 01:26:57.730
Probably a little over it.

01:26:57.730 --> 01:26:58.730
Tim Ferriss: Sounds like a lot of units.

01:26:58.730 --> 01:26:59.730
Justin Gary: It was a lot of units.

01:26:59.730 --> 01:27:00.730
Tim Ferriss: It sounds like a lot.

01:27:00.730 --> 01:27:01.730
Justin Gary: Yeah.

01:27:01.730 --> 01:27:03.550
Most times people are going to start a new
game, it's like you print a thousand units,

01:27:03.550 --> 01:27:04.550
maybe 3,000.

01:27:04.550 --> 01:27:07.390
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, you better hope to God
you don't have a manufacturing anomaly.

01:27:07.390 --> 01:27:12.350
Justin Gary: Oh, dude, nightmare stories around
that for sure.

01:27:12.350 --> 01:27:13.989
That's a whole different beast.

01:27:13.989 --> 01:27:16.340
But again, this is not — I had worked with
manufacturers before.

01:27:16.340 --> 01:27:21.310
I had made games working for another company,
so this part wasn't totally new to me, but

01:27:21.310 --> 01:27:24.900
it was — and I wouldn't have put all of
that money into the — I used to joke, "If

01:27:24.900 --> 01:27:27.230
this doesn't work, I'm going to have to build
a house out of Ascension boxes.

01:27:27.230 --> 01:27:28.350
That's all I've got."

01:27:28.350 --> 01:27:34.280
But I wasn't, again, just to demystify the
risk, I had gotten so much positive feedback.

01:27:34.280 --> 01:27:37.469
I had stores and distributors already waiting
to order the thing.

01:27:37.469 --> 01:27:40.970
So now it's like, okay, it's willing to put
the money in and take a risk.

01:27:40.970 --> 01:27:46.179
Tim Ferriss: And also just for folks who are
unaware, I mean there are all sorts of ways

01:27:46.179 --> 01:27:51.020
to potentially finance things if you have
that demonstrated demand.

01:27:51.020 --> 01:27:55.890
I'm not suggesting this, you've got to do
your own diligence on these things, but invoice

01:27:55.890 --> 01:28:01.030
factoring, if you have people who are pledging
and signing whatever it is, letters of intent

01:28:01.030 --> 01:28:05.880
or something more binding to purchase a certain
quantity, you can use that with manufacturers

01:28:05.880 --> 01:28:09.010
to then get finance terms.

01:28:09.010 --> 01:28:11.040
And there are companies that independently
do this as well.

01:28:11.040 --> 01:28:12.500
So there are ways to work around it.

01:28:12.500 --> 01:28:17.310
My question that I want to ask next is related
to GAMA.

01:28:17.310 --> 01:28:24.920
How many exhibitors or people with games like
yourself, game developers, were there at this

01:28:24.920 --> 01:28:25.920
show?

01:28:25.920 --> 01:28:27.400
Actually we don't even need to be specific
to game developers.

01:28:27.400 --> 01:28:30.880
How many booths or exhibits were at this show?

01:28:30.880 --> 01:28:33.290
Justin Gary: At the time, I think it was about
30.

01:28:33.290 --> 01:28:34.480
Tim Ferriss: Oh, okay.

01:28:34.480 --> 01:28:35.480
All right.

01:28:35.480 --> 01:28:36.590
Justin Gary: It's not huge.

01:28:36.590 --> 01:28:40.179
Now it's bigger, maybe two or three times
that.

01:28:40.179 --> 01:28:41.179
It's less than 100.

01:28:41.179 --> 01:28:42.179
Tim Ferriss: It's not gigantic.

01:28:42.179 --> 01:28:43.179
Got it.

01:28:43.179 --> 01:28:44.179
Justin Gary: No.

01:28:44.179 --> 01:28:45.179
That's one of the nice things.

01:28:45.179 --> 01:28:48.320
This kind of game industry is still a little
smaller than a New York Toy Fair is the more

01:28:48.320 --> 01:28:53.310
big one that's like, it happens and that's
got thousands and thousands of booths and

01:28:53.310 --> 01:28:55.610
you're spending millions of dollars to have
a presence there.

01:28:55.610 --> 01:28:57.380
It's like a much bigger piece.

01:28:57.380 --> 01:28:58.380
This one's a little smaller.

01:28:58.380 --> 01:29:02.620
Tim Ferriss: So the reason I'm asking is that
in fact, if somebody wants just a separate

01:29:02.620 --> 01:29:06.400
collection of stories around these types of
trade shows, and I think it was the New York

01:29:06.400 --> 01:29:12.290
Toy Fair, Todd McFarlane, the legendary comic
book artist, has some stories around this

01:29:12.290 --> 01:29:15.610
from our conversations, which are hilarious.

01:29:15.610 --> 01:29:20.900
What I was wondering, and maybe it doesn't
apply because there were 30 tables or 30 booths,

01:29:20.900 --> 01:29:27.719
but did you do anything to draw attention
to yourself or the game at this event?

01:29:27.719 --> 01:29:33.090
Or was it simply enough to pay for a booth
and then assume that there would be enough

01:29:33.090 --> 01:29:35.320
foot traffic to bring the right people to
you?

01:29:35.320 --> 01:29:37.940
Justin Gary: So the principle you're driving
at is critical.

01:29:37.940 --> 01:29:43.040
You need to get people's attention, you need
to find a way at these booths, even at Gen

01:29:43.040 --> 01:29:47.219
Con, which is the tabletop, more public-facing
table convention where I actually did launch

01:29:47.219 --> 01:29:51.199
Ascension and there's a lot more people and
a lot more vying for traffic at shows like

01:29:51.199 --> 01:29:54.630
GAMA, anywhere, or even on a store shelf.

01:29:54.630 --> 01:29:57.699
Why is someone going to stop and pay attention
to what you're doing?

01:29:57.699 --> 01:30:02.000
The great thing about where we are in our
modern world is it's easier than ever to make

01:30:02.000 --> 01:30:03.000
stuff.

01:30:03.000 --> 01:30:05.969
You can print your own books, you can print
your own games on demand, you can make anything

01:30:05.969 --> 01:30:06.969
you want.

01:30:06.969 --> 01:30:09.800
The downside is we're flooded with stuff,
not surprising.

01:30:09.800 --> 01:30:11.580
And so how do you separate from the crowd?

01:30:11.580 --> 01:30:12.860
How do you draw people's attention?

01:30:12.860 --> 01:30:17.639
So at GAMA, I mean, I already told you by
accident almost, the shiny beads helped.

01:30:17.639 --> 01:30:18.639
Nobody had those shiny beads.

01:30:18.639 --> 01:30:19.940
That was a really attractive thing.

01:30:19.940 --> 01:30:23.560
But I also invested in a video drop.

01:30:23.560 --> 01:30:28.300
I had a TV screen and I made a little cool
rotating loop showing the things.

01:30:28.300 --> 01:30:30.100
Most people there didn't do that.

01:30:30.100 --> 01:30:34.440
And so finding something that draws people's
eye and brings them in.

01:30:34.440 --> 01:30:36.420
So it can be a cool unique component.

01:30:36.420 --> 01:30:38.780
It can be a cool giant visual.

01:30:38.780 --> 01:30:41.940
I had a game I launched years later called
Bad Beets, B-E-E-T-S.

01:30:41.940 --> 01:30:46.219
It's a kind of play on the poker term of bad
beats, but with actual beets.

01:30:46.219 --> 01:30:47.880
I had someone dress in a giant beet costume.

01:30:47.880 --> 01:30:51.560
I put one of my team members into a giant
beet costume.

01:30:51.560 --> 01:30:53.590
And yeah, it's gimmicky, but it worked.

01:30:53.590 --> 01:30:58.080
So if you have a great product, it doesn't
matter if nobody ever looks at it.

01:30:58.080 --> 01:31:01.590
And also all the gimmicks in the world won't
help you if you don't have a great product.

01:31:01.590 --> 01:31:03.210
So step one, have something great.

01:31:03.210 --> 01:31:05.470
Step two, have a reason for people to pay
attention to you.

01:31:05.470 --> 01:31:11.540
Tim Ferriss: The workshopping attention grabbing
in real life I think is really undervalued

01:31:11.540 --> 01:31:13.590
in today's increasingly digital world.

01:31:13.590 --> 01:31:21.830
And this applies to, for instance, workshopping
book material by giving speaking engagements

01:31:21.830 --> 01:31:23.400
even to a very small group.

01:31:23.400 --> 01:31:27.290
You'll figure out very quickly what works
and what doesn't, what's confusing and what

01:31:27.290 --> 01:31:31.480
isn't, what people will remember versus what
they immediately forget or didn't even pay

01:31:31.480 --> 01:31:33.630
attention to in the first place.

01:31:33.630 --> 01:31:40.050
And the trade show stories are bringing back
memories because I recall back in my former

01:31:40.050 --> 01:31:45.320
life had my sports nutrition company and I
would go to these trade shows.

01:31:45.320 --> 01:31:50.050
And as you know, I have to imagine this is
true for these other trade shows.

01:31:50.050 --> 01:31:52.480
Once you get there, you are a captive audience.

01:31:52.480 --> 01:31:57.960
And if you want a chair, it's like, oh, yeah,
you can rent a chair for 300 bucks for a day.

01:31:57.960 --> 01:32:02.620
And I was just going in somewhat naive because
I paid the exhibitor fee and I was like, "Oh,

01:32:02.620 --> 01:32:03.710
great, I'll figure out the rest.

01:32:03.710 --> 01:32:05.480
Shouldn't be too bad."

01:32:05.480 --> 01:32:06.920
But it was pretty bad.

01:32:06.920 --> 01:32:12.590
So I remember trying to figure out how to
draw attention and at one particular trade

01:32:12.590 --> 01:32:17.360
show, I had next to no budget, because there
were a couple of things that were mandatory

01:32:17.360 --> 01:32:22.230
that you had to rent, which struck me as a
little bizarre, but it exhausted my budget.

01:32:22.230 --> 01:32:28.650
So what I ended up doing was also getting,
I bought a TV at Best Buy, a big screen that

01:32:28.650 --> 01:32:36.840
I could return, and I put on highlight reels
of Muay Thai kickboxing because it's definitely

01:32:36.840 --> 01:32:38.420
going to grab some attention.

01:32:38.420 --> 01:32:44.840
But to provide a little context, it was a
strong man/sports event.

01:32:44.840 --> 01:32:47.720
It was kind of like the Arnold Classic in
Columbus.

01:32:47.720 --> 01:32:49.989
For people who might know that, that's a huge
event.

01:32:49.989 --> 01:32:54.960
This was much smaller, but there were a lot
of athletes and competitors and so on milling

01:32:54.960 --> 01:32:55.960
about.

01:32:55.960 --> 01:33:02.520
And I also brought, I think it was four or
five of these hand grippers called the Captains

01:33:02.520 --> 01:33:07.440
of Crush, which are incredibly and increasingly
difficult to close depending on the poundage

01:33:07.440 --> 01:33:12.310
to the extent that some of them are so difficult
that there are maybe five or six people or

01:33:12.310 --> 01:33:17.070
12 people in the world who at that time could
close whatever the highest-rated Captains

01:33:17.070 --> 01:33:18.070
of Crush was.

01:33:18.070 --> 01:33:21.810
So I laid those out for people to test with
all these athletes milling about, and then

01:33:21.810 --> 01:33:29.820
put this video up, ended up getting the couch
and chairs at a Goodwill, and I had to bribe

01:33:29.820 --> 01:33:32.580
a guy to help me with this truck to get it
over.

01:33:32.580 --> 01:33:33.690
Justin Gary: Oh, yeah.

01:33:33.690 --> 01:33:39.320
Tim Ferriss: It ended up being a really hilarious
experience, but it taught me a lot about what

01:33:39.320 --> 01:33:41.090
worked and what didn't.

01:33:41.090 --> 01:33:43.989
And I was able to iterate because it was a
multi-day event.

01:33:43.989 --> 01:33:51.041
And taking a step back for a second, and then
I want to come back to Ascension and all the

01:33:51.041 --> 01:33:52.980
adventures there.

01:33:52.980 --> 01:34:00.139
What have you learned about good playtesting
or good playtesting versus not terribly effective

01:34:00.139 --> 01:34:01.139
playtesting?

01:34:01.139 --> 01:34:05.780
And what I mean by that is when you're providing
your game to people, certainly one pass fail

01:34:05.780 --> 01:34:09.380
is do they want to play it past the point
that you need them to play it?

01:34:09.380 --> 01:34:12.160
That's sort of like the Viagra test.

01:34:12.160 --> 01:34:16.929
They send it out to test patients for whatever
it was, hypotension, hypertension, something

01:34:16.929 --> 01:34:17.929
like that.

01:34:17.929 --> 01:34:22.200
And then lo and behold, all of these guys
over the age of X didn't want to send back

01:34:22.200 --> 01:34:23.200
their medication.

01:34:23.200 --> 01:34:25.010
And it's like, huh, interesting.

01:34:25.010 --> 01:34:26.010
Let's look at that.

01:34:26.010 --> 01:34:28.490
And that's how Viagra came to be in its current
iteration.

01:34:28.490 --> 01:34:32.540
But there's that, and the reason I'm asking
this, I know this is going to be a very long

01:34:32.540 --> 01:34:40.580
paragraph question, is that when I am doing
the equivalent with my books and chapters,

01:34:40.580 --> 01:34:44.130
there are definitely better and worse ways
to elicit feedback.

01:34:44.130 --> 01:34:46.140
If you're like, "What do you think of this
chapter?"

01:34:46.140 --> 01:34:47.140
It's a crapshoot.

01:34:47.140 --> 01:34:50.860
And if people are trying to be nice, which
comes down to who you select also to be your

01:34:50.860 --> 01:34:57.960
playtesters, in my case proofreaders, you're
going to get a bit of a scattershot of responses

01:34:57.960 --> 01:34:59.420
and it might not be actionable.

01:34:59.420 --> 01:35:06.920
But if I ask, "Read this chapter, tell me
the 20 percent that I should absolutely keep

01:35:06.920 --> 01:35:08.520
no matter what."

01:35:08.520 --> 01:35:13.980
And if you then tell me the 20 percent, if
you had to cut 20 percent that you would cut,

01:35:13.980 --> 01:35:18.790
you're going to get very, very different type
of surgical feedback.

01:35:18.790 --> 01:35:23.461
Similarly to someone who's maybe less experienced
as a reader, you could just say, "Note any

01:35:23.461 --> 01:35:27.880
place that is confusing or where you find
your mind wandering," and then that gets you

01:35:27.880 --> 01:35:29.440
to also stuff that you might cut.

01:35:29.440 --> 01:35:35.060
What have you learned about eliciting feedback
or evaluating feedback when you're letting

01:35:35.060 --> 01:35:36.100
people test a game?

01:35:36.100 --> 01:35:37.390
Justin Gary: Yeah, that's a great question.

01:35:37.390 --> 01:35:42.890
So it's going to depend upon the phase that
you're in.

01:35:42.890 --> 01:35:46.550
Every time I go through the core design loop,
I like to think of myself like a scientist.

01:35:46.550 --> 01:35:50.920
I have some hypothesis that I'm trying to
test, and that's what I'm focused on in that

01:35:50.920 --> 01:35:52.260
testing session.

01:35:52.260 --> 01:35:56.210
So when you're early on, you're like, is this
even fun?

01:35:56.210 --> 01:36:00.890
Is this idea of rolling dice to try to punch
somebody and making combos fun or whatever

01:36:00.890 --> 01:36:01.890
it is.

01:36:01.890 --> 01:36:02.890
You want to just see, "Is it fun?"

01:36:02.890 --> 01:36:04.280
I'm not asking, "Is it balanced?"

01:36:04.280 --> 01:36:05.410
I'm not asking, "Is it pretty?"

01:36:05.410 --> 01:36:06.890
I'm not asking, "Does the game last too long?"

01:36:06.890 --> 01:36:08.540
I'm asking, "Is this part fun?"

01:36:08.540 --> 01:36:09.880
And that's what I'm focused on.

01:36:09.880 --> 01:36:14.460
And so I know when I'm testing that if I start
getting feedback on these other parts, I'm

01:36:14.460 --> 01:36:15.460
not worried about that.

01:36:15.460 --> 01:36:17.780
I can set that aside, because that's not what
I'm looking for here.

01:36:17.780 --> 01:36:21.720
And the whole, I want to keep playing, I want
to play again test, the Viagra test as you

01:36:21.720 --> 01:36:25.190
put it, that's not going to come true until
later on in the cycle.

01:36:25.190 --> 01:36:28.520
Your early prototypes are most likely not
going to meet that, so you're looking for

01:36:28.520 --> 01:36:29.670
other things.

01:36:29.670 --> 01:36:33.750
So I do, like you, I have a questionnaire
that I'll use.

01:36:33.750 --> 01:36:38.591
I have, it's up to for free on thinklikeagamedesigner.com/media,
or we can link it in the show notes, which

01:36:38.591 --> 01:36:39.990
has a bunch of specific questions.

01:36:39.990 --> 01:36:42.360
You can just print it out and use it.

01:36:42.360 --> 01:36:45.090
So I will ask, "All right, what are the three
things you liked the most about the game?

01:36:45.090 --> 01:36:47.110
What are the three things you liked the least
about the game?

01:36:47.110 --> 01:36:49.400
Where did you get confused?"

01:36:49.400 --> 01:36:54.340
And much like your book question, you really
want to pay attention to where people get

01:36:54.340 --> 01:36:55.500
lost and confused.

01:36:55.500 --> 01:36:59.323
And nonverbal communication is at least as
important as anything they're going to write

01:36:59.323 --> 01:37:00.323
down and tell you.

01:37:00.323 --> 01:37:02.719
Because people, if you say, "Give me the three
things you like the most, three things you

01:37:02.719 --> 01:37:04.020
like the least, what would you change?"

01:37:04.020 --> 01:37:05.020
Whatever.

01:37:05.020 --> 01:37:06.630
People will say something to you just because
they feel like they have to say something

01:37:06.630 --> 01:37:07.630
to you.

01:37:07.630 --> 01:37:09.130
It doesn't always mean that's really what
they feel.

01:37:09.130 --> 01:37:10.130
You're prompting them to it.

01:37:10.130 --> 01:37:14.360
So you have to use your intuition to say,
"A, is this relevant to the hypothesis I'm

01:37:14.360 --> 01:37:16.310
trying to test and the stage that I'm in?

01:37:16.310 --> 01:37:20.500
B, does this align with what I see visually
of what they're doing?"

01:37:20.500 --> 01:37:23.730
My mom tells me she loves all my games even
when she really has no idea what's going on

01:37:23.730 --> 01:37:24.730
in some of them.

01:37:24.730 --> 01:37:25.730
She's very nice.

01:37:25.730 --> 01:37:30.060
But if I see that somebody's getting lost
or leaning back or checking their phone, watching

01:37:30.060 --> 01:37:32.520
for those nonverbal cues is so important.

01:37:32.520 --> 01:37:36.440
And you sort of train yourself over time to
get better at that.

01:37:36.440 --> 01:37:41.860
But so asking questions to prompt for the
specifics, being focused on what's important

01:37:41.860 --> 01:37:45.060
to me right now, looking for nonverbal cues.

01:37:45.060 --> 01:37:49.020
And then in best case scenario, just like
you mentioned, you have some more sophisticated

01:37:49.020 --> 01:37:52.690
readers and you have less sophisticated readers,
you have more sophisticated game players and

01:37:52.690 --> 01:37:53.690
less sophisticated game players.

01:37:53.690 --> 01:37:58.160
So if you can test with other game designers,
they're going to be better at zeroing in on,

01:37:58.160 --> 01:38:02.540
"I don't care that the card doesn't have pictures
on it or that the numbers aren't balanced.

01:38:02.540 --> 01:38:04.920
I know what you're looking for and I can give
you feedback assigned to it."

01:38:04.920 --> 01:38:09.210
If you just take it to somebody, just a regular
person, they're going to get hung up on a

01:38:09.210 --> 01:38:10.210
lot of little things.

01:38:10.210 --> 01:38:12.790
And so their feedback's a little bit harder.

01:38:12.790 --> 01:38:15.340
You have to parse out a little bit more of
what's important.

01:38:15.340 --> 01:38:19.610
Of course, as you get more and more focused
on the final product, somebody that's in your

01:38:19.610 --> 01:38:21.869
target audience, their feedback matters a
lot.

01:38:21.869 --> 01:38:27.010
And then the last thing I'll say is a quote
that I think is also one of your favorites.

01:38:27.010 --> 01:38:29.230
It's definitely one of mine from Neil Gaiman.

01:38:29.230 --> 01:38:32.520
"When your reader says that something is wrong,
they're almost always right.

01:38:32.520 --> 01:38:34.590
And when they say how to fix it, they're almost
always wrong."

01:38:34.590 --> 01:38:39.040
I don't know if I'm butchering it, but that
is 100 percent true in games too.

01:38:39.040 --> 01:38:43.429
If you're testing with multiple people in
your target audience that are consistently

01:38:43.429 --> 01:38:47.210
saying that something's not good, you as a
designer have to fix it.

01:38:47.210 --> 01:38:49.250
They definitely do not have the right answer
for how to fix it.

01:38:49.250 --> 01:38:50.260
That's your job.

01:38:50.260 --> 01:38:54.670
But that's another key part when I'm testing
is that, I'm looking for themes, looking for

01:38:54.670 --> 01:38:55.670
patterns.

01:38:55.670 --> 01:38:58.750
One person telling me they don't like something,
maybe I can dismiss it, but if 20 people that

01:38:58.750 --> 01:39:01.409
are all in my target audience say that, that's
something I've got to focus on.

01:39:01.409 --> 01:39:02.409
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

01:39:02.409 --> 01:39:07.810
No matter how pretty the paragraph, no matter
how shiny the fishbowl pebble you might have.

01:39:07.810 --> 01:39:08.810
Justin Gary: That's right.

01:39:08.810 --> 01:39:12.611
Tim Ferriss: If a bunch of people are saying,
"I don't get it, it's confusing," you've got

01:39:12.611 --> 01:39:14.350
to cut it.

01:39:14.350 --> 01:39:15.960
Not always the easiest thing.

01:39:15.960 --> 01:39:17.630
So what happened with Ascension?

01:39:17.630 --> 01:39:23.520
So you have enough Ascension kits to build
a house, at least you put in the order.

01:39:23.520 --> 01:39:24.580
Justin Gary: That's right.

01:39:24.580 --> 01:39:25.820
Tim Ferriss: And what unfolds?

01:39:25.820 --> 01:39:30.910
Justin Gary: So then I go and I get a booth
at Gen Con as mentioned, and this was where

01:39:30.910 --> 01:39:32.250
I'm going to have the unveiling.

01:39:32.250 --> 01:39:34.699
And now again, I'm very poor at this point.

01:39:34.699 --> 01:39:39.940
Tim Ferriss: Now, Gen Con, for folks who don't
know, can you just maybe paint a picture or

01:39:39.940 --> 01:39:43.540
an analogy for what Gen Con is and the significance
of Gen Con?

01:39:43.540 --> 01:39:49.429
Justin Gary: Yeah, so Gen Con is the tabletop
and role-playing game mecca of the United

01:39:49.429 --> 01:39:50.909
States, possibly you could say the world.

01:39:50.909 --> 01:39:53.040
It's from Lake Geneva is where it started.

01:39:53.040 --> 01:39:54.040
It's why it's called Gen Con.

01:39:54.040 --> 01:39:55.040
It's not there anymore.

01:39:55.040 --> 01:39:59.760
But that's where it came from, where the original
Dungeons &amp; Dragons people got together and

01:39:59.760 --> 01:40:00.760
played.

01:40:00.760 --> 01:40:05.730
It was the first place where people gathered,
and it kind of built this core of what gaming

01:40:05.730 --> 01:40:06.820
became today.

01:40:06.820 --> 01:40:11.860
And so now it happens in Indianapolis, it's
like 60,000 people every year, somewhere in

01:40:11.860 --> 01:40:12.860
that neighborhood.

01:40:12.860 --> 01:40:16.190
And every type of game you can imagine, people
are playing all hours of the night.

01:40:16.190 --> 01:40:20.719
They call it the best four days in gaming,
but it's got anything you can dream of is

01:40:20.719 --> 01:40:22.920
there and many things you can't dream of.

01:40:22.920 --> 01:40:27.880
So it's kind of the place to be, if you will,
for tabletop and role-playing game nerds like

01:40:27.880 --> 01:40:28.880
myself.

01:40:28.880 --> 01:40:30.980
Tim Ferriss: All right, so you show up at
Gen Con.

01:40:30.980 --> 01:40:31.980
Justin Gary: All right.

01:40:31.980 --> 01:40:34.610
Tim Ferriss: You've gone from 30 booths to
God knows how many, right?

01:40:34.610 --> 01:40:38.060
Justin Gary: Yeah, can't count how many, and
I'm just a little guy.

01:40:38.060 --> 01:40:39.960
I have no company.

01:40:39.960 --> 01:40:41.790
Tim Ferriss: You're at a Taylor Swift concert
now.

01:40:41.790 --> 01:40:43.520
Justin Gary: Yeah, exactly.

01:40:43.520 --> 01:40:44.520
Exactly.

01:40:44.520 --> 01:40:48.230
And so what I had was I bought a 10 by 10
booth, which is the smallest booth you can,

01:40:48.230 --> 01:40:49.270
10 foot by 10 foot booth.

01:40:49.270 --> 01:40:55.639
And then they had a newbie competition, a
little submit your marketing plan and we can

01:40:55.639 --> 01:40:58.320
give you a 10 by 20 booth for the price of
a 10 by 10.

01:40:58.320 --> 01:40:59.719
And I won that contest.

01:40:59.719 --> 01:41:04.130
So fortunately, I got a 10 by 20 booth, which
is still very small in the scheme of what's

01:41:04.130 --> 01:41:05.389
there.

01:41:05.389 --> 01:41:07.690
And I set up my stuff.

01:41:07.690 --> 01:41:13.530
And people had started to hear about my game
because I showed it off at GAMA before, and

01:41:13.530 --> 01:41:15.230
I start kind of putting it out there in whatever
way I can.

01:41:15.230 --> 01:41:18.110
But this is just posting online and sharing
with friends.

01:41:18.110 --> 01:41:20.489
I don't have any marketing budget or anything
like that, but I'm posting and whatever I

01:41:20.489 --> 01:41:21.489
can get.

01:41:21.489 --> 01:41:26.830
And then I get there and not only do have
— we had some of the early adopters who

01:41:26.830 --> 01:41:31.660
had heard about it run to the booth, but then
they play the game and they loved it so much,

01:41:31.660 --> 01:41:36.070
they would then take it, open up, buy it,
open it up, and then start showing it to their

01:41:36.070 --> 01:41:37.070
friends.

01:41:37.070 --> 01:41:38.250
They would set up.

01:41:38.250 --> 01:41:42.030
And so we ended up — this is not allowed
at Gen Con anymore, by the way, but they would

01:41:42.030 --> 01:41:49.010
just take over nearby tables at the seated
area and just start demoing the game for me,

01:41:49.010 --> 01:41:50.150
totally for free.

01:41:50.150 --> 01:41:53.080
Didn't ask them to, didn't pay them —
Tim Ferriss: The Ascension splinter cells.

01:41:53.080 --> 01:41:54.790
Justin Gary: It did.

01:41:54.790 --> 01:41:57.650
And I would start seeing people play it in
the halls, and then I would see people carrying

01:41:57.650 --> 01:41:58.650
boxes around.

01:41:58.650 --> 01:42:04.260
And so it became this kind of viral, before
this sort of viral sensation, in a way that

01:42:04.260 --> 01:42:05.639
it was very exciting.

01:42:05.639 --> 01:42:08.960
It was a very incredible moment.

01:42:08.960 --> 01:42:11.739
And so we sold out of everything we brought.

01:42:11.739 --> 01:42:16.340
So this is a very rewarding, very cool experience.

01:42:16.340 --> 01:42:20.090
My team, it's not just me now, I've got other
people there working really hard to make this

01:42:20.090 --> 01:42:21.090
come to life.

01:42:21.090 --> 01:42:23.909
Team is like four of us.

01:42:23.909 --> 01:42:29.489
And then I got an offer that was very difficult
offer to refuse.

01:42:29.489 --> 01:42:33.521
So at this point, I had early product that
was shipped for Gen Con, but the rest of the

01:42:33.521 --> 01:42:34.700
stuff was still on the way.

01:42:34.700 --> 01:42:38.310
So I still haven't sold these 10,000 units
with all my life savings tied up into it.

01:42:38.310 --> 01:42:42.970
And I get a call from another very, very big
gaming company, and I don't think I'll say

01:42:42.970 --> 01:42:45.250
the name just because I don't know.

01:42:45.250 --> 01:42:46.250
Anyway.

01:42:46.250 --> 01:42:47.699
And they say, "Hey, we like your game.

01:42:47.699 --> 01:42:49.860
We want to buy it out, all of it.

01:42:49.860 --> 01:42:51.090
We'll take it over.

01:42:51.090 --> 01:42:52.090
It'll be our game.

01:42:52.090 --> 01:42:53.239
We'll pay you a royalty.

01:42:53.239 --> 01:42:54.409
You don't have to worry about anything.

01:42:54.409 --> 01:42:55.960
It'll be all done."

01:42:55.960 --> 01:42:58.030
Now, this is a very tough moment for me.

01:42:58.030 --> 01:42:59.690
This is, in a sense, it's a dream come true.

01:42:59.690 --> 01:43:00.719
They buy all the stocks.

01:43:00.719 --> 01:43:05.130
So my money's off the table, they are a big
company, they can push it, they can sell it,

01:43:05.130 --> 01:43:08.930
and then I can just go back to making games
and I'll have some amount of royalty and that'll

01:43:08.930 --> 01:43:12.130
be good to go.

01:43:12.130 --> 01:43:18.640
And I sat with it, and at the end of the day,
I was like, this is my baby.

01:43:18.640 --> 01:43:22.409
I've worked so hard to get it here, and I
want to see it through.

01:43:22.409 --> 01:43:23.880
I want to see it through.

01:43:23.880 --> 01:43:24.880
I want it to stay mine.

01:43:24.880 --> 01:43:26.920
I didn't want to sell it off.

01:43:26.920 --> 01:43:29.290
And I said no.

01:43:29.290 --> 01:43:32.790
And it was one of those, I might regret this
one later.

01:43:32.790 --> 01:43:37.940
And then 30 days later, product shows up finally
after delays.

01:43:37.940 --> 01:43:43.360
Funnily enough, the product launch delay was
launched such that it actually launched while

01:43:43.360 --> 01:43:50.159
I was at Burning Man, which was not supposed
to be, but there it was.

01:43:50.159 --> 01:43:55.400
And when I come back from Burning Man — so
I have a little release party out in the middle

01:43:55.400 --> 01:44:02.480
of the desert on the fly, and when I come
back, we'd sold out, everything was sold out,

01:44:02.480 --> 01:44:04.880
the entire run, and we had to make a reorder.

01:44:04.880 --> 01:44:08.210
And it was like, the company's working.

01:44:08.210 --> 01:44:10.650
I can reorder, start making expansion.

01:44:10.650 --> 01:44:15.570
And the rest of the company's history kind
of evolved from that in a way that, well,

01:44:15.570 --> 01:44:17.849
I'm glad I made the decision I did.

01:44:17.849 --> 01:44:20.040
Tim Ferriss: All right, many questions.

01:44:20.040 --> 01:44:24.420
So at Gen Con, how many games did you sell,
roughly?

01:44:24.420 --> 01:44:28.730
Justin Gary: Oh, man, I don't remember exactly,
but it's the hundreds.

01:44:28.730 --> 01:44:29.730
Tim Ferriss: Hundreds.

01:44:29.730 --> 01:44:30.730
Justin Gary: Some number of hundreds.

01:44:30.730 --> 01:44:33.731
Tim Ferriss: So I want to bookmark this.

01:44:33.731 --> 01:44:38.040
This is not going to be my first question,
but just how you sell 10,000 after selling

01:44:38.040 --> 01:44:39.190
a few hundred?

01:44:39.190 --> 01:44:41.000
Was it word of mouth online?

01:44:41.000 --> 01:44:42.000
Was it something else?

01:44:42.000 --> 01:44:45.640
Just what factors do you think contributed
to that, because it's not an immediate obvious

01:44:45.640 --> 01:44:46.640
outcome, right?

01:44:46.640 --> 01:44:47.640
Justin Gary: Yes.

01:44:47.640 --> 01:44:50.550
Tim Ferriss: At least not for me listening
to this story.

01:44:50.550 --> 01:44:57.690
But before we get to that, because this all
undergirds a lot of what you're talking about,

01:44:57.690 --> 01:45:03.430
how do the economics of say tabletop games
or card games work?

01:45:03.430 --> 01:45:04.670
What's the kind of traditional model?

01:45:04.670 --> 01:45:05.880
How do people get paid?

01:45:05.880 --> 01:45:06.950
Who's getting paid?

01:45:06.950 --> 01:45:08.040
What are the percentages?

01:45:08.040 --> 01:45:09.180
Is terms of royalties?

01:45:09.180 --> 01:45:11.469
What is that a percentage of?

01:45:11.469 --> 01:45:17.880
Could you just give us an overview of what
the economics of that world look like as a

01:45:17.880 --> 01:45:18.880
template?

01:45:18.880 --> 01:45:19.880
Justin Gary: Sure.

01:45:19.880 --> 01:45:25.980
So when you're producing games, most of the
time you're going to produce them overseas.

01:45:25.980 --> 01:45:29.630
So now with cardboard stuff, it's starting
to become a little bit more economical to

01:45:29.630 --> 01:45:30.630
do it stateside.

01:45:30.630 --> 01:45:33.449
We do some of our games stateside, but mostly
do it overseas.

01:45:33.449 --> 01:45:38.889
And whatever the retail price of the game
is, you typically need — the cost of the

01:45:38.889 --> 01:45:41.390
game has to be about a fifth of that,.

01:45:41.390 --> 01:45:46.210
And the reason is that as a manufacturer,
you are going to sell the game to a distributor

01:45:46.210 --> 01:45:48.560
at about 60 percent off the price of retail.

01:45:48.560 --> 01:45:53.040
They're going to sell it to a retailer at
50 percent off the price of retail.

01:45:53.040 --> 01:45:56.510
So they've got a 10 percent margin for distribution,
and then the retailer has that 50 percent

01:45:56.510 --> 01:45:57.690
margin for the rest of it.

01:45:57.690 --> 01:46:02.530
Tim Ferriss: So if you're manufacturing, if
you were a game designer and manufacturer,

01:46:02.530 --> 01:46:07.710
then it sounds like 20 percent margins generally
on games, something like that?

01:46:07.710 --> 01:46:08.710
Justin Gary: Yeah.

01:46:08.710 --> 01:46:12.510
Now, if you're a game designer, typically
if you have an external game designer and

01:46:12.510 --> 01:46:16.070
you pay a royalty to that designer off whatever
you're selling.

01:46:16.070 --> 01:46:18.900
So if you're selling it to distribution, you
sell a percentage off the distribution price.

01:46:18.900 --> 01:46:21.469
If you sell it direct to consumer, you sell
off to consumer price.

01:46:21.469 --> 01:46:23.630
And that can vary depending upon the designer.

01:46:23.630 --> 01:46:29.420
In tabletop games, typically it's going to
be anywhere between six and 12 percent at

01:46:29.420 --> 01:46:30.540
the high end.

01:46:30.540 --> 01:46:34.960
For more mass market games, big scale games,
it could go as low as two percent or three

01:46:34.960 --> 01:46:36.639
percent, but somewhere in that ballpark.

01:46:36.639 --> 01:46:37.639
Tim Ferriss: Okay, I got it.

01:46:37.639 --> 01:46:46.250
This is important, because it highlights how
challenging your decision to say no was.

01:46:46.250 --> 01:46:51.440
Because we're not talking about, I'm keeping
90 percent of — well, I mean maybe with

01:46:51.440 --> 01:46:56.000
direct-to-consumer you are, but it's not like
90 percent margins versus six.

01:46:56.000 --> 01:47:00.230
It's like there's actually a narrower gap,
which is also true, for instance, in books

01:47:00.230 --> 01:47:04.520
for self-publishing and traditional deals,
people think they're miles and miles apart.

01:47:04.520 --> 01:47:11.920
In some cases maybe they are, but when you
start to factor in all of the costs, the delta

01:47:11.920 --> 01:47:16.360
between the percentage that you take, say
self-publishing versus traditional, it's not

01:47:16.360 --> 01:47:18.670
as wide as people might think.

01:47:18.670 --> 01:47:21.130
So thank you for giving that.

01:47:21.130 --> 01:47:22.890
Is there anything else you'd like to add to
that?

01:47:22.890 --> 01:47:28.230
Justin Gary: Yeah, well, it comes down to
it was a little bit more than just the money

01:47:28.230 --> 01:47:30.000
and the dollars in the sense of it.

01:47:30.000 --> 01:47:34.099
It was a matter of like, this was my baby,
this is my IP.

01:47:34.099 --> 01:47:37.920
You've worked with publishers where they now
take over the rights of stuff and you can't

01:47:37.920 --> 01:47:39.290
just do what you want with the book anymore.

01:47:39.290 --> 01:47:41.290
You can't do things —
Tim Ferriss: I would imagine if you want to

01:47:41.290 --> 01:47:46.810
do Ascension expansion packs or Ascension,
A, B, C, D, and E, you want to do a whole

01:47:46.810 --> 01:47:48.310
derivative line.

01:47:48.310 --> 01:47:54.239
If the publisher owns those rights or effectively
owns those rights, you're in a tight spot.

01:47:54.239 --> 01:47:55.239
Justin Gary: That's right.

01:47:55.239 --> 01:47:58.340
And now think about where we are now, 13 years
later, there's over 16 standalone expansions

01:47:58.340 --> 01:47:59.340
for Ascension.

01:47:59.340 --> 01:48:02.880
We made Ascension Tactics, which is an Ascension
miniatures game, which we did another expansion

01:48:02.880 --> 01:48:03.880
to that.

01:48:03.880 --> 01:48:06.800
Ascension's been featured in a major motion
picture.

01:48:06.800 --> 01:48:11.080
We are working on trying to take the IP and
make cartoons and make other things, comic

01:48:11.080 --> 01:48:12.080
books out of it.

01:48:12.080 --> 01:48:15.400
There's all of these things that are fun.

01:48:15.400 --> 01:48:16.590
We've had conversations about this sort of
stuff.

01:48:16.590 --> 01:48:19.909
It's fun to create and grow and expand your
storylines.

01:48:19.909 --> 01:48:24.500
When I first made Ascension, I had sketched
out a three-year story arc of what the world

01:48:24.500 --> 01:48:27.730
of Ascension would be if I got the whole thing
done.

01:48:27.730 --> 01:48:31.780
And now of course, it's 13 years later, so
I've had to make some more stories since then.

01:48:31.780 --> 01:48:33.010
But that's the part I love.

01:48:33.010 --> 01:48:35.989
Just like when growing up playing Dungeons
&amp; Dragons, the process of coming up with the

01:48:35.989 --> 01:48:39.980
stories and making it and bringing that world
to life is just, I love it.

01:48:39.980 --> 01:48:41.330
And so I didn't want to lose that.

01:48:41.330 --> 01:48:47.040
For people that are listening, that are trying
to make these decisions, it's not obvious.

01:48:47.040 --> 01:48:50.290
Even though I've been fortunately successful
with it, it's not obvious that it's the right

01:48:50.290 --> 01:48:51.570
decision for everybody.

01:48:51.570 --> 01:48:56.929
I spend way less of my time just designing
games because I'm running a company.

01:48:56.929 --> 01:49:01.920
I have to manage logistics, I have to manage
people, I have to plan out the marketing strategies.

01:49:01.920 --> 01:49:04.340
There's all these things that come with running
a company.

01:49:04.340 --> 01:49:08.369
And so I think a lot of people would actually
be better off, if all you want to do is make

01:49:08.369 --> 01:49:12.369
games, then selling to a publisher is a great
strategy.

01:49:12.369 --> 01:49:15.470
I wanted to build worlds and build something
bigger.

01:49:15.470 --> 01:49:17.070
And so here I am.

01:49:17.070 --> 01:49:21.349
Tim Ferriss: And I'll just repeat a name I
mentioned earlier, Stephen Key.

01:49:21.349 --> 01:49:24.989
I don't think he does what some people would
refer to as venturing, which is basically

01:49:24.989 --> 01:49:29.690
building the business to do it mostly yourself
or a lot of it yourself.

01:49:29.690 --> 01:49:35.750
He loves developing new toys and new products,
and he's really good at the licensing game.

01:49:35.750 --> 01:49:40.530
And that, as far as I know, maybe things have
changed, but that's the game game he plays.

01:49:40.530 --> 01:49:44.830
And I would say just on the do something bigger
side, there are different ways of doing something

01:49:44.830 --> 01:49:45.830
bigger.

01:49:45.830 --> 01:49:51.610
So if you want to have the freedom to do the
extensions and so on like you have, on some

01:49:51.610 --> 01:49:54.570
level you almost certainly have to retain
rights.

01:49:54.570 --> 01:49:59.920
But for a product developer who doesn't have
the business knowhow or doesn't want to develop

01:49:59.920 --> 01:50:05.409
it, doesn't want to spend time that way, licensing
to a gigantic company that has a global footprint

01:50:05.409 --> 01:50:10.690
and distribution could be the perfect path
or the best available path for making a large

01:50:10.690 --> 01:50:16.040
impact in terms of distribution and having
some type of — putting a spin on the ball

01:50:16.040 --> 01:50:17.040
of culture.

01:50:17.040 --> 01:50:20.650
Justin Gary: Yeah, speaking of putting a spin
on the ball, the game Bakugan I mentioned

01:50:20.650 --> 01:50:24.530
earlier, is a game that I work on that's Spin
Master, the company that owns it, they have

01:50:24.530 --> 01:50:28.340
toy plastic balls, and there's a game that,
I designed the game, but they own the rights,

01:50:28.340 --> 01:50:30.400
they own the game, they own the distribution,
they do everything.

01:50:30.400 --> 01:50:34.409
And that is by far the biggest game I've ever
worked on.

01:50:34.409 --> 01:50:36.250
I mean, it's all over the world.

01:50:36.250 --> 01:50:37.250
It's in toy stores.

01:50:37.250 --> 01:50:40.700
Anywhere you go, there's a cartoon, multiple
cartoons.

01:50:40.700 --> 01:50:41.700
It's enormous.

01:50:41.700 --> 01:50:44.690
And so that is also fun and also a really
cool, exciting thing.

01:50:44.690 --> 01:50:46.400
And so that path is totally viable.

01:50:46.400 --> 01:50:51.030
So you can do work for hire, you can create
projects and sell them and make a royalty.

01:50:51.030 --> 01:50:53.410
You can launch and publish your own things.

01:50:53.410 --> 01:50:56.500
There's an enormous number of ways to go forward.

01:50:56.500 --> 01:51:00.960
And a lot of it comes down to just getting
clear, defining what it is that you want,

01:51:00.960 --> 01:51:03.460
what's important to you, it's like, "How do
I make a living as a game designer?"

01:51:03.460 --> 01:51:06.440
That's like the surface level question, how
questions are a trap.

01:51:06.440 --> 01:51:07.440
You need to get below that.

01:51:07.440 --> 01:51:09.290
It's like, "Wait, what does it mean for me?

01:51:09.290 --> 01:51:10.520
What is it I actually want?

01:51:10.520 --> 01:51:12.130
What does success look like for me?"

01:51:12.130 --> 01:51:14.520
Then even deeper than that, "Why am I doing
this?"

01:51:14.520 --> 01:51:17.860
Because I want to make super cool worlds,
and I want to grow into a universe, or because

01:51:17.860 --> 01:51:21.719
I want to collaborate with people, or because
I want to reach millions of people, or because

01:51:21.719 --> 01:51:24.550
I just want to have something to play with
my friends and do something on the side.

01:51:24.550 --> 01:51:29.230
Those questions, there's no wrong answer to
any of those questions, but if you don't dive

01:51:29.230 --> 01:51:33.500
deep enough for yourself, you're going to
end up chasing down a path, like I did many

01:51:33.500 --> 01:51:36.449
times, where I'm just going to compete, or
move on a path for the sake of it, because

01:51:36.449 --> 01:51:38.520
I want the most dollars.

01:51:38.520 --> 01:51:42.060
The most dollars is the wrong game to play,
I'll tell you that.

01:51:42.060 --> 01:51:43.739
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, totally.

01:51:43.739 --> 01:51:46.090
It can lead you astray really quickly.

01:51:46.090 --> 01:51:50.360
Important to keep your eyes on the cash flow,
but also —

01:51:50.360 --> 01:51:51.360
Justin Gary: Oh, yes.

01:51:51.360 --> 01:51:52.360
Tim Ferriss: It can be a trap.

01:51:52.360 --> 01:51:53.781
Justin Gary: Don't run out of dollars, but
—

01:51:53.781 --> 01:51:54.781
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, don't run out of dollars.

01:51:54.781 --> 01:51:56.130
Justin Gary: But don't just optimize for dollars.

01:51:56.130 --> 01:51:59.679
Tim Ferriss: All right, so maybe this ties
in.

01:51:59.679 --> 01:52:07.120
You've mentioned a number of successes, do
you have, in your entrepreneurial journey,

01:52:07.120 --> 01:52:13.540
after starting your company, any favorite
failures or near-death experiences, or anything

01:52:13.540 --> 01:52:15.739
like that that you can describe?

01:52:15.739 --> 01:52:17.000
Justin Gary: Oh, yes.

01:52:17.000 --> 01:52:20.119
Oh, yes, I can.

01:52:20.119 --> 01:52:25.119
The life of an entrepreneur is definitely
one that has a lot of ups and downs, as many

01:52:25.119 --> 01:52:26.520
of your guests can attest to it.

01:52:26.520 --> 01:52:28.710
You can attest, I'm sure.

01:52:28.710 --> 01:52:32.790
So what I mentioned earlier, the game SolForge,
we originally — we've since relaunched it,

01:52:32.790 --> 01:52:37.380
but at the time it was a purely digital trading
card game, and I partnered with Richard Garfield,

01:52:37.380 --> 01:52:39.409
this is the guy that created Magic: The Gathering.

01:52:39.409 --> 01:52:42.930
This is a dream come true for me.

01:52:42.930 --> 01:52:44.270
I'll tell you the brief story.

01:52:44.270 --> 01:52:49.070
So we meet, we're at a game conference, I'd
seen him around before, but we hadn't really

01:52:49.070 --> 01:52:50.070
met.

01:52:50.070 --> 01:52:55.530
We're at a game conference called PAX Dev,
it's like a developer conference, and he's

01:52:55.530 --> 01:52:59.590
giving a talk on design, so of course I'm
in the audience listening because I want to

01:52:59.590 --> 01:53:02.489
learn from the master.

01:53:02.489 --> 01:53:05.630
At the end there's a Q and A, and then somebody
asks him, "Hey, what's your favorite game

01:53:05.630 --> 01:53:06.630
right now?"

01:53:06.630 --> 01:53:12.000
He says, "Ascension," and I literally jump
up out of my seat from the back of the audience

01:53:12.000 --> 01:53:14.630
and go, "Woo-hoo," like a stupid kid.

01:53:14.630 --> 01:53:18.040
Everybody laughs, but it gives me the opening,
and so I go and I start a conversation with

01:53:18.040 --> 01:53:20.210
him after the talk.

01:53:20.210 --> 01:53:21.699
We start talking and we get excited.

01:53:21.699 --> 01:53:26.010
It's clear we're both excited about the same
project, and so SolForge is born.

01:53:26.010 --> 01:53:33.030
Now, this was a project that was my first
digital game I'd ever done, and I was a little

01:53:33.030 --> 01:53:34.030
cocky at this point.

01:53:34.030 --> 01:53:35.030
I won't lie.

01:53:35.030 --> 01:53:37.969
I'd succeeded, my first project, Ascension
was a huge success.

01:53:37.969 --> 01:53:40.639
Everything I touch turns to gold, it's all
going to be great, we're doubling in size

01:53:40.639 --> 01:53:42.199
every year.

01:53:42.199 --> 01:53:47.900
We do a crowdfund, and I thought it was going
to cost $250,000 to make that game.

01:53:47.900 --> 01:53:52.170
We crowdfunded with that target, and we ended
up crowdfunding double that goal, almost double

01:53:52.170 --> 01:53:53.170
that goal, like $500,000.

01:53:53.170 --> 01:53:54.300
It's like, "Oh, this is amazing.

01:53:54.300 --> 01:53:55.300
We're going to crush it.

01:53:55.300 --> 01:53:56.310
We're so good."

01:53:56.310 --> 01:54:00.949
As it turns out, making digital games is very
expensive, and it turns out that that cost

01:54:00.949 --> 01:54:03.099
$3 million to make.

01:54:03.099 --> 01:54:04.380
Tim Ferriss: Oh, man.

01:54:04.380 --> 01:54:08.960
Justin Gary: So I had to go and try to raise
funds again, and we have to launch the game

01:54:08.960 --> 01:54:10.320
before we're ready.

01:54:10.320 --> 01:54:15.900
Again, we have followers, we have cash, but
the cash burn is — over time I can see the

01:54:15.900 --> 01:54:20.310
cash burn is higher than the income, and it's
not working, and I overextended.

01:54:20.310 --> 01:54:24.110
I started borrowing more money, I start trying
to like, "I know I could make this work.

01:54:24.110 --> 01:54:25.180
I'm not going to let it go.

01:54:25.180 --> 01:54:26.180
This is my baby.

01:54:26.180 --> 01:54:27.180
I'm going to keep it going."

01:54:27.180 --> 01:54:28.829
Tim Ferriss: How are you raising and borrowing
money?

01:54:28.829 --> 01:54:30.670
If you don't mind adding a little more detail.

01:54:30.670 --> 01:54:31.670
Justin Gary: Yeah.

01:54:31.670 --> 01:54:36.389
So it starts off with we just do a kind of
friends and family convertible notes kind

01:54:36.389 --> 01:54:42.220
of round, so that means they give me a loan,
and then in theory it turns into equity over

01:54:42.220 --> 01:54:43.220
time.

01:54:43.220 --> 01:54:44.220
Tim Ferriss: Into equity.

01:54:44.220 --> 01:54:45.220
Justin Gary: Yeah.

01:54:45.220 --> 01:54:47.449
Then after that, then I start taking some
of those refactoring loans like you talked

01:54:47.449 --> 01:54:52.599
about, so I'm getting loans against my future
income on Ascension to pay for SolForge, and

01:54:52.599 --> 01:54:57.199
then starting to run up credit cards, and
all the things you really are not supposed

01:54:57.199 --> 01:54:58.920
to do.

01:54:58.920 --> 01:55:03.380
I get to a point where I have to finally realize,
"Wow, I'm going to go bankrupt.

01:55:03.380 --> 01:55:04.500
I can't do this anymore.

01:55:04.500 --> 01:55:06.320
I'm going to lose everything."

01:55:06.320 --> 01:55:11.239
This was just one of the hardest things that
I have ever faced, because not only is it

01:55:11.239 --> 01:55:15.280
like I feel like I owe it to my team, who
I'm going to have to lay off these people

01:55:15.280 --> 01:55:18.980
whose livelihoods depend on it, we have fans
and people who love the game, and not only

01:55:18.980 --> 01:55:23.090
is this — in a traditional tabletop game,
if I stop making Ascension, let's say, you

01:55:23.090 --> 01:55:26.520
still have your copy of Ascension at home
and you could play it for as long as you want,

01:55:26.520 --> 01:55:27.550
it doesn't matter if I don't make anymore.

01:55:27.550 --> 01:55:30.770
But when I stop making SolForge, it's a digital
game, that means the server is turned off,

01:55:30.770 --> 01:55:33.450
that means your entire collection is gone,
that means you can't play it anymore, everything

01:55:33.450 --> 01:55:34.450
you spent, it's all gone.

01:55:34.450 --> 01:55:40.050
I'm like failing myself, I'm failing my team,
I'm failing the fans, everything, and I have

01:55:40.050 --> 01:55:42.340
to make some very, very hard decisions.

01:55:42.340 --> 01:55:43.929
Tim Ferriss: Not to mention the friends and
family.

01:55:43.929 --> 01:55:47.070
Justin Gary: Of course, yeah, people — I
literally have — my parents had taken a

01:55:47.070 --> 01:55:49.820
loan against their house to help me at one
point.

01:55:49.820 --> 01:55:51.420
I am so overextended.

01:55:51.420 --> 01:55:56.460
I am so in the thick of it, shame and — it
is tough.

01:55:56.460 --> 01:56:02.340
I have to come to a realization, and come
to the other side of it where I had to go

01:56:02.340 --> 01:56:04.790
through this process of accepting.

01:56:04.790 --> 01:56:08.710
I had to get to the other place like, "Okay,
it happened.

01:56:08.710 --> 01:56:09.710
I've lost everything.

01:56:09.710 --> 01:56:10.710
I failed, now what?"

01:56:10.710 --> 01:56:14.290
Right now, this is a different kind of fear-setting
exercise because now I'm on the other side

01:56:14.290 --> 01:56:15.890
of my worst nightmare.

01:56:15.890 --> 01:56:21.770
It took me a little while, but it's like,
"Okay, you know what?

01:56:21.770 --> 01:56:23.639
I will still be alive.

01:56:23.639 --> 01:56:26.940
I will be able to pay off some of these debts
over time with some other things.

01:56:26.940 --> 01:56:27.940
I can recover from this.

01:56:27.940 --> 01:56:28.940
It's going to be okay.

01:56:28.940 --> 01:56:30.930
The people who got laid off, they're going
to get another job."

01:56:30.930 --> 01:56:36.719
It was not easy, but then once I accepted
that worst case outcome, and then I was able

01:56:36.719 --> 01:56:39.890
to say, "Okay, now how do I make this less
likely?"

01:56:39.890 --> 01:56:47.040
So I had to lay people off, but I also renegotiated
debts, and negotiated payment plans, and reduced

01:56:47.040 --> 01:56:53.340
our overhead, and took on more contract work,
and slowly but surely I was able to dig myself

01:56:53.340 --> 01:56:54.579
out of the hole.

01:56:54.579 --> 01:56:59.040
I don't think there's any chance I would've
been able to do that if not for being mentally

01:56:59.040 --> 01:57:03.679
ready with, "Okay, I've lost it all, I'm talking
to bankruptcy attorneys," and that gives you

01:57:03.679 --> 01:57:07.200
a different kind of attitude and approach
when you're trying to — you're negotiating

01:57:07.200 --> 01:57:10.179
with a lender that you owe money to, and they're
like, "You owe me money, you should collect,"

01:57:10.179 --> 01:57:13.270
I'm like, "I know, but either I'm going to
go bankrupt and you're not going to get anything,

01:57:13.270 --> 01:57:17.880
or we're going to work something out, and
I'm going to do my best to pay you off."

01:57:17.880 --> 01:57:24.050
Fortunately I was able to turn that around,
and it was not only did I learn — the important

01:57:24.050 --> 01:57:29.730
lesson is you can go through a lot, and you
can recover from it, but also to not put myself

01:57:29.730 --> 01:57:35.030
in that position again, not to overextend
to that degree, and learn to make smaller

01:57:35.030 --> 01:57:36.770
bets, and grow as you can.

01:57:36.770 --> 01:57:41.440
It was a very important and powerful thing,
which then of course, I'm now been able to

01:57:41.440 --> 01:57:43.469
come back to with reinvigorating that very
same game.

01:57:43.469 --> 01:57:51.310
Tim Ferriss: So to ask a question related
to that, you have this dream come true partnering

01:57:51.310 --> 01:57:59.810
with Richard to create this game, how did
you two communicate, or what led to the ability

01:57:59.810 --> 01:58:05.360
to maintain that relationship and now work
together again?

01:58:05.360 --> 01:58:09.920
Because there are, I suppose, as I'm imagining
different ways this could have played out,

01:58:09.920 --> 01:58:15.170
I'm sure there are ways with many different
collaborators, where things could have exploded,

01:58:15.170 --> 01:58:17.920
and it would've damaged the relationship,
and that would've been the end of that.

01:58:17.920 --> 01:58:19.310
So why didn't that happen?

01:58:19.310 --> 01:58:24.480
Justin Gary: You've got to be honest with
people, that's really what it comes down to.

01:58:24.480 --> 01:58:28.620
Act in integrity when you work with partners,
sometimes you have bad news, you tell them

01:58:28.620 --> 01:58:30.340
bad news.

01:58:30.340 --> 01:58:35.290
Have some humility around when your ideas
are not working, or when you're trying things

01:58:35.290 --> 01:58:37.350
and say, "Here's what we're going to do, and
here's what…"

01:58:37.350 --> 01:58:40.860
When I talk about nobody knows what they're
doing, when I realize the other executives

01:58:40.860 --> 01:58:44.199
do it, I don't hide the fact that I don't
know what I'm doing.

01:58:44.199 --> 01:58:47.420
I've learned a lot, I've got good ideas, I've
tried things that work, but whenever you're

01:58:47.420 --> 01:58:51.190
pushing the boundary and trying something
new, sometimes it's not going to work.

01:58:51.190 --> 01:58:55.190
So when you're working with somebody, and
again, fortunately Richard has been — he's

01:58:55.190 --> 01:58:57.409
a professional, he's not only has he made
games that were uber successful, but he's

01:58:57.409 --> 01:59:01.880
also made plenty of games that weren't uber
successful too, so he knows this.

01:59:01.880 --> 01:59:06.270
Anybody that's done this for long enough,
any creative field you cannot predict.

01:59:06.270 --> 01:59:07.930
Everything is not going to be a hit.

01:59:07.930 --> 01:59:12.480
So when it's my first major failure, for me,
it felt like the end of the world, but the

01:59:12.480 --> 01:59:16.310
more you work with professionals, the more
you realize that that's just part of the process.

01:59:16.310 --> 01:59:17.590
Tim Ferriss: Amazing.

01:59:17.590 --> 01:59:24.820
What have you learned or what decisions have
you made related to your company or entrepreneurship,

01:59:24.820 --> 01:59:31.480
how you run things, that you think are worth
mentioning?

01:59:31.480 --> 01:59:35.560
It could be after that experience, it could
just be in general, maybe ways that you have

01:59:35.560 --> 01:59:41.270
simplified things or challenged assumptions,
turned things upside down, anything at all

01:59:41.270 --> 01:59:42.350
that comes to mind.

01:59:42.350 --> 01:59:48.860
Justin Gary: One of the things that I've realized
is that the same way you design games, designing

01:59:48.860 --> 01:59:51.160
a great game and designing a great company
are not all that different.

01:59:51.160 --> 01:59:56.440
In a great game, you have clear goals, you
know what you're trying to do, it's very clearly

01:59:56.440 --> 01:59:57.600
defined what you're trying to do.

01:59:57.600 --> 02:00:03.230
You are getting a lot of feedback, and rewards,
either that be from points for achievements

02:00:03.230 --> 02:00:04.230
or [inaudible].

02:00:04.230 --> 02:00:05.790
You're getting constant feedback loops.

02:00:05.790 --> 02:00:07.930
The challenge level is appropriate.

02:00:07.930 --> 02:00:09.840
You're focused on learning one thing at a
time.

02:00:09.840 --> 02:00:12.329
The skill level is appropriate where you want
to be.

02:00:12.329 --> 02:00:15.540
Probably the most important thing about games,
when we play games, we take a certain kind

02:00:15.540 --> 02:00:21.159
of mindset, if I'm playing a game, I expect
to lose, I expect to have challenges.

02:00:21.159 --> 02:00:25.110
In fact, that's the whole point, if you play
a game and there's never a challenge, why

02:00:25.110 --> 02:00:26.280
are we even doing it, right?

02:00:26.280 --> 02:00:27.280
Tim Ferriss: Right.

02:00:27.280 --> 02:00:31.409
Justin Gary: So I have tried to cultivate
all four of those things in my company.

02:00:31.409 --> 02:00:37.480
We set clear goals where it's like, "Okay,
we're going to try to have this metric, this

02:00:37.480 --> 02:00:40.150
specific metric, like our email subscriber
list, we're going to try to increase it by

02:00:40.150 --> 02:00:43.332
this amount, and here's how we're going to
do it over this quarter," or we're going to

02:00:43.332 --> 02:00:46.440
try to get a new game in this category, that
we're going to have a prototype ready to go

02:00:46.440 --> 02:00:47.460
in two weeks.

02:00:47.460 --> 02:00:48.460
Clear goals.

02:00:48.460 --> 02:00:52.150
We have something that allows us to have daily
focus and feedback.

02:00:52.150 --> 02:00:57.300
What I love is the rule of three, I think
I first learned this from Chris Bailey, his

02:00:57.300 --> 02:01:01.230
book The Productivity Project, but I've applied
it at every level of my company.

02:01:01.230 --> 02:01:06.501
So what it is, is you have everybody posts
their three daily goals, what are the top

02:01:06.501 --> 02:01:08.730
three things that I want to focus on?

02:01:08.730 --> 02:01:12.610
Three is really important because we all have
an infinite laundry list of stuff to do, we're

02:01:12.610 --> 02:01:15.710
never going to finish our to-do list, but
if you can finish your most important three

02:01:15.710 --> 02:01:19.710
things, you can make an enormous amount of
progress, and we scale that to every level

02:01:19.710 --> 02:01:20.710
of the company.

02:01:20.710 --> 02:01:25.310
So every team we have have three weekly goals,
we have monthly goals, we have quarterly goals,

02:01:25.310 --> 02:01:27.270
and so everything kind of scales down.

02:01:27.270 --> 02:01:29.650
I even built this into my own personal life.

02:01:29.650 --> 02:01:34.270
I have a thing, The Level Up Journal, which
I've made, which I've got, it just has a journal

02:01:34.270 --> 02:01:38.660
that just — it fits in your pocket, it has
three goals, three habits, and then a little

02:01:38.660 --> 02:01:40.000
gratitude practice.

02:01:40.000 --> 02:01:45.300
So every piece of it, by focusing on very
few things and making those the priority,

02:01:45.300 --> 02:01:46.940
it makes a big difference.

02:01:46.940 --> 02:01:52.920
Then lastly, with the mindset, we set very
aggressive goals, and it's okay not to hit

02:01:52.920 --> 02:01:58.290
them, like a game you want to win, you take
winning seriously, but you don't cry when

02:01:58.290 --> 02:01:59.290
you lose a game.

02:01:59.290 --> 02:02:01.370
You say, "Okay, what can we learn from this?

02:02:01.370 --> 02:02:02.370
How do we get better?"

02:02:02.370 --> 02:02:07.670
Start to take that attitude and approach it,
sometimes easier than others, for sure, but

02:02:07.670 --> 02:02:11.540
cultivating that mindset and focusing on what's
important, and making sure that the goals

02:02:11.540 --> 02:02:14.940
are clear, and everybody knows what they are,
and you're moving day to day, that's how I

02:02:14.940 --> 02:02:16.480
structure the company.

02:02:16.480 --> 02:02:20.660
Again, it came from just the principles of
design, applying them to what we do, and I

02:02:20.660 --> 02:02:21.750
found it to be really powerful.

02:02:21.750 --> 02:02:26.389
Tim Ferriss: Where do people, if people in
the company are posting, for instance, the

02:02:26.389 --> 02:02:29.949
rule of three, their three priorities for
the day, where are they posting these?

02:02:29.949 --> 02:02:36.139
Justin Gary: So we have a company Discord,
we have a channel in our company Discord that

02:02:36.139 --> 02:02:37.530
everybody posts their three goals for the
day.

02:02:37.530 --> 02:02:40.880
There's a separate one for weekly goals, separate
one for monthly goals, and a separate one

02:02:40.880 --> 02:02:43.989
for quarterly goals, and so you can always
look back and see what everybody's doing at

02:02:43.989 --> 02:02:46.550
any given point, if you care to, at their
main focus.

02:02:46.550 --> 02:02:50.949
It also helps us to stay connected because
we're a 100 percent remote team now, so even

02:02:50.949 --> 02:02:54.409
though I may not talk to a given person or
see a given person, I can quickly glance and

02:02:54.409 --> 02:02:57.170
see what's most important to them right now.

02:02:57.170 --> 02:02:58.860
So it keeps us all connected and accountable.

02:02:58.860 --> 02:03:00.530
Tim Ferriss: Two questions.

02:03:00.530 --> 02:03:06.450
The first is, is there any follow-up on those
three or is it just the act of someone publicly

02:03:06.450 --> 02:03:13.080
stating their priorities that makes everyone
feel connected, and hopefully gives them some

02:03:13.080 --> 02:03:17.870
felt sense of accountability that you think
leads to the better output?

02:03:17.870 --> 02:03:21.150
Justin Gary: Yeah, let's start there.

02:03:21.150 --> 02:03:24.480
So yeah, the daily is — there's not much
follow-up on the daily, it's about staying

02:03:24.480 --> 02:03:27.480
connected, and I think it just forces you
to plan.

02:03:27.480 --> 02:03:30.520
It forces you to assess for yourself, "Okay,
what actually is important to me?"

02:03:30.520 --> 02:03:34.829
A lot of people will just start checking emails
and going down rabbit holes, and they're working,

02:03:34.829 --> 02:03:38.940
they are working, but they're not doing what's
important, they're just doing whatever's in

02:03:38.940 --> 02:03:42.710
front of them, and so this forces, just as
a habit it forces you to think things through.

02:03:42.710 --> 02:03:43.710
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, great point.

02:03:43.710 --> 02:03:44.710
Then?

02:03:44.710 --> 02:03:46.650
Justin Gary: Then for the weeklies, we do
have that, so we have our weekly goals.

02:03:46.650 --> 02:03:51.110
We have a check in at the end of the week,
where people will post, "Hey, here were my

02:03:51.110 --> 02:03:52.440
goals for last week.

02:03:52.440 --> 02:03:53.440
Here's how they went.

02:03:53.440 --> 02:03:56.980
Here are now my goals for the next week,"
or, "What can I do?

02:03:56.980 --> 02:03:59.170
If I didn't meet a goal, what am I doing to
fix it?"

02:03:59.170 --> 02:04:02.989
So there is some accountability, and I read
through those every week for the team.

02:04:02.989 --> 02:04:04.790
Tim Ferriss: That's on Friday?

02:04:04.790 --> 02:04:08.340
So people are setting their priorities for
the following week on Friday.

02:04:08.340 --> 02:04:09.579
Justin Gary: Correct.

02:04:09.579 --> 02:04:12.550
Tim Ferriss: Why Discord versus other tools?

02:04:12.550 --> 02:04:13.550
How did you choose that?

02:04:13.550 --> 02:04:18.790
Justin Gary: We tried a lot of them, and I'm
obsessed with productivity tools as you are,

02:04:18.790 --> 02:04:21.520
I think, with a lot of these things, sometimes
to a fault.

02:04:21.520 --> 02:04:22.900
We tried Slack.

02:04:22.900 --> 02:04:25.660
We tried a couple of other custom ones.

02:04:25.660 --> 02:04:26.850
We tried Asana.

02:04:26.850 --> 02:04:28.040
We tried Monday.

02:04:28.040 --> 02:04:29.091
We tried a bunch of them.

02:04:29.091 --> 02:04:35.360
But the nice thing about Discord is, one,
it's very easy to use, you can just start

02:04:35.360 --> 02:04:40.520
up a channel for free, you can have voice
chat and text chat.

02:04:40.520 --> 02:04:44.550
You can also upload bigger files, and you
can with — Slack restricts that unless you

02:04:44.550 --> 02:04:49.599
have a paid program, it's much easier to load
files and share things.

02:04:49.599 --> 02:04:55.690
Honestly, Discord started as a gamer-focused
thing, and so we actually built our fan community

02:04:55.690 --> 02:04:56.690
in Discord.

02:04:56.690 --> 02:04:59.670
So you can go to the Stone Blade Discord,
and it's kind of funny because it's sort of

02:04:59.670 --> 02:05:01.890
a front-of-the-shop, back-of-the-shop kind
of vibe.

02:05:01.890 --> 02:05:04.840
It's like the front of the shop is where all
the fans are there, they're all chatting about

02:05:04.840 --> 02:05:06.793
the games, we can jump in and chat with them.

02:05:06.793 --> 02:05:11.150
Then we are in this private channels that
you can't see, but we're talking about making

02:05:11.150 --> 02:05:15.060
the next things that you're going to be talking
about six months from now.

02:05:15.060 --> 02:05:19.710
So it automatically gives us a sense of connection
to our fans, because the same place where

02:05:19.710 --> 02:05:23.630
we're doing our work, one click away you can
see and interact with fans.

02:05:23.630 --> 02:05:28.870
It has a nice bonus effect of making us feel
a little bit more connected to the community.

02:05:28.870 --> 02:05:32.800
Tim Ferriss: How much of your work do you
do on laptop versus phone?

02:05:32.800 --> 02:05:34.900
What percent would you say?

02:05:34.900 --> 02:05:38.570
Justin Gary: Oh, 90 plus percent laptop, for
sure.

02:05:38.570 --> 02:05:41.460
My phone is, I'll occasionally check emails
to make sure I'm not missing anything, but

02:05:41.460 --> 02:05:42.460
I hate working on my phone.

02:05:42.460 --> 02:05:43.460
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

02:05:43.460 --> 02:05:46.850
I figured it was probably something like that,
I'm the same way.

02:05:46.850 --> 02:05:49.739
What is the mobile experience with Discord?

02:05:49.739 --> 02:05:52.110
I've never used it on mobile.

02:05:52.110 --> 02:05:53.110
Justin Gary: It's fine.

02:05:53.110 --> 02:05:55.760
Again, mostly when I'm there, I can check
messages.

02:05:55.760 --> 02:06:00.099
I turn off all notifications for everything,
I don't want my phone bothering me, or pinging

02:06:00.099 --> 02:06:01.099
me, or distracting me.

02:06:01.099 --> 02:06:05.520
I'm using my phone consciously because I want
to look at something specific.

02:06:05.520 --> 02:06:09.400
It means I have a meeting and I'm not at my
desk, I'll go to Discord, go to the meeting

02:06:09.400 --> 02:06:11.970
thing, check it, or I have something I want
to post at a public channel.

02:06:11.970 --> 02:06:15.020
This is another key thing about it that's
great, because you have so many different

02:06:15.020 --> 02:06:16.920
channels and you can make threaded discussions.

02:06:16.920 --> 02:06:21.300
One of the things I try to encourage is that
we have, as much as possible make communications

02:06:21.300 --> 02:06:22.300
public.

02:06:22.300 --> 02:06:24.650
A lot of times people will have side chats
and side conversations, and come up with some

02:06:24.650 --> 02:06:28.840
great idea, and then it will get lost because
people are just in a private channel somewhere,

02:06:28.840 --> 02:06:32.130
and so we'll make sure that people have public
discussions as much as possible so people

02:06:32.130 --> 02:06:36.090
can add comments, and can get involved and
stay connected if they want to.

02:06:36.090 --> 02:06:40.889
But to continue with your question, the mobile
experience, it's mostly just for some — I

02:06:40.889 --> 02:06:44.900
will only use it if it's like there's some
specific communication, or meeting, or connection

02:06:44.900 --> 02:06:46.739
I need to do, that's when I'll use my phone.

02:06:46.739 --> 02:06:51.570
If I'm going to work, it's because I'm set
up at a laptop, designating time to work and

02:06:51.570 --> 02:06:52.570
connect.

02:06:52.570 --> 02:06:54.500
Tim Ferriss: All right.

02:06:54.500 --> 02:07:00.300
We could keep going for hours, and maybe we
will do more rounds, but there's a prompt

02:07:00.300 --> 02:07:09.940
here that I would love to explore, which is
how a trip to Thailand helped you to escape

02:07:09.940 --> 02:07:12.199
your need for control.

02:07:12.199 --> 02:07:18.690
As someone who focuses on systems, predictability,
controllability, et cetera, this might be

02:07:18.690 --> 02:07:20.300
therapeutic for me to hear.

02:07:20.300 --> 02:07:23.550
So could you unpack this for us?

02:07:23.550 --> 02:07:24.920
Justin Gary: Yes.

02:07:24.920 --> 02:07:25.920
Yes.

02:07:25.920 --> 02:07:29.639
I think we're very similar in the sense, Tim,
it's not — I wouldn't call myself a control

02:07:29.639 --> 02:07:32.510
freak just because I don't want to say mean
things about myself, but I very much want

02:07:32.510 --> 02:07:36.520
things to be good, and I believe I know how
to make them good, and I want to make sure

02:07:36.520 --> 02:07:37.770
that they are good.

02:07:37.770 --> 02:07:39.679
So that's what control freaks say.

02:07:39.679 --> 02:07:44.210
But the point being like, "Look, I know I'm
good at my job, and for the most part, with

02:07:44.210 --> 02:07:48.030
most of the jobs that people are doing, I
know how to do them as well as they do, that's

02:07:48.030 --> 02:07:49.869
how I know if they're doing a good job or
not."

02:07:49.869 --> 02:07:52.400
This is the story I'm telling myself.

02:07:52.400 --> 02:07:58.869
I hired a lot of very smart, very capable
people, and I ended up, every extra person

02:07:58.869 --> 02:08:02.040
I hired suddenly became more work for me,
because I had to keep checking to make sure

02:08:02.040 --> 02:08:06.969
that they were on track, or setting up more
systems to have this, if they weren't doing

02:08:06.969 --> 02:08:12.090
something the way I thought they should be
coming in, or they would come to me with questions.

02:08:12.090 --> 02:08:13.640
At some point I'm like, "Listen, this is too
much.

02:08:13.640 --> 02:08:14.730
This has gone kind of crazy.

02:08:14.730 --> 02:08:17.290
I need to step away."

02:08:17.290 --> 02:08:20.920
So I said, "Look, I'm going to take a three-week
trip to Thailand.

02:08:20.920 --> 02:08:21.960
I'm not bringing my laptop.

02:08:21.960 --> 02:08:22.960
I'm not reachable.

02:08:22.960 --> 02:08:25.420
I'm going to do that in six months."

02:08:25.420 --> 02:08:28.000
I gave the team plenty of time, "What do you
need from me?

02:08:28.000 --> 02:08:30.840
How do we set this up so that you don't need
to talk to me?"

02:08:30.840 --> 02:08:33.610
They, "Okay, we need access to these things
that only you have access to.

02:08:33.610 --> 02:08:34.780
We need to know this, whatever."

02:08:34.780 --> 02:08:38.760
It was very little as it turned out, and then
I go to Thailand.

02:08:38.760 --> 02:08:43.040
Now, I am still too much of a control freak,
so I really did bring at least my iPad with

02:08:43.040 --> 02:08:47.639
me so I could check in, but I didn't respond,
I didn't do anything.

02:08:47.639 --> 02:08:54.340
What magically happened is everybody just
stepped up, and they answered the questions

02:08:54.340 --> 02:08:58.310
that they would've asked me themselves because
they were perfectly capable of doing that.

02:08:58.310 --> 02:09:02.290
They started taking initiative, and building
new things, and doing stuff that I didn't

02:09:02.290 --> 02:09:03.710
even think to do.

02:09:03.710 --> 02:09:10.090
I realized, with some embarrassment, that
I had been holding them back, that I had,

02:09:10.090 --> 02:09:15.020
by not allowing them to fail, or do things
that would be different than I would, I had

02:09:15.020 --> 02:09:19.590
been constraining them from becoming leaders,
and doing the things that they should be doing.

02:09:19.590 --> 02:09:24.099
I had built work for myself, I had made them
less effective at their job, and I had made

02:09:24.099 --> 02:09:26.940
my life less good.

02:09:26.940 --> 02:09:30.840
So that was one of the best vacations I ever
took, because it actually really changed the

02:09:30.840 --> 02:09:37.380
entire way that I managed, changed the entire
way I viewed leadership, and I love Thailand.

02:09:37.380 --> 02:09:39.239
Tim Ferriss: Amazing.

02:09:39.239 --> 02:09:45.670
Just building the systems that then persist
after you get back, what a liberating experience

02:09:45.670 --> 02:09:48.909
that must have been on so many levels, the
gifts that keeps on giving.

02:09:48.909 --> 02:09:49.909
Justin Gary: Yup.

02:09:49.909 --> 02:09:51.980
It's something you talk about a lot, and it
cannot be underestimated.

02:09:51.980 --> 02:09:52.980
What is it?

02:09:52.980 --> 02:09:57.550
So you asked the question, what would it take
for me to let go?

02:09:57.550 --> 02:10:01.380
What would have to be true for you to be willing
to not get into this?

02:10:01.380 --> 02:10:04.230
It could be some key metric that you care
about that they have to report.

02:10:04.230 --> 02:10:06.230
It could be that they have to stay within
a certain budget.

02:10:06.230 --> 02:10:10.559
It could be that they're — in reality, you're
just making up stories of, "Oh, well, I just

02:10:10.559 --> 02:10:12.630
feel like I need it."

02:10:12.630 --> 02:10:16.460
Let those things go, let people grow.

02:10:16.460 --> 02:10:22.550
In my experience, it's just been incredible,
I now have a team that can literally run itself.

02:10:22.550 --> 02:10:24.710
I get in, and I focus on the key new projects.

02:10:24.710 --> 02:10:29.210
I focus on the most important things I think
are wrong, but for the most part, I have stepped

02:10:29.210 --> 02:10:33.380
away from a lot of the day-to-day, and I have
watched my team get better and better, and

02:10:33.380 --> 02:10:34.380
better.

02:10:34.380 --> 02:10:37.610
Again, it's part of that mindset, they make
mistakes just like I make mistakes, and sometimes

02:10:37.610 --> 02:10:39.090
the question is, "What do we learn from it?"

02:10:39.090 --> 02:10:41.050
And don't make the same mistake twice.

02:10:41.050 --> 02:10:43.869
We've learned a lot along the way, and it's
been pretty amazing.

02:10:43.869 --> 02:10:48.869
Tim Ferriss: So Justin, we're coming up on
almost two and a half hours, two hours and

02:10:48.869 --> 02:10:54.590
17 minutes or 18 minutes, I'll ask just a
few more questions, and then we can slowly

02:10:54.590 --> 02:10:56.670
land this plane.

